window.SUBJECTS.push({"id":"geo","n":"Geography","tab":"Geography","w":"Earth, physical geography, India, resources, population and the world.","topics":[{"id":"earth","n":"Earth and the Solar System","qs":[{"id":"q0061","q":"Which of the following statements best describes the position of the North Temperate Zone?","o":["Between the Arctic Circle and North Pole","Between the Tropic of Cancer and Arctic Circle","Between the Equator and Tropic of Cancer","Between the Equator and Arctic Circle"],"a":1,"x":"The North Temperate Zone lies between the Tropic of Cancer (23½°N) and the Arctic Circle (66½°N).","e":"Torrid zone: between the two Tropics. Frigid zones: beyond the Arctic and Antarctic Circles.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0108","q":"If the Earth's axis were not tilted, what change would most likely occur regarding the temperature pattern?","o":["The temperature at the poles would increase","Equatorial regions would receive slanting rays","Tropical regions would freeze","Seasonal variation would be less pronounced"],"a":3,"x":"Seasons exist because Earth's axis is tilted 23½°. With no tilt, each place would get the same sun angle all year, so seasons would almost disappear.","e":"That is why the Tropics of Cancer and Capricorn sit at 23½° north and south.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0119","q":"Why does a lunar eclipse NOT occur every full moon?","o":["The Earth rotates too quickly","The Earth does not always cast a shadow","The Moon becomes invisible during full moons","The Moon’s orbit is tilted relative to the Earth’s orbit"],"a":3,"x":"The Moon's orbit is tilted about 5° to Earth's orbit, so at most full moons the Moon passes above or below Earth's shadow. An eclipse happens only near the nodes.","e":"A lunar eclipse can happen only at full moon; a solar eclipse only at new moon.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0141","q":"Which of the following best explains why the equatorial regions are warmer?","o":["Equator has lower atmospheric pressure.","Equatorial regions have more deserts.","Equator is closer to the sun.","Equatorial regions receive vertical rays of the sun."],"a":3,"x":"Near the equator the Sun's rays fall almost vertically, so the same energy is concentrated on a smaller area. That makes equatorial regions warmer.","e":"Near the poles the rays are slanting and spread over a larger area.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0185","q":"The difference between sidereal and solar day on Earth is primarily due to the _______.","o":["Earth’s orbital motion around the Sun","Earth’s elliptical orbit","moon's gravitational pull","seasonal changes in the atmosphere"],"a":0,"x":"A sidereal day (about 23 h 56 min) is one rotation against the stars. A solar day is about 4 minutes longer because Earth also moves along its orbit and must turn a bit more to face the Sun again.","e":"Over a year this adds up to one extra rotation: about 366 sidereal days in 365 solar days.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0280","q":"What is the shape of the Earth?","o":["Geoid","Oval","Round","Circular"],"a":0,"x":"Earth is a geoid, 'earth-shaped': roughly a sphere, flattened at the poles and bulging at the equator.","e":"The equatorial diameter is about 43 km more than the polar diameter.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0427","q":"Between the orbits of which planets are asteroids found?","o":["Mars and Jupiter","Earth and Mars","Saturn and Jupiter","Mercury and Venus"],"a":0,"x":"The asteroid belt lies between the orbits of Mars and Jupiter; Ceres, a dwarf planet, is its largest body.","e":"Order: Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, [asteroid belt], Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune.","src":["ASO 22 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0467","q":"Which of the following is the thinnest of all the layers on the Earth's surface?","o":["Crust","Core","Soil","Mantle"],"a":0,"x":"The crust is the thinnest layer: about 5 km under oceans and 30–70 km under continents. The mantle is about 2,900 km thick.","e":"Crust → mantle → outer core → inner core.","src":["ASO 27 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q1611","q":"About ______ percent of the Earth's surface is water-covered.","o":["71","70","73","75"],"a":0,"x":"About 71% of Earth's surface is covered by water; land is about 29%.","e":"Oceans hold about 97% of Earth's water; freshwater is about 3%.","src":["Patwari 20 Aug 2022 S2"]},{"id":"q1615","q":"Which of the following are the small pieces of rocks that move around the sun?","o":["Planets","Galaxy","Meteoroids","Constellations"],"a":2,"x":"Meteoroids are small rocky pieces moving around the Sun; one burning in Earth's atmosphere is a meteor (shooting star), and one reaching the ground is a meteorite.","e":"Asteroids are larger rocky bodies, mostly between Mars and Jupiter.","src":["Patwari 18 Sep 2022 S1"]},{"id":"q1795","q":"Which direction is indicated by the Pole star?","o":["East","West","North","South"],"a":2,"x":"The Pole Star (Polaris) sits almost directly above the North Pole, so it always shows the north direction.","e":"It is found using the pointer stars of the Great Bear (Saptarishi).","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1835","q":"The tropic of cancer lies at ______.","o":["66(1/2)°N","23(1/2)°N","66(1/2)°S","23(1/2)°S"],"a":1,"x":"The Tropic of Cancer lies at about 23½°N; the Tropic of Capricorn at 23½°S; the Arctic Circle at 66½°N.","e":"The Tropic of Cancer passes through eight Indian states.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2076","q":"Which of the following are the components of maps? I. Distance II. Direction III. Symbol","o":["I and II","II and III","I and III","I, II and III"],"a":3,"x":"The three components of a map are distance (scale), direction and symbols.","e":"Maps show north with an arrow.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2113","q":"The tropic of Capricon lies at ________________.","o":["23(1/2)°S","23(1/2)°N","66(1/2)°S","66(1/2)°N"],"a":0,"x":"The Tropic of Capricorn lies at about 23½°S; the Tropic of Cancer at 23½°N.","e":"The Sun is overhead at Capricorn on 22 December.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2915","q":"Which of the following statement regarding interior of the earth is correct? I. The crust form only 1 percent of the volume of the earth. II. The mantle forms 84 percent of the volume of the earth.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"The crust makes up only about 1% of the earth's volume and the mantle about 84%; the rest is the core.","e":"","src":["Junior Clerk 17 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5016","q":"Which of the following statement is NOT correct about planet Venus?","o":["It is the second planet from the Sun.","It is Earth’s second closest planetary neighbour.","It’s one of the four inner, terrestrial (or rocky) planets.","It has a thick, toxic atmosphere filled with carbon dioxide."],"a":1,"x":"Venus is the second planet from the Sun and Earth's closest planetary neighbour, not its second closest.","e":"","src":["Stenographer 26 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6954","q":"According to Kepler's _________ law, the square of the time period of the planet's orbit is directly proportional to the cube of the semimajor axis of its elliptical path.","o":["first","second","third","fourth"],"a":2,"x":"Kepler's third law: the square of a planet's orbital period is proportional to the cube of the semi-major axis of its orbit (T² ∝ a³).","e":"","src":["Stenographer 8 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q5402","q":"Our solar system is a small part of which of the following Galaxies?","o":["Andromeda Galaxy","Sombrero Galaxy","Triangulum Galaxy","Milky Way"],"a":3,"x":"Our solar system is part of the Milky Way galaxy.","e":"","src":["Personal Assistant 29 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q5443","q":"Earth is the _____ largest planet in the solar system;","o":["5th","3rd","4th","6th"],"a":0,"x":"Earth is the fifth largest planet, after Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune.","e":"","src":["Personal Assistant 29 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5451","q":"Which of the following planets does not have any moon?","o":["Saturn","Jupiter","Venus","Uranus"],"a":2,"x":"Venus (like Mercury) has no moon.","e":"","src":["Personal Assistant 29 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q3464","q":"Which of the following is/are the main mineral of Oceanic crust? I. Silica II. Magnesium","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"The oceanic crust is made mainly of silica and magnesium, so it is called 'sima'; the continental crust is silica and alumina ('sial').","e":"","src":["Store Keeper 19 Apr 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q3812","q":"Which of the following is a domain of environment? I. Hydroshpere II. Lithosphere","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"The four domains of the environment are the lithosphere (land), hydrosphere (water), atmosphere (air) and biosphere (life).","e":"","src":["Fire Operator 22 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q3216","q":"By what amount is the Indian Standard Time ahead of the Greenwich Mean Time?","o":["2 hours 30 minutes","4 hours","5 hours 30 minutes","7 hours"],"a":2,"x":"Indian Standard Time (82°30'E) is 5 hours 30 minutes ahead of Greenwich Mean Time.","e":"15° of longitude = 1 hour.","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 18 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1394","q":"Which of the following eras is considered as the age of the dinosaurs?","o":["Cenozoic","Precambrian","Palaeozoic","Mesozoic"],"a":3,"x":"The Mesozoic Era (about 252–66 million years ago: Triassic, Jurassic, Cretaceous) is the 'Age of Dinosaurs'; it ended with an asteroid-linked mass extinction.","e":"Cenozoic: 'Age of Mammals'. Palaeozoic: 'Age of Fishes and Amphibians'.","src":["TGT 24 Jun 2023 S3"]},{"id":"q2199","q":"One revolution of the earth around the sun takes approximately ___________________.","o":["365 days and 6 hours or one year","366 days and 6 hours or one year","265 days and 6 hours","367 days and 6 hours or one year"],"a":0,"x":"The Earth takes about 365¼ days (365 days 6 hours) to revolve around the Sun; the extra quarter-days make a leap year every four years.","e":"","src":["TGT 13 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2290","q":"If 'B' is the radial outward velocity of galaxy, 'D' is the distance of galaxy from Earth, and 'H' is the Hubble constant. Then, B = ?","o":["H × D","H + D","H - D","H ÷ D"],"a":0,"x":"Hubble's law: a galaxy's recession velocity = Hubble constant × distance (v = H × D).","e":"","src":["TGT 13 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5578","q":"Which is the hottest planet in the solar system?","o":["Mars","Venus","Uranus","Neptune"],"a":1,"x":"Venus is the hottest planet because its thick carbon dioxide atmosphere traps heat.","e":"","src":["TGT 2 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q5584","q":"Which one is not a dwarf planet?","o":["Pluto","Eris","Makemake","Venus"],"a":3,"x":"Pluto, Eris and Makemake are dwarf planets; Venus is a planet.","e":"","src":["TGT 2 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q9826","q":"Which of the following regarding earthquake is correct? I. P waves – Longitudinal II. S waves – Transverse","o":["Both I and II","Only II","Only I","Neither I nor II"],"a":0,"x":"P waves are longitudinal and S waves are transverse, so both statements are correct.","e":"","src":["TGT 1 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q7647","q":"What is the name of the innermost layer of the Earth?","o":["Core","Mantle","Crust","Lithosphere"],"a":0,"x":"The core is the innermost layer of the Earth.","e":"","src":["PGT 29 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q7668","q":"The frigid zone is said to lie near _______.","o":["Poles","Equator","Tropic of Cancer","Tropic of Capricorn"],"a":0,"x":"The frigid zones lie near the poles.","e":"","src":["PGT 29 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q7669","q":"How many longitudinal lines are there?","o":["180","360","45","90"],"a":1,"x":"There are 360 lines of longitude (meridians).","e":"","src":["PGT 29 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q8124","q":"How many days does Mercury take to complete a round around the Sun’s orbit?","o":["20","44","30","88"],"a":3,"x":"Mercury takes about 88 days to go around the Sun.","e":"","src":["PGT 21 Jun 2023 S1"]},{"id":"q8385","q":"Which of the following is NOT included in the planet group?","o":["Mars","Venus","Pluto","Jupiter"],"a":2,"x":"Pluto was reclassified as a dwarf planet in 2006.","e":"","src":["PGT 5 Jul 2023 S1"]},{"id":"q10004","q":"Who among the following, provided evidence that the universe is expanding?","o":["Harold Jeffrey","Edwin Hubble","Immanuel Kant","Carl Weizascar"],"a":1,"x":"Edwin Hubble showed that the universe is expanding.","e":"","src":["Junior Engineer (Electrical) or Section Officer (Electrical) 22 Jun 2022 S2"]},{"id":"q4767","q":"Which of the following is known as the solid crust or the hard top layer of the Earth?","o":["Lithosphere","Biosphere","Atmosphere","Hydrosphere"],"a":0,"x":"The lithosphere is the Earth's solid crust.","e":"","src":["Scientific Assistant (Documents) 25 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5673","q":"Which planet among the following is called the ‘Ice Giant’?","o":["Saturn","Jupiter","Uranus","Mercury"],"a":2,"x":"Uranus and Neptune are called ice giants; Jupiter and Saturn are gas giants.","e":"","src":["Driver 30 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q8197","q":"Which of these constellations is visible during winter in the early part of the night and looks like a distorted letter W or M?","o":["Orion","Canis Major","Cassiopeia","Ursa Minor"],"a":2,"x":"Cassiopeia looks like a distorted W or M in the winter sky.","e":"","src":["Junior Engineer (Electrical) or Section Officer (Electrical) 22 Jun 2022 S1"]},{"id":"q9790","q":"Big Dipper, the Great Bear or the Saptarshi is an example of ______.","o":["Constellations","Asteroids","Meteoroids","Planets"],"a":0,"x":"The Big Dipper (Saptarishi) is a constellation.","e":"","src":["Junior Engineer 28 Jun 2022 S2"]},{"id":"q9970","q":"Which planet takes nearly 164 years to complete one orbit around the Sun?","o":["Jupiter","Uranus","Saturn","Neptune"],"a":3,"x":"Neptune takes about 164 years to orbit the Sun.","e":"","src":["Junior Engineer (Electrical) or Section Officer (Electrical) 22 Jun 2022 S3"]}]},{"id":"physical","n":"Physical Geography","qs":[{"id":"q0026","q":"‘ El Nino ’ is an ocean current of which of the following oceans?","o":["Atlantic Ocean","Indian Ocean","Pacific Ocean","Arctic Ocean"],"a":2,"x":"El Niño is the periodic warming of the central and eastern tropical Pacific Ocean off Peru; it tends to weaken the Indian monsoon.","e":"La Niña is the opposite, cooling phase.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0154","q":"The Inter-Tropical Convergence Zone (ITCZ) shifts in which of the following directions during the summer season?","o":["South","East","North","West"],"a":2,"x":"In summer the ITCZ moves north with the overhead Sun. Over India it reaches about 20–25°N (the monsoon trough), pulling in the south-west monsoon.","e":"In winter it moves south of the equator.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0193","q":"Which of the following processes is most responsible for the formation of exfoliation domes in granitic terrains?","o":["Chemical weathering","Biological activity","Freeze-thaw action","Pressure release"],"a":3,"x":"Exfoliation domes form by pressure release (unloading): as overlying rock erodes away, granite expands and peels off in curved sheets.","e":"Example: Half Dome in Yosemite, USA.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0270","q":"Which of the following factors influences the temperature and rainfall pattern of a place?","o":["Area","Altitude","Population density","Ocean depth"],"a":1,"x":"Altitude affects temperature and rainfall: temperature falls about 6.5°C for every 1,000 m of height.","e":"That is why Shimla is cooler than Delhi in summer.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0315","q":"Which of the following factors is NOT a primary influence on the formation and direction of ocean currents?","o":["Salinity gradients","Wind patterns","Temperature differentials","Lunar phases"],"a":3,"x":"Ocean currents are shaped by winds, differences in temperature and salinity, and Earth's rotation (Coriolis force). Lunar phases affect tides, not currents.","e":"The Gulf Stream is a warm current; the Labrador Current a cold one.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0347","q":"Which of the following rocks is primarily formed through the organic accumulation and compaction of plant material?","o":["Sandstone","Limestone","Stalactites","Coal"],"a":3,"x":"Coal is an organic sedimentary rock formed as buried plant matter was compacted and heated over millions of years.","e":"Stages: peat → lignite → bituminous → anthracite (highest carbon).","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0348","q":"Which of the following statements related to minerals is correct?","o":["They are naturally occurring substances with undefinable internal structures.","They are homogenous, naturally occurring substances with definable internal structures.","They are heterogenous, naturally occurring substances with definable internal structures.","They are heterogenous, artificially occurring substances."],"a":1,"x":"A mineral is a homogeneous, naturally occurring substance with a definite chemical composition and a definable internal structure.","e":"Rocks are combinations of minerals; granite contains quartz, feldspar and mica.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0400","q":"Which of the following are formed due to deposition, accumulation and concentration in horizontal strata?","o":["Igneous rocks","Iron ores","Metamorphic rocks","Sedimentary rocks"],"a":3,"x":"Sedimentary rocks form as sediments are deposited, accumulated and compacted in horizontal layers, and minerals in them occur in beds.","e":"Examples: sandstone, shale, limestone; coal and potash also occur in beds.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0422","q":"Which of the following are types of extrusive igneous rocks? 1. Pumice 2. Obsidian 3. Andesite 4. Sphalerite","o":["Only 1 and 2 are correct.","Only 1 is correct.","Only 2 is correct.","Only 1, 2 and 3 are correct."],"a":3,"x":"Extrusive (volcanic) igneous rocks cool quickly on the surface: pumice, obsidian and andesite (also basalt). Sphalerite is a zinc ore mineral, not a rock.","e":"Extrusive = cools fast on the surface = fine grains or glass.","src":["ASO 22 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0473","q":"The shaking energies of earthquakes of magnitude 6 and 4 on the Richter scale differ by a factor of:","o":["ten","hundred","five","two"],"a":1,"x":"The Richter scale is logarithmic: each whole number is a 10-times rise in measured shaking amplitude, so magnitudes 6 and 4 differ by 10 × 10 = 100.","e":"Energy released rises about 31.6 times per unit, so about 1,000 times between 4 and 6.","src":["ASO 27 Aug 2025 S1"],"k":"In the strict sense, energy differs by about 1,000 times; DSSSB's key uses the 100-fold difference in shaking amplitude."},{"id":"q0497","q":"Which among the following types of mountains is also known as \"Horst\"?","o":["Volcanic Mountains","Fold Mountains","Block Mountains","Residual Mountains"],"a":2,"x":"Block mountains form when land between faults rises; the raised block is a horst and a sunken block is a graben (rift valley).","e":"Examples: Vosges and Black Forest (Europe); Satpura in India is often cited.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0697","q":"Which among the following type of mountains is also known as \"Mountains of Accumulation\"?","o":["Block Mountains","Residual Mountains","Volcanic Mountains","Fold Mountains"],"a":2,"x":"Volcanic mountains are called 'mountains of accumulation' because they build up from lava and ash piling around a vent.","e":"Examples: Mount Fuji (Japan), Mount Kilimanjaro (Tanzania).","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1751","q":"_____________ are also called Primary Rocks.","o":["Igneous Rocks","Metamorphic Rocks","Sedimentary Rocks","Igneous and Sedimentary Rocks"],"a":0,"x":"Igneous rocks are called primary rocks because they form first, from cooled magma, and other rocks form from them.","e":"Igneous → sedimentary → metamorphic.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1796","q":"Which layer of atmosphere is used in Radio Transmission?","o":["Thermosphere","Exosphere","Troposphere","Mesosphere"],"a":0,"x":"The ionosphere, within the thermosphere (about 80–400 km), reflects radio waves back to earth and makes long-distance radio transmission possible.","e":"Troposphere → stratosphere (ozone) → mesosphere → thermosphere (ionosphere) → exosphere.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1837","q":"Which of the following is the example of Intrusive Igneous Rock? I. Granite II. Basalt","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":0,"x":"Granite is an intrusive igneous rock (cooled slowly underground, large crystals); basalt is extrusive (cooled quickly at the surface).","e":"Intrusive = inside the earth = coarse grains.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q1947","q":"Which of the following is/are factor(s) of soil formation? I. Time II. Parent Rock","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Soil forms from parent rock over time, shaped by climate, relief and living organisms.","e":"Five factors: parent rock, climate, relief, organisms, time.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1949","q":"Which is the third layer of the atmosphere?","o":["Exosphere","Mesosphere","Stratosphere","Thermosphere"],"a":1,"x":"From the ground up the atmosphere's layers are troposphere, stratosphere, mesosphere (the third), thermosphere and exosphere.","e":"The mesosphere burns up meteors.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1991","q":"Stratosphere extends up to a height of ______ km.","o":["100","20","60","50"],"a":3,"x":"The stratosphere lies above the troposphere and reaches up to about 50 km; it contains the ozone layer and is good for jet flights.","e":"Troposphere to about 13 km; stratosphere to about 50 km.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2034","q":"Which of the following is/are a type of tide? I. Neap II. Spring","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Spring tides (very high) come at full and new moon, when Sun, Moon and Earth line up; neap tides (low) come at the first and third quarter moon.","e":"Spring = strong; neap = weak.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2079","q":"Which of the following statement regarding Exosphere is correct? I. This is the upper most layer of the atmosphere. II. This layer has very thin air. III. Light gases like Helium and Hydrogen float into the space from here.","o":["Only I","II and III","Only III","I, II and III"],"a":3,"x":"The exosphere is the uppermost layer of the atmosphere; its air is extremely thin and light gases like helium and hydrogen escape into space from it. All three statements are correct.","e":"Exosphere: the outer edge of the atmosphere.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2109","q":"Which layers of earth is responsible for tremors?","o":["Outer core","Inner core","Crust","Mantle"],"a":2,"x":"Tremors (earthquakes) happen because of movements in the earth's crust (NCERT Class 7).","e":"The point where it starts is the focus; above it on the surface is the epicentre.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2114","q":"Ionosphere is part of which layer of atmosphere?","o":["Thermosphere","Mesosphere","Stratosphere","Troposphere"],"a":0,"x":"The ionosphere is part of the thermosphere (about 80–400 km); it reflects radio waves.","e":"Radio waves bounce off the ionosphere.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2116","q":"Which layer of environment protects us from the harmful rays and scorching heat of the sun?","o":["Biosphere","Atmosphere","Hydrosphere","Lithosphere"],"a":1,"x":"The atmosphere protects living things from the Sun's harmful ultraviolet rays (through the ozone layer) and from extreme heat.","e":"Ozone layer = stratosphere.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2156","q":"What is the drainage pattern called which resembles the branches of a tree?","o":["Radial","Centripetal","Dendritic","Trellis"],"a":2,"x":"A dendritic drainage pattern looks like the branches of a tree; it forms on uniform rock, e.g. the Ganga plain rivers.","e":"Radial: from a central high (Amarkantak); trellis: folded rocks; centripetal: into a basin.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2402","q":"What are the longitudinal sand ridges and crescent shaped sand dunes located in the extended Peninsular plateau called?","o":["Barchans","Arroyo","Esker","Ejecta blanket"],"a":0,"x":"Barchans are crescent-shaped sand dunes; along with longitudinal sand ridges they cover the Thar desert (NCERT).","e":"Barchan horns point downwind.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2446","q":"What are the meanders associated with?","o":["The mature stage of a river in the evolution of fluvial land forms","The premature stage of a mountain formation","The mature stage of an ocean bed formation","The mature stage of a low land formation due to air erosion"],"a":0,"x":"Meanders, the large bends of a river, form in its mature stage on flat plains where lateral erosion dominates.","e":"Cut-off meanders become ox-bow lakes.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2674","q":"Which of the following pair of ocean current – type is/are correct? I. Labrador – Cold II. Gulf – Warm","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"The Labrador Current is cold and the Gulf Stream is warm; where they meet off Newfoundland, fog and rich fishing grounds result.","e":"Grand Banks fishing ground.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2912","q":"Which is the lowest layer of the atmosphere?","o":["Troposphere","Thermosphere","Mesosphere","Stratosphere"],"a":0,"x":"The troposphere is the lowest layer of the atmosphere (up to about 13 km); all weather happens in it.","e":"","src":["Junior Clerk 17 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6200","q":"Ground water stood between layers of hard rock below the water table is known as _____________.","o":["aquifer","well","pond","bawri"],"a":0,"x":"Groundwater held between layers of hard rock below the water table is an aquifer.","e":"","src":["Junior Clerk 9 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q6206","q":"Which of the following statement regarding lithosphere is correct? I. It is made up of rocks and minerals. II. It is covered by a thin layer of soil. III. It is also a source of mineral wealth.","o":["Only I","II and III","Only II","I, II and III"],"a":3,"x":"The lithosphere is made of rocks and minerals, covered by a thin layer of soil, and is a source of mineral wealth (NCERT Class 7).","e":"","src":["Junior Clerk 9 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q6207","q":"Which of the following statement regarding Earthquake waves is correct? I. P-waves are longitudinal waves. II. S-waves are transverse waves.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"P-waves (primary) are longitudinal and S-waves (secondary) are transverse; S-waves cannot pass through liquids.","e":"","src":["Junior Clerk 9 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q4049","q":"Which among the following mountain ranges is the longest continental mountain range in the world?","o":["Andes","Rockies","Himalayas","Western Ghats"],"a":0,"x":"The Andes in South America (about 7,000 km) is the longest continental mountain range in the world.","e":"","src":["Stenographer 23 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q5012","q":"As the river enters the plain, it twists and turns to forming large bends known as:","o":["ox-bow","meanders","delta","levees"],"a":1,"x":"On the plains a river winds in large bends called meanders; cut-off meanders become ox-bow lakes.","e":"","src":["Stenographer 26 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6858","q":"The aurora (the Northern Lights and Southern Lights) occur in the ___________ layer of atmosphere.","o":["thermosphere","mesosphere","stratosphere","exosphere"],"a":0,"x":"The auroras (Northern and Southern Lights) occur in the thermosphere.","e":"","src":["Stenographer 14 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q6909","q":"A major part of the ____________ is the marine ecosystem in the ocean.","o":["hydrosphere","lithosphere","atmosphere","biosphere"],"a":0,"x":"Oceans, and so marine ecosystems, form the major part of the hydrosphere.","e":"","src":["Stenographer 14 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q8922","q":"The Peninsular Plateau of India is mainly composed of:","o":["Ancient crystalline, igneous, and metamorphic rocks of Precambrian origin","Marine limestone formations of the Mesozoic era","Extensive basaltic lava flows from the Holocene period","Quaternary alluvial deposits spread across river valleys"],"a":0,"x":"The Peninsular Plateau is mainly made of ancient crystalline, igneous and metamorphic rocks of Precambrian age.","e":"","src":["MTS 3 Mar 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q9320","q":"The Deccan Plateau is largely composed of which of the following?","o":["Sandstone","Basaltic lava","Alluvial soil","Granite only"],"a":1,"x":"Much of the Deccan Plateau, especially the Deccan Traps, is made of basaltic lava.","e":"","src":["MTS 24 Feb 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q3599","q":"Which of the following is a solid form of water?","o":["Water in oceans","Water vapour","Glaciers","Natural geysers"],"a":2,"x":"Glaciers are water in solid form (ice); oceans are liquid and water vapour is gas.","e":"","src":["Fire Operator 20 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q3637","q":"Wind erosion is significant in which of the following regions?","o":["Wetlands","Coastal regions","Heavy rainfall and steep slopes","Arid and semi arid regions"],"a":3,"x":"Wind erosion is most significant in arid and semi-arid regions with loose, dry soil and little vegetation.","e":"","src":["Fire Operator 20 Apr 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q3214","q":"Which of the following valleys is a deep valley with very steep to straight sides and is almost equal in width at its top as well as its bottom?","o":["Gorge","Canyon","V-shaped valley","Rift Valley"],"a":0,"x":"A gorge is a deep valley with very steep, almost vertical sides, about as wide at the top as at the bottom; a canyon is wider at the top.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 18 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q3215","q":"Which of the following clouds are formed at high altitudes of 8000-12000 m?","o":["Cirrus","Cumulus","Stratus","Nimbus"],"a":0,"x":"Cirrus clouds form at high altitudes (8,000–12,000 m) and are thin, feathery and made of ice crystals.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 18 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q3217","q":"When the rivers originate from a hill and flow in all directions, what is the drainage pattern called?","o":["Dendritic","Trellis","Radial","Centripetal"],"a":2,"x":"A radial drainage pattern forms when rivers flow outward in all directions from a central hill, e.g. from Amarkantak.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 18 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q3259","q":"Which of the following clouds areblack or dark grey in colour?","o":["Cumulus","Nimbus","Cirrus","Stratus"],"a":1,"x":"Nimbus clouds are dark grey or black rain-bearing clouds at low altitude.","e":"Cumulonimbus = thunderclouds.","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 18 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q4338","q":"Which of the following scales is used to measure the intensity of a tornado?","o":["Beaufort Scale","Decibel Scale","Richter Scale","Fujita Scale"],"a":3,"x":"The Fujita (now Enhanced Fujita) scale rates tornado intensity from damage.","e":"Richter – earthquakes; Beaufort – wind speed.","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 24 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q4621","q":"Limnology refers to the study of:","o":["Marine life and ecosystems.","Inland freshwater both standing and running.","Igneous, metamorphic and sedimentary rocks.","All aspects of soil."],"a":1,"x":"Limnology is the study of inland waters: lakes, ponds and rivers.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 25 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6357","q":"Which of the following pair is correct? I. Thermosphere – Helps in radio waves transmission. II. Mesosphere – Meteoroids burn up in this layer.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"The ionosphere in the thermosphere reflects radio waves; meteors burn up in the mesosphere.","e":"","src":["Care Taker 8 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q0970","q":"Which global pressure belt lies around 30° North and South latitudes and is associated with arid climates?","o":["Equatorial low","Polar high","Sub-tropical high","Sub-polar low"],"a":2,"x":"The sub-tropical high-pressure belts lie around 30°N and 30°S; descending dry air there creates most of the world's hot deserts (Sahara, Thar, Kalahari).","e":"Also called the 'horse latitudes'.","src":["PRT 18 Feb 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q1125","q":"Which of the following is the primary cause of wind speed in different regions of India?","o":["Unequal heating of the Earth's surface","Earth's magnetic field","Rotation of the Moon","Ocean currents"],"a":0,"x":"Wind is caused by differences in air pressure, which come from unequal heating of the earth's surface; air moves from high to low pressure.","e":"Land heats faster than water, hence sea and land breezes.","src":["PRT 26 Mar 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1446","q":"The sinuous curves made by a river along its course are known as ______.","o":["aretes","meanders","moraines","cirques"],"a":1,"x":"Meanders are the loop-like bends a river makes in its middle and lower course; a cut-off meander becomes an oxbow lake.","e":"Aretes, cirques and moraines are glacial landforms.","src":["TGT 25 Jun 2023 S3"]},{"id":"q2286","q":"The ozone layer exists in which among the following regions of the atmosphere?","o":["Stratosphere","Mesosphere","Thermosphere","Exosphere"],"a":0,"x":"The ozone layer is in the stratosphere.","e":"","src":["TGT 13 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5693","q":"As saltwater, what percentage of the earth's water is found in oceans?","o":["0.57","0.97","0.67","0.99"],"a":1,"x":"About 97% of the Earth's water is salt water in the oceans.","e":"","src":["TGT 4 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q5865","q":"Which of the following statement is correct? I. The Labrador Ocean current is warm current. II. Gulf stream is a cold current.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":3,"x":"Both are wrong: the Labrador Current is cold and the Gulf Stream is warm.","e":"","src":["TGT 6 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q6260","q":"Which of the following statement is correct? I. Evaporation of water may take place in absence of sunlight. II. Water vapour gets added to air by evaporation and transpiration.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Evaporation can happen without sunlight, and water vapour enters the air through evaporation and transpiration.","e":"","src":["TGT 9 Oct 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q7513","q":"Which of the following statement is correct? I. Sedimentary rocks may contains fossils. II. Igneous rocks can change into metamorphic rocks under great heat and pressure.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Sedimentary rocks may contain fossils, and igneous rocks can turn into metamorphic rocks under heat and pressure.","e":"","src":["TGT 1 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q7533","q":"Which of the following is correct about insolation? I. It influences distribution of temperature. II. It increases from the equator towards the poles.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":0,"x":"Insolation influences temperature, but it decreases (not increases) from the equator to the poles.","e":"","src":["TGT 7 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q7556","q":"Which of the following pair is correct? I. Permanent wind – westerlies II. Local wind – loo","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"The westerlies are permanent winds and the loo is a local wind, so both pairs are correct.","e":"","src":["TGT 7 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q9806","q":"According to Köppen classification, what type of climate does Arunachal Pradesh experience?","o":["Cwg – Monsoon with dry winter","Amw – Monsoon with short dry season","Bwhw – Semi-arid steppe climate","Dfc – Cold humid winter with short summer"],"a":3,"x":"In Köppen's scheme, Arunachal Pradesh has a Dfc climate: cold, humid winters with short summers (NCERT).","e":"","src":["TGT 24 Aug 2025"]},{"id":"q8823","q":"What role do the Himalayas play in influencing the climate of India?","o":["They cause a reduction in monsoon rainfall in southern India.","They increase the effect of the summer monsoon in the country.","They prevent cold winds from entering northern India.","They allow tropical winds to influence India’s climate."],"a":2,"x":"The Himalayas block cold Central Asian winds from entering northern India.","e":"","src":["Special Educator 26 Jun 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q8852","q":"The Indo-Gangetic Plain is described as an \"aggradational plain.\" What does this primarily mean?","o":["It was formed by the erosion of mountains.","It was formed by the shifting of tectonic plates.","It was formed by the depositional work of rivers.","It was formed by volcanic activity."],"a":2,"x":"The Indo-Gangetic Plain is aggradational: it was built up by rivers depositing silt.","e":"","src":["Special Educator 14 Mar 2026"]},{"id":"q6591","q":"____________ is the uppermost layer of the atmosphere.","o":["Mesosphere","Stratosphere","Exosphere","Ionosphere"],"a":2,"x":"The exosphere is the outermost layer of the atmosphere.","e":"","src":["PGT 3 Jul 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6637","q":"Which among the following mountains is an example of volcanic mountains?","o":["Mount Fuji","Himalayas","Vosges Mountain","Rockies"],"a":0,"x":"Mount Fuji in Japan is a volcanic mountain; the Himalayas and Rockies are fold mountains.","e":"","src":["PGT 18 Jul 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6682","q":"Two statements are given below. Which of the following statement(s) is/are true? 1. A block mountain is also called as Horst. 2. The Satpura mountain range is an example of Relict Mountains in India.","o":["Only 1","Only 2","Both 1&2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":2,"x":"A block mountain is also called a horst, and the Satpura range is an example of relict mountains.","e":"","src":["PGT 30 Jul 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q7085","q":"Which of the following statement is correct? I. Air pressure is lowest at sea level. II. Air always moves from high to low pressure areas.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":1,"x":"Air pressure is highest at sea level, and air moves from high to low pressure, so only II is correct.","e":"","src":["PGT 17 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q7103","q":"Which of the following is a type of igneous rock? I. Intrusive II. Extrusive","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Igneous rocks are intrusive (formed inside the earth) or extrusive (formed on the surface).","e":"","src":["PGT 11 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q7104","q":"Which of the following pair is correct? I. Stratosphere – second layer II. Thermosphere – third layer","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":0,"x":"The stratosphere is the second layer; the thermosphere is the fourth, not the third.","e":"","src":["PGT 11 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q7131","q":"The lower most layer of Soil is called what?","o":["A-Horizon","Bedrock","B-Horizon","C-Horizon"],"a":1,"x":"The lowest layer beneath the soil horizons is the bedrock.","e":"","src":["PGT 31 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q7252","q":"Which among the following statements is incorrect?","o":["Igneous rocks are formed from molten rock deep inside the Earth.","Sedimentary rocks are formed from layers of sand, silt, dead plants, and animal skeletons.","Metamorphic rocks are formed from other rocks that are changed by heat and pressure underground.","Sandstone is an example of igneous rock."],"a":3,"x":"Sandstone is a sedimentary rock, not igneous, so statement D is wrong.","e":"","src":["PGT 29 Jul 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q7623","q":"Salinity is expressed as the amount of salt (in grams) dissolved in sea water per how many grams?","o":["100 Grams","1000 Grams","100 Kilograms","10000 Grams"],"a":1,"x":"Salinity is the grams of salt dissolved in 1,000 grams of sea water.","e":"","src":["PGT 17 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q7646","q":"Which of the following rocks is formed through molten magma?","o":["Metamorphic Rocks","Sedimentary Rocks","Igneous Rocks","Mineral Rocks"],"a":2,"x":"Igneous rocks form from molten magma.","e":"","src":["PGT 29 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q7758","q":"What was Panthalassa?","o":["Mega-Ocean","Mega-continent","Mega-glacier","Mega-mountain"],"a":0,"x":"Panthalassa was the vast global ocean surrounding the supercontinent Pangaea.","e":"","src":["PGT 22 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q8438","q":"The word ‘Monsoon’ is derived from","o":["the Arabic word ‘Mausim’","the Greek word ‘Mosin’","the Roman word ‘Moonsim’","the Latin word ‘Monsin’"],"a":0,"x":"'Monsoon' comes from the Arabic word 'mausim', meaning season.","e":"","src":["PGT 22 Jun 2023 S1"]},{"id":"q9667","q":"Which among the following layers of atmosphere rises due to the presence of ozone?","o":["Mesosphere","Stratosphere","Exosphere","Ionosphere"],"a":1,"x":"Temperature rises with height in the stratosphere because ozone absorbs UV radiation.","e":"","src":["PGT 29 Jun 2021"]},{"id":"q10032","q":"The distinct zone in the ocean below which the salinity increases sharply is known as ______.","o":["Hydration","Insolation","Halocline","Infiltration"],"a":2,"x":"The halocline is the ocean layer where salinity changes sharply with depth.","e":"","src":["Junior Engineer (Civil) or Section Officer (Civil) 27 Jun 2022 S1"]},{"id":"q4444","q":"The chemical processes responsible for decomposition of rocks with the help of water and atmospheric gases is called ____________.","o":["chemical weathering","decomposition","erosion","hydrolysis"],"a":0,"x":"The breakdown of rocks by water and atmospheric gases is chemical weathering.","e":"","src":["Laboratory Assistant (Documents) 24 Oct 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q4724","q":"Planetary wind is also known as __________.","o":["Seasonal Wind","Primary wind","Monsoon Wind","Pressure wind"],"a":1,"x":"Planetary winds are also called primary or permanent winds.","e":"","src":["Laboratory Assistant (HRD Quality Control) 25 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q4903","q":"Stratosphere, the layer of earth's atmosphere, lies between the troposphere and:","o":["lithosphere","thermosphere","mesosphere","exosphere"],"a":2,"x":"The stratosphere lies between the troposphere and the mesosphere.","e":"","src":["Assistant Bacteriologist 26 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q4907","q":"Which of the following mountains is a fold mountain with rugged relief and high conical peaks?","o":["The Vindhya Mountains","The Satpura Mountains","The Kilimanjaro Mountains","The Himalayan Mountains"],"a":3,"x":"The Himalayas are young fold mountains with rugged relief and high conical peaks.","e":"","src":["Assistant Bacteriologist 26 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5057","q":"The breaking up and decay of exposed rocks, by temperature changes, frost action, plants, animals and human activity is called as-","o":["Landslide","Tempering","Weathering","Annealing"],"a":2,"x":"Weathering is the breakdown of exposed rocks by temperature change, frost, plants, animals and people.","e":"","src":["Scientific Assistant (Physics) 26 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q5230","q":"The rhythmic rise and fall of ocean water twice in a day is called _______.","o":["wave","tide","ocean current","tsunami"],"a":1,"x":"The rhythmic rise and fall of sea water twice a day is the tide.","e":"","src":["Senior Scientific Assistant (Chemistry) 27 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5247","q":"Which among the following instruments is used to measure the wind force?","o":["Anemometer","Hygrometer","Altimeter","Galvanometer"],"a":0,"x":"An anemometer measures wind speed.","e":"","src":["Assistant Engineer (Civil) 27 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5269","q":"Which of the following statement is NOT correct? I. Water vapour gets added to air by evaporation and transpiration. II. The circulation of water between ocean and land is known as the water cycle.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":3,"x":"Both statements are correct, so neither is 'not correct'.","e":"","src":["Senior Scientific Assistant (Physics) 27 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q6292","q":"Which of the following options is a model that describes the formation, breakdown and reformation of a rock as a result of sedimentary, igneous and metamorphic processes?","o":["Rock yard","Rock leveling","Rock cycle","Rock genesis"],"a":2,"x":"The rock cycle describes how rocks form, break down and re-form through igneous, sedimentary and metamorphic processes.","e":"","src":["Section Officer (Horticulture) 9 Oct 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q6709","q":"Which of the following pair is correct? I. Seasonal winds – blow throughout the year in a particular direction. II. Local winds – blow only during a particular period of day or year in a small area.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":1,"x":"Local winds blow in a small area at particular times; winds blowing all year in one direction are permanent, not seasonal.","e":"","src":["Investigator 26 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q7031","q":"Which among the following statements is incorrect?","o":["The Mesosphere is found above the stratosphere.","Stratosphere is the third layer of the atmosphere.","Mesosphere is the coldest of the atmospheric layers.","The ozone layer is found in Stratosphere."],"a":1,"x":"The stratosphere is the second layer of the atmosphere, not the third.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Medical Lab Technology) 1 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q7380","q":"Which of the following pair is correct? I. Stratosphere – extends upto 80 km II. Mesosphere – extends upto 50 km","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":3,"x":"Both are wrong: the stratosphere extends up to about 50 km and the mesosphere up to about 80 km.","e":"","src":["Physical Education Teacher 2 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q7403","q":"Which of the following statement is correct? I. Average height of troposphere is 13 km. II. Fog and hailstorm occur in troposphere.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"The troposphere averages about 13 km in height, and weather such as fog and hailstorms occurs in it.","e":"","src":["Physical Education Teacher 2 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q8248","q":"With respect to storms, what are typhoons?","o":["Hurricane-equivalent tropical cyclones in Western Pacific Ocean","Tropical cyclones with sustained winds of 74 m/h in Atlantic and Eastern, Central Pacific oceans","Tropical cyclones in the Northern Indian Ocean, which consists of the Arabian Sea and the Bay of Bengal","Tropical cyclone in Southern Indian Ocean"],"a":0,"x":"Typhoons are hurricane-strength tropical cyclones in the western Pacific Ocean.","e":"","src":["Librarian 10 Jul 2023 S1"]},{"id":"q9882","q":"In which of the following regions of India are barchans found?","o":["Peninsular Plateau","Ganga plain","Thar Desert","Coastal plains"],"a":2,"x":"Barchans (crescent-shaped dunes) are found in the Thar Desert.","e":"","src":["Scientific Assistant 27 Jul 2024 S2"]},{"id":"q9885","q":"What is the Environmental Temperature Normal Lapse Rate?","o":["6.5°C/km","5.5°C/km","10°C/km","4.5°C/km"],"a":0,"x":"The normal lapse rate is about 6.5°C per km.","e":"","src":["Scientific Assistant 27 Jul 2024 S2"]},{"id":"q9996","q":"How many major factors controls the climate of any place?","o":["Three","Six","Five","Four"],"a":1,"x":"NCERT lists six factors controlling climate: latitude, altitude, pressure and winds, distance from the sea, ocean currents and relief.","e":"","src":["Junior Engineer (Electrical) or Section Officer (Electrical) 22 Jun 2022 S2"]}]},{"id":"india","n":"India: Relief, Rivers and Climate","qs":[{"id":"q0025","q":"The suddenness and abruptness of the Indian Monsoon is known as:","o":["Break of the Monsoon","Onset of the Monsoon","Retreating Monsoon","Burst of the Monsoon"],"a":3,"x":"The sudden onset of the south-west monsoon with heavy rain, thunder and lightning in early June is called the 'burst' of the monsoon.","e":"It reaches the Kerala coast first, around 1 June.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0028","q":"The _______ is/are flanked by a stretch of narrow coastal strips, running along the Arabian Sea on the west and the Bay of Bengal on the east.","o":["Narmada river","Peninsula Plateau","Great Indian Desert","Lakshadweep Islands"],"a":1,"x":"The Peninsular Plateau is edged by the narrow Western Coastal Plains along the Arabian Sea and the broader Eastern Coastal Plains along the Bay of Bengal.","e":"The east coast is wider because big rivers (Mahanadi, Godavari, Krishna, Kaveri) build deltas there.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0033","q":"Which of the following originates from the Brahmagiri hills?","o":["Kaveri","Sabarmati","Narmada","Chambal"],"a":0,"x":"The Kaveri rises at Talakaveri in the Brahmagiri hills of Kodagu (Coorg), Karnataka, and reaches the Bay of Bengal through Tamil Nadu.","e":"Don't confuse it with the Godavari, which rises near Trimbakeshwar (Nashik) — also on a hill called Brahmagiri.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0065","q":"Select the correct pair of rivers which flow into the Bay of Bengal.","o":["Beas and Yamuna","Krishna and Kosi","Tapi and Mahanadi","Mahi and Damodar"],"a":1,"x":"The Krishna empties into the Bay of Bengal directly; the Kosi reaches it through the Ganga. Every other pair has a river that drains to the Arabian Sea or into the Ganga.","e":"West-flowing rivers to the Arabian Sea: Narmada, Tapi, Mahi, Sabarmati.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S2"],"k":"The Kosi is a tributary of the Ganga, so this pair is correct only in the sense of draining to the Bay of Bengal."},{"id":"q0066","q":"What is the local name of the calcareous soil deposit found in the upper part of the Ganga-Brahmaputra flood plains?","o":["Regur","Kankar","Khadar","Lal Mati"],"a":1,"x":"Kankar are calcareous (lime) nodules found in the older alluvium of the Ganga plains, in the drier upper parts away from the fresh floodplains.","e":"Bhangar = older alluvium away from rivers; khadar = new alluvium on the floodplains.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0073","q":"What is the total length of the coastline of India's mainland and island groups?","o":["7517 Kilometres","7577 Kilometres","7751 Kilometres","7157 Kilometres"],"a":0,"x":"India's traditional coastline figure, mainland plus islands, is 7,516.6 km (about 7,517 km), touching 9 states and 4 UTs.","e":"Gujarat has the longest mainland coastline.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S2"],"k":"A 2023-24 re-measurement with new methods puts the coastline at 11,098 km; exam keys set before that use 7,517 km."},{"id":"q0110","q":"Read the statements regarding the Ghaghara river. A. It originates from the glaciers of Mapchachungo. B. Tila, Seti and Beri are tributaries of the Ghaghara.","o":["Both A and B are correct.","Both A and B are incorrect.","Only A is correct.","Only B is correct."],"a":0,"x":"Both are right: the Ghaghara rises in the glaciers of Mapchachungo, and collects the Tila, Seti and Beri before leaving the mountains at Shishapani.","e":"The Ghaghara joins the Ganga near Chhapra in Bihar.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0144","q":"Which of the following lie(s) to the north of the Narmada river and covers a major area of the Malwa Plateau?","o":["The Central Highlands","Eastern Ghats","Western Ghats","Deccan Plateau"],"a":0,"x":"The Central Highlands lie north of the Narmada and cover most of the Malwa Plateau; the Deccan Plateau lies south of the Narmada.","e":"Rivers of the Central Highlands (Chambal, Betwa, Ken) flow north-east.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0189","q":"Which of the following is locally known as 'Mahawat' in the northern and northern plains of India?","o":["Cloudburst during the monsoon season","Cyclonic rainfall during winter season","Hot and dry winds during summer season","Cold waves during winter season"],"a":1,"x":"'Mahawat' is the local name for the light winter rain in north and north-west India from cyclonic western disturbances; it helps rabi crops such as wheat.","e":"The disturbances come from the Mediterranean region.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0197","q":"The Manasbal lake is located in which of the following Union Territories of India?","o":["Jammu and Kashmir","Ladakh","Chandigarh","Andaman and Nicobar Islands"],"a":0,"x":"Manasbal Lake is in Ganderbal district of Jammu and Kashmir and is known for lotus blooms and birdlife.","e":"Other J&K lakes: Dal, Wular (largest freshwater lake), Anchar. Ladakh has Pangong Tso and Tso Moriri.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0200","q":"Which of the following weather conditions is/are typical during the cold weather season in north India?","o":["Heavy rainfall","Clear sky, low temperatures, and low humidity","Hot and humid weather","Thunderstorms"],"a":1,"x":"North India's cold weather season (December–February) has clear skies, low temperatures, low humidity and light winds.","e":"The exception is light rain from western disturbances.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0231","q":"Which of the following contributes to most of the rainfall in India?","o":["Western Disturbances","Northeast Monsoon","Southwest Monsoon","Retreating Monsoon"],"a":2,"x":"The south-west monsoon (June–September) brings about 75% of India's annual rainfall.","e":"The north-east (retreating) monsoon matters mainly for Tamil Nadu's Coromandel coast.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0233","q":"How many seasons are officially designated by the Indian Meteorological Department (IMD) in India?","o":["3","4","2","5"],"a":1,"x":"The IMD recognises four seasons: winter (Jan–Feb), pre-monsoon or summer (Mar–May), south-west monsoon (Jun–Sep) and post-monsoon (Oct–Dec).","e":"Traditional Indian calendars count six seasons.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0264","q":"What is the average temperature range in Chennai during the cold weather season?","o":["24°C–25°C","30°C–35°C","10°C–15°C","15°C–20°C"],"a":0,"x":"Chennai stays warm in the cold season, around 24–25°C, because the sea moderates its temperature.","e":"In the same season the north-western plains average 10–15°C.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0302","q":"What are the strong, gusty, hot, dry daytime winds of north and north-west India called?","o":["Westerlies","Easterlies","Loo","Monsoon winds"],"a":2,"x":"The loo is the strong, gusty, hot and dry daytime wind of north and north-west India in May–June.","e":"Pre-monsoon showers elsewhere: 'mango showers' (Kerala, Karnataka), 'Kal Baisakhi' (Bengal, Assam).","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0310","q":"Which of the following states of India does NOT experience 'Loo' in the summer?","o":["Haryana","Punjab","Telangana","Rajasthan"],"a":2,"x":"The loo blows over the north-western plains: Punjab, Haryana, Rajasthan, Uttar Pradesh, Delhi and Bihar. Telangana, on the Deccan Plateau, does not get it.","e":"Rajasthan's Thar Desert is where the loo is hottest.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0319","q":"Which statement about tropical rainforests is correct?","o":["They are found in areas with less than 70 cm of rain.","Their trees shed leaves according to season.","They are found in areas with more than 200 cm of rain.","They experience a long dry season every year."],"a":2,"x":"Tropical evergreen rainforests grow where rain exceeds 200 cm a year, such as the Western Ghats, the north-east, and the Andaman and Nicobar Islands.","e":"They stay green all year: rosewood, mahogany, ebony.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0354","q":"What kind of vegetation is found in the desert of Rajasthan?","o":["Tropical Deciduous Forests","Mangrove Forests","Montane Forests","Tropical Thorn Forests and Scrubs"],"a":3,"x":"The Rajasthan desert has tropical thorn forests and scrub: acacia, khejri, cactus and babool, suited to less than 70 cm of rain.","e":"Plants there have long roots and thick, small leaves to save water.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0355","q":"Which of the following are the tributaries of the Indus river system?","o":["Ganga, Ken and Betwa","Shyok, Gilgit and Hunza","Chenab, Jhelum and Yamuna","Beas, Satluj and Ravi"],"a":1,"x":"The Shyok, Gilgit and Hunza join the Indus in its upper, mountain course. The Beas, Satluj and Ravi join through the Panjnad.","e":"The Indus rises near Lake Mansarovar in Tibet.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S2"],"k":"The Beas, Satluj and Ravi are also part of the Indus system; DSSSB's key marks the Shyok–Gilgit–Hunza set."},{"id":"q0383","q":"Which of the following is made up of fresh newer alluvium that is deposited by the rivers flowing down from the Himalayas to the plain?","o":["Khadar land","Terai land","Bangar land","Bhabar land"],"a":0,"x":"Khadar is the fresh, newer alluvium that rivers from the Himalayas deposit on their floodplains every year; it is very fertile.","e":"Bhangar is older alluvium on higher ground; Bhabar is a pebble belt at the foothills; Terai is the swampy belt south of Bhabar.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0384","q":"In which state are the Karbi Anglong hills located?","o":["Karnataka","Odisha","Maharashtra","Assam"],"a":3,"x":"The Karbi Anglong hills are in central Assam, part of the Meghalaya (Shillong) plateau extension.","e":"Karbi Anglong is a Sixth Schedule autonomous district.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0394","q":"What kind of planetary winds prevail over India during the cold weather season?","o":["Northeast trade winds","The polar easterlies","Southwest trade winds","The westerlies"],"a":0,"x":"In the cold weather season, north-east trade winds blow over India from land to sea, so most of the country stays dry.","e":"They pick up moisture over the Bay of Bengal and bring winter rain to Tamil Nadu.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0397","q":"Which of the following Indian rivers has its source in the Amarkantak plateau and flows into the Arabian Sea?","o":["Krishna","Godavari","Kaveri","Narmada"],"a":3,"x":"The Narmada rises at Amarkantak (Madhya Pradesh) and flows west through a rift valley into the Arabian Sea; the Son also rises at Amarkantak but flows east.","e":"The Tapi is the other big west-flowing rift-valley river.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0424","q":"The famous hill stations 'Dharamshala' and 'Mussoorie' are located in which range of the Himalayas?","o":["Shiwalik Range","Lesser Himalaya Range","Trans Himalaya Range","Greater Himalaya Range"],"a":1,"x":"Dharamshala, Mussoorie, Shimla, Nainital and Darjeeling are in the Lesser (Middle) Himalaya, the Himachal range.","e":"Shiwaliks are the outermost range; the Greater Himalaya (Himadri) holds the highest peaks.","src":["ASO 22 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0433","q":"In the physiography of India, the Palkonda range is a part of ______.","o":["Himalayan ranges","Vindhya hills","Eastern Ghats","Western Ghats"],"a":2,"x":"The Palkonda range of Andhra Pradesh is part of the Eastern Ghats, which are broken and discontinuous.","e":"Other Eastern Ghats hills: Javadi, Shevaroy, Nallamala. The highest peak is Mahendragiri (Odisha) or Arma Konda (Andhra Pradesh).","src":["ASO 22 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0434","q":"Where do western disturbances originate?","o":["Bay of Bengal","Mediterranean Sea","Indian Ocean","Arabian Sea"],"a":1,"x":"Western disturbances are low-pressure systems that start over the Mediterranean Sea and move east on the westerly jet stream to bring winter rain and snow to north India.","e":"The rain helps the wheat crop.","src":["ASO 22 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0438","q":"Identify the place of origin of the Betwa river.","o":["Himalayas","Aravalli range","Vindhya range","Western Ghats"],"a":2,"x":"The Betwa rises in the Vindhya range near Bhopal and joins the Yamuna at Hamirpur (Uttar Pradesh).","e":"Other Yamuna tributaries from the south: Chambal, Sind, Ken.","src":["ASO 22 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0462","q":"Which of the following statements is FALSE about the Thar Desert?","o":["The eastern part of the Thar Desert is known as the Rajasthan Bagar.","The Luni flows towards the north-east to the Rann of Kachchh.","The tract north of the Luni is known as thali or sandy plain.","The Thar Desert is drained by a number of short seasonal streams originating from the Aravali."],"a":1,"x":"The false statement is that the Luni flows north-east. The Luni rises in the Aravallis near Ajmer and flows south-west into the Rann of Kachchh.","e":"The Luni is salty in its lower course.","src":["ASO 27 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0463","q":"Which mountain range forms the northern border of India?","o":["Karakoram and Himalayas","Vindhya Range","Satpura Range","Aravali Range"],"a":0,"x":"The Himalayas and, in Ladakh, the Karakoram form India's northern border.","e":"K2 (Godwin Austen), in the Karakoram, is the highest peak claimed by India.","src":["ASO 27 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0464","q":"The main tributaries of the Indus River flowing in Indian Territory include all of the following EXCEPT ________.","o":["Sutlej","Tapti","Beas","Ravi"],"a":1,"x":"The Tapi (Tapti) is a west-flowing peninsular river into the Arabian Sea. The Sutlej, Beas and Ravi are Indus tributaries.","e":"The five rivers of Punjab: Jhelum, Chenab, Ravi, Beas, Sutlej.","src":["ASO 27 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0470","q":"Chambal, Sind, Betwa and Ken rivers flow through which of the following plateaus in India?","o":["Chota Nagpur Plateau","Deccan Plateau","Malwa Plateau","Meghalaya Plateau"],"a":2,"x":"The Chambal, Sind, Betwa and Ken flow north-east across the Malwa Plateau to join the Yamuna.","e":"The Malwa Plateau is made of lava (black soil) in the west.","src":["ASO 27 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q1594","q":"In 1955, with which of the following countries did India sign an agreement to construct Koshi Barrage?","o":["Sri Lanka","Nepal","China","Bangladesh"],"a":1,"x":"India and Nepal signed the Kosi Agreement in the mid-1950s for the Kosi barrage and embankments; the barrage stands at Bhimnagar on the Nepal side.","e":"The Kosi is called the 'Sorrow of Bihar'.","src":["Patwari 20 Aug 2022 S2"],"k":"The Kosi Agreement is usually dated 25 April 1954; Nepal is correct either way."},{"id":"q1614","q":"Which of the following rivers, along with their tributaries, form the Northern Plains?","o":["Indus, Yamuna and Brahmaputra","Indus, Ganga and Brahmaputra","Narmada, Ganga and Brahmaputra","Indus, Ganga and Yamuna"],"a":1,"x":"The Northern Plains were formed by three river systems and their tributaries: the Indus, Ganga and Brahmaputra.","e":"They are divided into the Punjab plains, Ganga plains and Brahmaputra plains.","src":["Patwari 17 Sep 2022 S3"]},{"id":"q1616","q":"Which Plateau generally refers to the volcanic upland north of the Vindhya Range, and Aravalli lies in the north-west of this plateau?","o":["Malwa","Bundelkhand","Baghelkhand","Deccan"],"a":0,"x":"The Malwa Plateau is the lava upland north of the Vindhyas, with the Aravallis to its north-west; it is drained by the Chambal and Betwa.","e":"The Bundelkhand and Baghelkhand uplands lie to its east.","src":["Patwari 18 Sep 2022 S1"]},{"id":"q0498","q":"Which among the following rivers was earlier known as Sadanira?","o":["Gandak","Kosi","Chambal","Betwa"],"a":0,"x":"The Gandak was known as the Sadanira ('ever-flowing') in ancient texts; it formed the border between Kosala and Videha.","e":"It rises in Nepal and joins the Ganga near Patna.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0535","q":"Nepal shares it's borders with which of the following Indian states?","o":["West Bengal","Mizoram","Rajasthan","Punjab"],"a":0,"x":"Nepal borders five Indian states: Uttarakhand, Uttar Pradesh, Bihar, West Bengal and Sikkim.","e":"Uttar Pradesh has the longest border with Nepal.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0537","q":"Which among the following rivers is also known as 'Gorakhpur's Sorrow'?","o":["West Jhelum","West Gandak","West Rapti","West Kosi"],"a":2,"x":"The West Rapti is called 'Gorakhpur's Sorrow' because of its floods in eastern Uttar Pradesh.","e":"The Kosi is 'Bihar's Sorrow'; the Damodar was 'Bengal's Sorrow'.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0538","q":"Eravimala, which is the highest peak located in the Anamalai Hills in the Western Ghats, is located in which of the following states?","o":["Kerala","Karnataka","Tamil Nadu","Maharashtra"],"a":0,"x":"Anamudi (2,695 m) in Kerala's Eravikulam National Park in the Anamalai hills is the highest peak of the Western Ghats and of peninsular India.","e":"Doddabetta (2,637 m) in the Nilgiris is the second highest.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0576","q":"Which among the following rivers is not a tributary of river Kaveri?","o":["Bhavani","Sabari","Noyyal","Kabini"],"a":1,"x":"The Sabari is a tributary of the Godavari. The Bhavani, Noyyal and Kabini are Kaveri tributaries.","e":"Other Kaveri tributaries: Hemavati, Shimsha, Arkavati, Amaravati.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0578","q":"Which among the following mountain peaks is not located on the Western Ghats?","o":["Kalsubai","Mullayanagiri","Anamudi","Dhupgarh"],"a":3,"x":"Dhupgarh (1,350 m) is in the Satpura range at Pachmarhi, Madhya Pradesh, not the Western Ghats. Kalsubai, Mullayanagiri and Anamudi are all in the Western Ghats.","e":"Kalsubai is Maharashtra's highest peak; Mullayanagiri is Karnataka's.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0615","q":"India does not share its borders with which of the following countries?","o":["China","Pakistan","Bhutan","Thailand"],"a":3,"x":"India shares land borders with Pakistan, Afghanistan, China, Nepal, Bhutan, Bangladesh and Myanmar. Thailand is a maritime neighbour only.","e":"Longest land border: with Bangladesh (4,096 km).","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0617","q":"Which among the following is not a tributary of Damodar river?","o":["Bokaro","Ghari","Konar","Bhavani"],"a":3,"x":"The Bhavani is a Kaveri tributary in Tamil Nadu. The Bokaro, Konar and Barakar are tributaries of the Damodar (Jharkhand–West Bengal).","e":"The Damodar Valley Corporation (1948) was India's first multipurpose river valley project.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0618","q":"Which among the following separates the Tamil Nadu state of India and the Mannar district of Sri Lanka?","o":["Durand Line","Palk Strait","Radcliffe Line","MacMohan Line"],"a":1,"x":"The Palk Strait separates Tamil Nadu from the Mannar district of Sri Lanka.","e":"Adam's Bridge (Ram Setu) lies between Palk Bay and the Gulf of Mannar.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0656","q":"Which among the following is the longest river in the state of Kerala?","o":["Pamba","Kallada","Periyar","Chaliyar"],"a":2,"x":"The Periyar (about 244 km) is Kerala's longest river; it feeds the Idukki dam and flows into the Arabian Sea near Kochi.","e":"The Bharathappuzha is the second longest.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0657","q":"Mahendragiri is the second highest peak of Odisha located in which of the following ranges?","o":["Eastern Ghats","Western Ghats","Aravalli Range","Vindhya Range"],"a":0,"x":"Mahendragiri in Gajapati district is in the Eastern Ghats; Odisha's highest peak is Deomali.","e":"The Eastern Ghats are broken up by rivers such as the Mahanadi, Godavari and Krishna.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0695","q":"Which among the following lines separates India and Pakistan?","o":["Durand Line","MacMohan Line","Hindenburg Line","Radcliffe Line"],"a":3,"x":"The Radcliffe Line, drawn by Sir Cyril Radcliffe in 1947, separates India and Pakistan (and India and Bangladesh).","e":"Durand Line: Afghanistan–Pakistan. McMahon Line: India–China, in the east.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0696","q":"Indravati river is the tributary of which of the following rivers?","o":["Krishna","Godavari","Narmada","Mahanadi"],"a":1,"x":"The Indravati is a tributary of the Godavari, rising in Odisha's Kalahandi and flowing through Chhattisgarh's Bastar.","e":"Chitrakote Falls on the Indravati is called the 'Niagara of India'.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0698","q":"Which among the following soils is formed as a result of high leaching?","o":["Laterite","Black","Red","Arid"],"a":0,"x":"Laterite soil forms in hot, heavy-rain areas where intense leaching washes away silica and lime, leaving iron and aluminium oxides.","e":"Found in the Western Ghats, Odisha and Assam; good for tea, coffee and cashew.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0735","q":"Amongst the following, which river was earlier known as the \"Sorrow of Bengal\"?","o":["Kosi","Damodar","Subarnarekha","Teesta"],"a":1,"x":"The Damodar was called the 'Sorrow of Bengal' because of its floods, until the Damodar Valley Corporation dams tamed it.","e":"Kosi: 'Sorrow of Bihar'. Brahmaputra: 'Sorrow of Assam'.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0736","q":"Which one of the following is not a Himalayan pass?","o":["Palghat pass","Banihal pass","Kora La","Zoji La"],"a":0,"x":"The Palghat (Palakkad) Gap is in the Western Ghats between the Nilgiri and Anamalai hills, linking Kerala and Tamil Nadu. Banihal, Kora La and Zoji La are Himalayan passes.","e":"Other Western Ghats passes: Thal Ghat, Bhor Ghat.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0738","q":"As per the Indus Waters Treaty between India and Pakistan, India has been allowed the usage of which of the following rivers?","o":["Indus, Jhelum, Chenab","Ravi, Indus, Chenab","Chenab, Jhelum, Ravi","Beas, Ravi, Sutlej"],"a":3,"x":"Under the Indus Waters Treaty (1960, brokered by the World Bank), India got the eastern rivers — Ravi, Beas and Sutlej — and Pakistan the western rivers — Indus, Jhelum and Chenab.","e":"India put the treaty 'in abeyance' in April 2025.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1754","q":"Which of the following peak is not located in India?","o":["Kanchenjunga","Nanga Parbat","Annapurna","Kamet"],"a":2,"x":"Annapurna (8,091 m) is in Nepal. Kanchenjunga (Sikkim) and Kamet (Uttarakhand) are in India, and India counts Nanga Parbat as part of Ladakh/PoK.","e":"India's highest peak: Kanchenjunga.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1797","q":"What is the approximate height of peak Nanga Parbat?","o":["8172 meters","8598 meters","8848 meters","8126 meters"],"a":3,"x":"Nanga Parbat is about 8,126 m high, the western anchor of the Himalayas and the ninth-highest mountain in the world.","e":"Called the 'Killer Mountain'.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1950","q":"India’s total area accounts for about ______ percent of the total geographical area of the world.","o":["1.8","2.1","2.4","2.7"],"a":2,"x":"India covers about 3.28 million sq km, roughly 2.4% of the world's land area, and is the seventh-largest country.","e":"2.4% of land but about 17% of people.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1951","q":"Which of the following pair of river – tributary is/are correct? I. Godavari – Purna II. Krishna – Bhima","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"The Purna is a tributary of the Godavari and the Bhima a tributary of the Krishna, so both pairs are correct.","e":"Other Godavari tributaries: Pranhita, Indravati, Manjra; Krishna: Tungabhadra, Koyna.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1989","q":"India has a land boundary of about ______ km.","o":["13700","15200","11300","16800"],"a":1,"x":"India's land boundary is about 15,200 km long; its coastline (with islands) is about 7,516 km.","e":"Longest border: Bangladesh (about 4,096 km).","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1990","q":"The northern plains of India are about ______ km long.","o":["3100","1200","2400","2000"],"a":2,"x":"The Northern Plains stretch about 2,400 km from east to west and are 240–320 km wide, formed by the Indus, Ganga and Brahmaputra systems.","e":"Bhabar → Terai → alluvial plains.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1992","q":"Which of the following is/are tributary of Indus? I. Zaskar II. Nubra III. Shyok","o":["I and II","I, II and III","Only II","II and III"],"a":1,"x":"The Zaskar, Nubra and Shyok (along with Hunza) are tributaries of the Indus in the Ladakh–Karakoram region.","e":"Punjab tributaries: Jhelum, Chenab, Ravi, Beas, Satluj.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2035","q":"The length of Ganga is over ______ km.","o":["2800","2500","3100","3300"],"a":1,"x":"The Ganga is about 2,525 km long, India's longest river; it rises as the Bhagirathi at Gangotri glacier.","e":"Bhagirathi and Alaknanda meet at Devprayag to form the Ganga.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2154","q":"Which of the following state in India is also Known as the ‘molasses basin’?","o":["Assam","Nagaland","Mizoram","Manipur"],"a":2,"x":"Mizoram is called the 'molasses basin' because it is made of soft, unconsolidated rocks (NCERT Class 9).","e":"Mizoram's hills are called the Lushai hills.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2400","q":"Which of the following river is also known as the ‘sorrow of Bihar’?","o":["Mahi","Pennar","Sabarmati","Kosi"],"a":3,"x":"The Kosi is called the 'Sorrow of Bihar' because of its floods and frequent changes of course.","e":"Damodar: 'Sorrow of Bengal'.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2401","q":"In which of the following state is the Konkan coast located?","o":["Odisha","Maharashtra","Meghalaya","Sikkim"],"a":1,"x":"The Konkan coast is the stretch of the west coast from Mumbai to Goa, mostly in Maharashtra.","e":"Gujarat → Konkan → Kannad → Malabar (north to south).","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2445","q":"Where is the highest peak of Peninsular plateau is located?","o":["Anaimalai hills","Javadi hills","Mahendragiri hills","Nallamala hills"],"a":0,"x":"Anamudi (2,695 m) in the Anaimalai hills of Kerala is the highest peak of the Peninsular plateau.","e":"Doddabetta (Nilgiris) is the second highest.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2447","q":"How many islands/islets are there in the Bay of Bengal Island groups?","o":["23","345","119","572"],"a":3,"x":"The Andaman and Nicobar group in the Bay of Bengal has about 572 islands and islets (NCERT).","e":"The Lakshadweep group in the Arabian Sea has 36 islands.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2491","q":"Why does the eastern coast has less number of ports and harbours?","o":["The eastern coast suffers from poor economic growth","Because of its emergent nature and extended continental shelf, it’s difficult to build good ports and harbours","The eastern coast is filled with deep gorges which makes it difficult to build ports and harbours","The western coast has extremely low population density so building ports and harbours will not be benefiting"],"a":1,"x":"The east coast is an emergent coast with a wide continental shelf, so it has few natural deep harbours; the west coast is submergent with deep, natural harbours.","e":"West coast: Mumbai, Kandla, Kochi natural harbours.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2529","q":"What is the name of the narrow belt ranging between 8-10 km parallel to the Shiwalik foothills at the break-up of the slope?","o":["Khadar","Bhangar","Bhabar","Tarat"],"a":2,"x":"The Bhabar is a narrow belt about 8–16 km wide along the Shiwalik foothills where rivers drop pebbles; streams disappear in it.","e":"Bhabar → Terai → Bhangar/Khadar.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2530","q":"Which of the following glacier is known as the origin of the Bramhaputra?","o":["Chemayungdung","Mapchachungo","Amarkantak","Milam"],"a":0,"x":"The Brahmaputra rises from the Chemayungdung glacier near Mansarovar lake in Tibet, where it is called the Tsangpo.","e":"Enters India in Arunachal as the Siang/Dihang.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2601","q":"Which of the following shares land boundary with India in the north-west? I. Pakistan II. Afghanistan","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"In the north-west India borders Pakistan and Afghanistan (Afghanistan through Gilgit-Baltistan, part of Indian territory).","e":"India has seven land neighbours.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2603","q":"Which of the following is/are west flowing river(s)? I. Narmada II. Tapi III. Mahanadi","o":["I and II","II and III","I and III","I, II and III"],"a":0,"x":"The Narmada and Tapi flow west into the Arabian Sea through rift valleys; the Mahanadi flows east into the Bay of Bengal.","e":"West-flowing rivers form estuaries, not deltas.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2671","q":"Which river forms the Dhuandhar falls?","o":["Narmada","Godavari","Mahanadi","Tapi"],"a":0,"x":"The Narmada forms the Dhuandhar Falls at Bhedaghat, near Jabalpur, Madhya Pradesh.","e":"Bhedaghat also has the Marble Rocks gorge.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2672","q":"Time along the standard meridian of India passing through ______ is taken as the standard time for the whole country.","o":["Kanpur","Mirzapur","Allahbad","Lucknow"],"a":1,"x":"Indian Standard Time is the time along 82°30'E, which passes near Mirzapur in Uttar Pradesh.","e":"IST = GMT + 5:30.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2871","q":"The width of Himalayas in Kashmir is about ______ km.","o":["500","400","300","600"],"a":1,"x":"The Himalayas are about 400 km wide in Kashmir and about 150 km wide in Arunachal Pradesh (NCERT Class 9).","e":"","src":["Junior Clerk 17 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2872","q":"The outer-most range of the Himalayas is called the ______.","o":["Vindhyachal","Shiwaliks","Outer range","Himadri"],"a":1,"x":"The Shiwaliks are the outermost range of the Himalayas, with low hills of 900–1,100 m.","e":"Himadri (inner) → Himachal (middle) → Shiwalik (outer).","src":["Investigator 26 Jul 2021","Junior Clerk 17 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2873","q":"Which of the following are tributaries of Krishna? I. Musi II. Bhima III. Koyna","o":["I and II","II and III","I and III","I, II and III"],"a":3,"x":"The Musi, Bhima and Koyna are all tributaries of the Krishna; Hyderabad stands on the Musi.","e":"","src":["Junior Clerk 17 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q6205","q":"What is the Northernmost range of Himalayas is called?","o":["Middle Himalaya","Shivalik","Great Himalaya","Naga"],"a":2,"x":"The Great Himalaya (Himadri) is the northernmost range, then the Middle Himalaya (Himachal) and the Shiwaliks.","e":"","src":["Junior Clerk 9 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q1291","q":"Which of the following rivers has the longest length?","o":["Godavari","Narmada","Krishna","Kaveri"],"a":0,"x":"Of these, the Godavari is the longest (about 1,465 km), followed by the Krishna (about 1,400 km), the Narmada (about 1,312 km) and the Kaveri (about 800 km).","e":"The Ganga (about 2,525 km) is India's longest river overall.","src":["Stenographer 19 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1306","q":"Which of the following is also known as the winter monsoon?","o":["South-west monsoon","North-west monsoon","South-east monsoon","North-east monsoon"],"a":3,"x":"The north-east monsoon (October–December) is called the winter or retreating monsoon; it brings rain to the Coromandel coast of Tamil Nadu.","e":"Tamil Nadu gets most of its rain in winter.","src":["Stenographer 19 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1329","q":"Kalsubai is the highest peak of which of the following states?","o":["Tamil Nadu","Maharashtra","Odisha","West Bengal"],"a":1,"x":"Kalsubai (1,646 m) in Ahmednagar district is the highest peak of Maharashtra, in the Sahyadri range.","e":"Karnataka's highest: Mullayanagiri. Kerala's: Anamudi.","src":["Stenographer 23 Apr 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1335","q":"Which among the following soils is also known as 'Usara soils'?","o":["Arid","Saline","Laterite","Alluvial"],"a":1,"x":"Saline soils are also called usar (or reh, kallar) soils: they have too much salt and are infertile, and are found in arid areas and waterlogged canal regions.","e":"Adding gypsum helps treat saline soils.","src":["Assistant Engineer (Civil) 27 Sep 2021 S3","Stenographer 23 Apr 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1341","q":"Which among the following states is also known as \"Orchid State of India\"?","o":["Tamil Nadu","Andhra Pradesh","Arunachal Pradesh","Madhya Pradesh"],"a":2,"x":"Arunachal Pradesh is called the 'Orchid State of India' for its hundreds of orchid species; the Orchid Research Centre is at Tipi.","e":"Sikkim is also famous for orchids.","src":["Stenographer 23 Apr 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5011","q":"Which of the following is situated at the end of the Pindari glacier and links Pindari valley to Milam valley?","o":["Traill’s Pass","Niti Pass","Thang La Pass","Mana Pass"],"a":0,"x":"Traill's Pass, at the end of the Pindari glacier in Uttarakhand, links the Pindari valley with the Milam valley.","e":"","src":["Stenographer 26 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6864","q":"Which of the following peaks is the highest peak of Karnataka?","o":["Phawngpui","Mullayanagiri","Doddabetta","Anamudi"],"a":1,"x":"Mullayanagiri (1,930 m) in the Chikmagalur hills is the highest peak of Karnataka.","e":"Doddabetta – Tamil Nadu; Anamudi – Kerala; Phawngpui – Mizoram.","src":["Stenographer 14 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q6958","q":"Which among the following rivers is not a tributary of Yamuna?","o":["Sindh","Betwa","Tons","Kosi"],"a":3,"x":"The Kosi is a tributary of the Ganga; the Sindh, Betwa and Tons flow into the Yamuna.","e":"","src":["Stenographer 8 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q7006","q":"Tawa is a tributary of which of the following rivers?","o":["Ganga","Narmada","Krishna","Godavari"],"a":1,"x":"The Tawa is the longest tributary of the Narmada, in Madhya Pradesh.","e":"","src":["Stenographer 14 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q4961","q":"With reference to physical features of India, the Himalayan Mountains form an arc, which covers a distance of about:","o":["1,400 km","3,400 km","2,400 km","4,400 km"],"a":2,"x":"The Himalayas form an arc about 2,400 km long.","e":"","src":["Personal Assistant 26 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q4965","q":"Which of the following rivers does NOT drain into the Bay of Bengal?","o":["Narmada","Kaveri","Krishna","Godavari"],"a":0,"x":"The Narmada flows west into the Arabian Sea; the Kaveri, Krishna and Godavari drain into the Bay of Bengal.","e":"","src":["Personal Assistant 26 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q5175","q":"In which state is Ghughua/Ghughwa fossil park located?","o":["Rajasthan","Bihar","Uttar Pradesh","Madhya Pradesh"],"a":3,"x":"The Ghughua fossil park, with plant fossils millions of years old, is in Dindori district, Madhya Pradesh.","e":"","src":["Personal Assistant 27 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q5397","q":"Which of the following states is known for the largest Salt Lake in the India?","o":["Gujarat","Uttar Pradesh","Madhya Pradesh","Rajasthan"],"a":3,"x":"Sambhar Lake in Rajasthan is India's largest inland salt lake.","e":"","src":["Personal Assistant 29 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q5437","q":"Which of the following is the India’s highest-commercial airport?","o":["Calicut International Airport","Veer Savarkar International Airport","Imphal Airport","Kushok Bakula Rimpochee Airport"],"a":3,"x":"Kushok Bakula Rimpochee Airport at Leh is India's highest commercial airport (about 3,256 m).","e":"","src":["Personal Assistant 29 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q10070","q":"Which of the following factors determines the climate of India? I. Distance from sea II. Distribution of land and water","o":["Only II","Only I","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Distance from the sea and the distribution of land and water both influence India's climate.","e":"","src":["Assistant Superintendent 20 Jul 2024 S1"]},{"id":"q10071","q":"Which of the following is a left bank tributary of Yamuna River?","o":["Chambal","Ken","Hindan","Betwa"],"a":2,"x":"The Hindan is a left-bank tributary of the Yamuna; the Chambal, Betwa and Ken join from the right.","e":"","src":["Assistant Superintendent 20 Jul 2024 S1"]},{"id":"q10106","q":"Which of the following hills are correctly arranged from west to east?","o":["Garo Hills, Khasi Hills and Jaintia Hills","Jaintia Hills, Garo Hills and Khasi Hills","Khasi Hills, Jaintia Hills, and Garo Hills","Garo Hills, Jaintia Hills and Khasi Hills"],"a":0,"x":"From west to east, Meghalaya's hills are the Garo, Khasi and Jaintia Hills.","e":"","src":["Assistant Superintendent 20 Jul 2024 S2"]},{"id":"q10109","q":"Mahananda is a tributary of which river?","o":["Ghaghara","Ganga","Kosi","Yamuna"],"a":1,"x":"The Mahananda is a tributary of the Ganga, joining it in West Bengal.","e":"","src":["Assistant Superintendent 20 Jul 2024 S2"]},{"id":"q10135","q":"What is the width of the Himalayas in Kashmir?","o":["150 km","600 km","400 km","700 km"],"a":2,"x":"The Himalayas are about 400 km wide in Kashmir and 150 km wide in Arunachal Pradesh.","e":"","src":["Assistant Superintendent 20 Jul 2024 S3"]},{"id":"q10138","q":"Agra and Darjeeling are located on the same latitude, but temperature of January in Agra is 16°C whereas it is only 4°C in Darjeeling. Their climate difference is due to which of the following?","o":["Effect of Western disturbance","Distance from sea","Difference in air pressure","Difference in their altitude"],"a":3,"x":"Darjeeling is much colder than Agra in January because it is at a far higher altitude.","e":"","src":["Assistant Superintendent 20 Jul 2024 S3"]},{"id":"q10146","q":"Which of the following states is NOT included in the Ganga plain?","o":["Uttar Pradesh","Haryana","Bihar","Assam"],"a":3,"x":"The Ganga plain covers Haryana, Uttar Pradesh, Bihar and West Bengal; Assam lies in the Brahmaputra plain.","e":"","src":["Assistant Superintendent 20 Jul 2024 S3"]},{"id":"q9498","q":"The Satluj originates in the ‘______’ near Mansarovar.","o":["Verinag","Rakshas tal","Chemayungdung","Bokhar Chu"],"a":1,"x":"The Satluj rises from Rakshastal near Lake Mansarovar in Tibet.","e":"","src":["Assistant Superintendent 14 Jul 2024 S1"]},{"id":"q9500","q":"Which of the following regions is NOT a high rainfall area of India?","o":["Deccan Plateau","Western Ghats","Hills of Meghalaya","Sub-Himalayan areas in the northeast"],"a":0,"x":"The Deccan Plateau is a low-rainfall (rain-shadow) area; the Western Ghats, Meghalaya hills and north-east sub-Himalaya get heavy rain.","e":"","src":["Assistant Superintendent 14 Jul 2024 S1"]},{"id":"q9512","q":"Which of the following states experiences the onset of the southwest monsoon around 1st June?","o":["Tamil Nadu","Gujarat","Kerala","Punjab"],"a":2,"x":"The south-west monsoon reaches Kerala around 1 June.","e":"","src":["Assistant Superintendent 14 Jul 2024 S1"]},{"id":"q8877","q":"Which of the following was the name given to Laccadive, Minicoy and Amindivi Islands in 1973?","o":["Divar Island","Andaman and Nicobar Islands","Pamban Island","Lakshadweep Island"],"a":3,"x":"The Laccadive, Minicoy and Amindivi Islands were renamed Lakshadweep in 1973.","e":"","src":["MTS 2 Mar 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q8889","q":"Which of the following is a tributary of the Damodar River that flows through Jharkhand and West Bengal?","o":["Subarnarekha","Barakar","Mahananda","Ajay"],"a":1,"x":"The Barakar is the main tributary of the Damodar, flowing through Jharkhand and West Bengal.","e":"","src":["MTS 2 Mar 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q8919","q":"Which of the following islands is part of the Andaman and Nicobar group?","o":["Barren Island","Majuli","Diu","Minicoy"],"a":0,"x":"Barren Island, India's only active volcano, is in the Andaman and Nicobar Islands.","e":"","src":["MTS 3 Mar 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q8966","q":"Which city is connected to Delhi, Mumbai, and Kolkata through the Golden Quadrilateral network?","o":["Chennai","Hyderabad","Indore","Nagpur"],"a":0,"x":"The Golden Quadrilateral highway links Delhi, Mumbai, Chennai and Kolkata.","e":"","src":["MTS 3 Mar 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q8967","q":"Which river flows through a rift valley between the Satpura and Vindhya ranges?","o":["Narmada River","Mahanadi River","Godavari River","Krishna River"],"a":0,"x":"The Narmada flows west through a rift valley between the Vindhya and Satpura ranges.","e":"","src":["MTS 3 Mar 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q9010","q":"What is another name for the Northern Plains?","o":["Indo-Gangetic Plains","Thar Desert","Peninsular Plateau","Deccan Plateau"],"a":0,"x":"The Northern Plains are also called the Indo-Gangetic(-Brahmaputra) Plains.","e":"","src":["MTS 3 Mar 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q9012","q":"The Purvanchal Hills, a part of the Himalayas, are located mainly in which of the following states?","o":["Arunachal Pradesh","Uttarakhand","Jammu & Kashmir","Himachal Pradesh"],"a":0,"x":"The Purvanchal hills lie along India's eastern border: Arunachal Pradesh, Nagaland, Manipur and Mizoram.","e":"","src":["MTS 3 Mar 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q9053","q":"The Kaveri River basin lies majorly within which of the following states?","o":["Madhya Pradesh and Odisha","Andhra Pradesh and Kerala","Maharashtra and Telangana","Tamil Nadu and Karnataka"],"a":3,"x":"Most of the Kaveri basin lies in Karnataka and Tamil Nadu (with a small part in Kerala).","e":"","src":["MTS 5 Mar 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q9076","q":"Which of the following coastal plains lies between the Western Ghats and the Arabian Sea?","o":["Konkan Coast","Coromandel Coast","Malwa Plateau","Northern Circars"],"a":0,"x":"The Konkan coast lies between the Western Ghats and the Arabian Sea (Maharashtra and Goa).","e":"","src":["MTS 6 Mar 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q9083","q":"The highest peak in South India, Anamudi, is located in which of the following states?","o":["Karnataka","Andhra Pradesh","Kerala","Tamil Nadu"],"a":2,"x":"Anamudi (2,695 m) in Kerala's Anaimalai hills is the highest peak in South India.","e":"","src":["MTS 6 Mar 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q9123","q":"The Tawang region, known for high-altitude passes and glacial lakes, is located in which of the following states?","o":["Manipur","Sikkim","Nagaland","Arunachal Pradesh"],"a":3,"x":"Tawang is in Arunachal Pradesh.","e":"","src":["MTS 6 Mar 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q9148","q":"What type of geological origin characterizes the Lakshadweep Islands?","o":["Fluvial","Deltaic","Coral","Volcanic"],"a":2,"x":"The Lakshadweep islands are of coral origin; many Andaman and Nicobar islands are volcanic or mountain peaks.","e":"","src":["MTS 6 Mar 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q9156","q":"Which among the following zones is known for swampy conditions and re-emergent rivers that lack definite channels?","o":["Tarai","Khadar","Bhangar","Bhabar"],"a":0,"x":"The Tarai, south of the Bhabar, is a swampy belt where streams re-emerge without definite channels.","e":"","src":["MTS 6 Mar 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q9194","q":"Which Indian island group contains both volcanic and non-volcanic islands?","o":["Minicoy","Maldives","Lakshadweep","Andaman and Nicobar"],"a":3,"x":"The Andaman and Nicobar Islands include volcanic islands (such as Barren Island) and non-volcanic ones.","e":"","src":["MTS 8 Mar 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q9195","q":"Which of the following is the longest National Highway in India?","o":["NH-48","NH-27","NH-19","NH-44"],"a":3,"x":"NH-44, from Srinagar to Kanyakumari, is India's longest national highway.","e":"","src":["MTS 8 Mar 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q9201","q":"Which of the following rivers forms an estuary on the western coast of India?","o":["Krishna","Mahanadi","Narmada","Godavari"],"a":2,"x":"The Narmada (and Tapi) form estuaries on the west coast; east-flowing rivers form deltas.","e":"","src":["MTS 8 Mar 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q9277","q":"Mount Thullier, the tallest peak in the Nicobar Islands, is found in:","o":["Car Nicobar","South Andaman","Little Nicobar","Great Nicobar"],"a":3,"x":"Mount Thullier is the highest peak of Great Nicobar.","e":"","src":["MTS 8 Mar 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q9305","q":"The Nilgiri Hills lie at the junction of which two major hill ranges?","o":["Vindhya and Aravalli","Dhauladhar and Pir Panjal","Eastern and Western Ghats","Karakoram and Zanskar"],"a":2,"x":"The Nilgiri Hills lie where the Eastern and Western Ghats meet.","e":"","src":["MTS 24 Feb 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q9342","q":"Which of the following rivers meets the Ganga near Chhapra in Bihar and is also known as Karnali in Nepal?","o":["Ghaghara","Gandak","Saryu","Ken"],"a":0,"x":"The Ghaghara, called Karnali in Nepal, joins the Ganga near Chhapra in Bihar.","e":"","src":["MTS 25 Feb 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q9344","q":"How is the Peninsular Plateau best described in shape?","o":["Rectangular","Triangular","Linear","Circular"],"a":1,"x":"The Peninsular Plateau is roughly triangular in shape.","e":"","src":["MTS 25 Feb 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q9419","q":"The western part of the Great Indian Plains, which is arid and semi-arid, is known as:","o":["Terai","Bhabar","Rajasthan Plain","Khadar"],"a":2,"x":"The arid and semi-arid western part of the Northern Plains is the Rajasthan Plain.","e":"","src":["MTS 26 Feb 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q9432","q":"The Satpura Range lies between which two rivers?","o":["Ganga and Yamuna","Narmada and Tapti","Brahmaputra and Subansiri","Godavari and Krishna"],"a":1,"x":"The Satpura range lies between the Narmada and the Tapi rivers.","e":"","src":["MTS 26 Feb 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q9465","q":"Which island group of India experiences convectional rainfall and supports dense equatorial vegetation?","o":["Agatti","Lakshadweep","Ritchie's Archipelago","Andaman and Nicobar"],"a":3,"x":"The Andaman and Nicobar Islands get convectional rainfall and have dense equatorial forests.","e":"","src":["MTS 26 Feb 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q3870","q":"Arma Konda peak of Andhra Pradesh, is situated in which of the following mountain ranges?","o":["Eastern Ghats","Western Ghats","Aravalli range","Karakoram range"],"a":0,"x":"Arma Konda (1,680 m) in Andhra Pradesh is the highest peak of the Eastern Ghats; some sources give Jindhagada instead.","e":"","src":["Store Keeper 22 Apr 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q3871","q":"The Konar river is a tributary of which of the following rivers?","o":["Krishna","Chenab","Damodar","Ravi"],"a":2,"x":"The Konar is a tributary of the Damodar in Jharkhand; the Konar dam is part of the Damodar Valley Corporation.","e":"","src":["Store Keeper 22 Apr 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q3600","q":"Where is the Lakshadweep located?","o":["Bay of Bengal","Arabian sea","Pacific ocean","Atlantic ocean"],"a":1,"x":"The Lakshadweep islands lie in the Arabian Sea off Kerala; Andaman and Nicobar lie in the Bay of Bengal.","e":"","src":["Fire Operator 20 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q3601","q":"Which of the following sub-divisions of the Himalayas comprises the Karakoram, Ladakh, Zaskar and Pir Panjal ranges?","o":["Darjeeling and Sikkim Himalayas","Arunachal Himalayas","Himachal and Uttarakhand Himalayas","Kashmir or Northwestern Himalayas"],"a":3,"x":"The Kashmir (North-western) Himalayas include the Karakoram, Ladakh, Zaskar and Pir Panjal ranges.","e":"","src":["Fire Operator 20 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q3602","q":"The technique of shifting or slash and burn cultivation practiced by the ethnic tribal communities of the Arunachal Himalayas, is known as _______ cultivation.","o":["Jhumming","Terrace","Contour","Shelter Belts"],"a":0,"x":"Slash-and-burn shifting cultivation practised in the Arunachal Himalayas is called 'jhumming'.","e":"","src":["Fire Operator 20 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q3603","q":"The higher reaches of the Darjeeling and Sikkim Himalayas are inhabited by which of the following tribes?","o":["Lepcha","Zulu","Himba","Bushman"],"a":0,"x":"The Lepchas live in the higher reaches of the Darjeeling and Sikkim Himalayas; the Zulu, Himba and Bushmen are African groups.","e":"","src":["Fire Operator 20 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q3604","q":"Before entering into Bangladesh, in which direction does the Bramhaputra river turn almost 90 degree at Dhubri?","o":["North","South","East","West"],"a":1,"x":"At Dhubri in Assam the Brahmaputra turns sharply south before entering Bangladesh.","e":"","src":["Fire Operator 20 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q3641","q":"Where is the Great Indian Desert located?","o":["Southeast of the Aravalli hills","Northwest of the Aravalli hills","South of Malwa Plateau","East of the Malwa Plateau"],"a":1,"x":"The Great Indian (Thar) Desert lies north-west of the Aravalli hills.","e":"","src":["Fire Operator 20 Apr 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q3642","q":"Which of the following water body separates Andaman and Nicobar island?","o":["5 Degree Channel","10 degree channel","15 degree channel","20 degree channel"],"a":1,"x":"The Ten Degree Channel separates the Andaman Islands from the Nicobar Islands.","e":"The Eight Degree Channel separates Minicoy from the Maldives.","src":["Fire Operator 20 Apr 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q3677","q":"The Eastern Hills and Mountain system has their general alignment in which direction?","o":["East to west","West to East","North to south","South to North"],"a":2,"x":"The Eastern hills and mountains (Purvanchal) run broadly north to south along India's eastern border.","e":"","src":["Fire Operator 20 Apr 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q3678","q":"Which one of the following river is the largest tributary of the Indus?","o":["Beas","Ravi","Satluj","Chenab"],"a":3,"x":"The Chenab is the largest tributary of the Indus.","e":"Punjab rivers: Jhelum, Chenab, Ravi, Beas, Satluj.","src":["Fire Operator 20 Apr 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q3680","q":"Which of the following countires are separated by India through Gulf of Mannar?","o":["Maldives","Sri Lanka","Myanmar","Nepal"],"a":1,"x":"The Gulf of Mannar and Palk Strait separate India from Sri Lanka.","e":"Adam's Bridge (Ram Setu) lies between them.","src":["Fire Operator 20 Apr 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q3815","q":"Odisha is located on the ______ seacoast of India.","o":["Northern","Southern","Eastern","Western"],"a":2,"x":"Odisha lies on India's eastern coast, along the Bay of Bengal.","e":"","src":["Fire Operator 22 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2713","q":"Which among the following lakes is the largest inland salt lake in India?","o":["Sambhar","Kolleru","Pushkar","Pulicat"],"a":0,"x":"Sambhar Lake in Rajasthan is India's largest inland saltwater lake.","e":"Chilika (Odisha) is the largest coastal lagoon.","src":["Ahlmad 16 Oct 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2714","q":"Which among the following mountain ranges is famous for tourist spots like Amarkantak and Omkareshwar temples?","o":["Eastern Ghats","Satpura and Vindhyachal Range","Western Ghats","Karakoram and Pir Panjal Range"],"a":1,"x":"Amarkantak (source of the Narmada) lies in the Maikal hills where the Vindhya and Satpura ranges meet, and Omkareshwar sits on the Narmada between them.","e":"","src":["Ahlmad 16 Oct 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2715","q":"Which among the following is not a tributary of Chambal river?","o":["Shipra","Parbati","Banas","Tawa"],"a":3,"x":"The Tawa is a tributary of the Narmada; the Shipra, Parbati and Banas are tributaries of the Chambal.","e":"","src":["Ahlmad 16 Oct 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2716","q":"Dhupgarh Peak is the highest peak of which of the following mountain ranges?","o":["Eastern Ghats","Satpura Range","Western Ghats","Pir Panjal Range"],"a":1,"x":"Dhupgarh (1,350 m), in the Pachmarhi hills of Madhya Pradesh, is the highest peak of the Satpura Range.","e":"","src":["Ahlmad 16 Oct 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2758","q":"The Kunchikal falls is located in which of the following states?","o":["Kerala","Tamil Nadu","Meghalaya","Karnataka"],"a":3,"x":"Kunchikal Falls, one of India's highest waterfalls, is in Shimoga district, Karnataka.","e":"","src":["Ahlmad 16 Oct 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2759","q":"Which among the following rivers do not flow into Bay of Bengal?","o":["Mahanadi","Indus","Krishna","Godavari"],"a":1,"x":"The Indus flows into the Arabian Sea; the Mahanadi, Krishna and Godavari flow into the Bay of Bengal.","e":"","src":["Ahlmad 16 Oct 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2760","q":"Shipki La is the mountain range located in which of the following states?","o":["Uttarakhand","Rajasthan","Himachal Pradesh","Sikkim"],"a":2,"x":"Shipki La is a mountain pass (not a range) in Kinnaur, Himachal Pradesh, where the Satluj enters India from Tibet.","e":"","src":["Ahlmad 16 Oct 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2829","q":"Vetravati is an alternate name of which of the following rivers?","o":["Beas","Chenab","Betwa","Ravi"],"a":2,"x":"Vetravati is the old name of the Betwa, a tributary of the Yamuna flowing through Madhya Pradesh and Uttar Pradesh.","e":"","src":["Ahlmad 16 Oct 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2830","q":"Rohtang Pass is a high mountain pass on the eastern end of the ____________ Range.","o":["Zaskar","Aravalli","Pir Panjal","Western Ghats"],"a":2,"x":"Rohtang Pass (about 3,980 m) is on the eastern Pir Panjal range, linking Manali (Kullu) with Lahaul-Spiti; the Atal Tunnel runs under it.","e":"","src":["Ahlmad 16 Oct 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2979","q":"What is the total length of the coastline of India including the Andaman islands and the Lakshadweep islands?","o":["7516.6 km","7208.7 km","7345.8 km","7524.6 km"],"a":0,"x":"India's coastline, including Andaman and Nicobar and Lakshadweep, is about 7,516.6 km; the mainland coast is about 6,100 km.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 17 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2981","q":"Which of the following rivers is smallest?","o":["Godavari","Narmada","Indus","Kaveri"],"a":3,"x":"Of these the Kaveri is the shortest (about 800 km). The Godavari is about 1,465 km, the Narmada about 1,312 km, and the Indus about 2,900 km.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 17 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2982","q":"Which of the following is known as the Summer Monsoon?","o":["North-west Monsoon","South-west monsoon","North-east monsoon","South-east monsoon"],"a":1,"x":"The south-west monsoon (June–September) is the summer monsoon, bringing most of India's rain; the north-east monsoon is the winter monsoon.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 17 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q3258","q":"What are the 'kayals' in the Malabar coast?","o":["Mountains","Backwaters","Plateau","Red soil"],"a":1,"x":"'Kayals' are the backwaters (lagoons) of the Malabar coast in Kerala, used for boating and fishing.","e":"Vembanad is the largest.","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 18 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q3260","q":"The Karewa formations are helpful for cultivation of which of the following?","o":["Rice","Wheat","Zafran","Sugarcane"],"a":2,"x":"The Karewas of Kashmir are thick lake-deposited (glacial clay) formations known for growing zafran (saffron).","e":"Pampore is the saffron town.","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 18 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q3916","q":"With which of the following name Brahmaputra river is known in Bangladesh?","o":["Rupnarayan","Jamuna","Ghugri","Mahananda"],"a":1,"x":"In Bangladesh the Brahmaputra is called the Jamuna; in Tibet it is the Tsang Po.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 22 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q3917","q":"All the streams disappear in which of the following belt?","o":["Bhabar","Terai","Duns","Doab"],"a":0,"x":"In the Bhabar belt along the foot of the Shiwaliks, streams disappear into the porous pebble deposits; they re-emerge in the Terai.","e":"Bhabar = streams vanish; Terai = they reappear.","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 22 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q3918","q":"Which of the following river rise from the spring near Mahabaleshwar?","o":["Narmada","Mahanadi","Krishna","Kaveri"],"a":2,"x":"The Krishna rises from a spring near Mahabaleshwar in the Western Ghats of Maharashtra.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 22 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q4295","q":"Name the highest peak in Maharashtra.","o":["Kalsubai Shikhar","Salher","Dhodap","Taramati"],"a":0,"x":"Kalsubai (1,646 m) in the Sahyadri is the highest peak in Maharashtra.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 24 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q4340","q":"Which of the following is located at Allahabad?","o":["Neer Garh Waterfall","Triveni Sangam (confluence of Ganga, Yamuna and Saraswati)","Assi Ghat","Chilla Wildlife Sanctuary"],"a":1,"x":"The Triveni Sangam at Prayagraj (Allahabad) is the confluence of the Ganga, Yamuna and the mythical Saraswati.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 24 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q4341","q":"The Tapti river does not flow through which of these following states?","o":["Madhya Pradesh","Gujarat","Maharashtra","Telangana"],"a":3,"x":"The Tapi flows through Madhya Pradesh, Maharashtra and Gujarat, not Telangana.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 24 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q4342","q":"The Dibang, Lohit and Kenula are tributaries to which of the following rivers?","o":["Brahmaputra","Godavari","Krishna","Mahanadi"],"a":0,"x":"The Dibang, Lohit and Kenula are tributaries of the Brahmaputra in Arunachal Pradesh.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 24 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q4343","q":"The Kangto peak in the Himalayas is located in which of the following states?","o":["Assam","Sikkim","Arunachal Pradesh","West Bengal"],"a":2,"x":"Kangto peak (7,060 m) is in Arunachal Pradesh, on the China border.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 24 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q4385","q":"Which of the following make the seven sister states?","o":["Arunachal Pradesh, Assam, Meghalaya, Manipur, Sikkim, Nagaland and Tripura","Arunachal Pradesh, Assam, Meghalaya, Manipur, Mizoram, Nagaland and Tripura","Andhra Pradesh, Assam, Meghalaya, Manipur, Sikkim, Nagaland and Tripura","Andhra Pradesh, Assam, Meghalaya, Manipur, Mizoram, Nagaland and West Bengal"],"a":1,"x":"The Seven Sisters are Arunachal Pradesh, Assam, Meghalaya, Manipur, Mizoram, Nagaland and Tripura; Sikkim is the 'brother'.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 24 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q4387","q":"Guru Shikhar is the highest peak of which mountain range?","o":["Satpura","Sahyadri","Vindhya","Aravalli"],"a":3,"x":"Guru Shikhar (1,722 m) on Mount Abu is the highest peak of the Aravalli range.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 24 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q4549","q":"The mountain range that encloses the Thar desert from the southeast is the:","o":["Aravalli Range","Shivalik Range","Vindhya Range","Satpura Range"],"a":0,"x":"The Aravalli range borders the Thar Desert on its south-east.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 25 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q4550","q":"The Bhavani River originates from which of the following ranges?","o":["Kudurekuha Jamly","Nallamala Hills","Cardamom Hills","Nilgiri Hills"],"a":3,"x":"The Bhavani river rises in the Nilgiri hills and joins the Kaveri at Bhavani, Tamil Nadu.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 25 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q6355","q":"India shares its land boundaries with Pakistan and Afghanistan in the ______.","o":["East","North west","North east","South east"],"a":1,"x":"India shares land borders with Pakistan and Afghanistan in the north-west.","e":"","src":["Care Taker 8 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q4419","q":"What is the height of the world's tallest rail bridge being built on the Chenab River in Jammu and Kashmir?","o":["355 Metres","359 Metres","365 Metres","367 Metres"],"a":1,"x":"The Chenab rail bridge in J&K, the world's highest railway bridge, stands 359 m above the river.","e":"","src":["Hindi Translator-cum-Assisstant 24 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0952","q":"Which of the following statements about the Eastern and Western Ghats is NOT correct?","o":["The Eastern Ghats are discontinuous, irregular and dissected by rivers","The Eastern Ghats are higher in elevation than the Western Ghats","The average elevation of the Western Ghats is 900–1600 metres","The Eastern Ghats extend from the Mahanadi valley to the Nilgiris"],"a":1,"x":"The Western Ghats are higher and continuous (average 900–1,600 m); the Eastern Ghats are lower, broken by rivers and stretch from the Mahanadi valley to the Nilgiris. So 'Eastern Ghats are higher' is wrong.","e":"Anamudi (2,695 m, Western Ghats) is the highest peak of South India.","src":["PRT 18 Feb 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q0983","q":"The Great Indian Plains were primarily formed by which geological process?","o":["Glacial erosion","Volcanic eruption","Sediment deposition","Tectonic uplift"],"a":2,"x":"The Great Indian (Northern) Plains were built by the deposition of alluvial sediment brought by the Indus, Ganga and Brahmaputra systems into a trough (foredeep) south of the Himalayas.","e":"The plains cover about 7 lakh sq km.","src":["PRT 19 Feb 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1015","q":"Which of the following is a prominent feature of the Peninsular Plateau in India?","o":["Glacial moraines","Rift valleys","Coral islands","Fold mountains"],"a":1,"x":"Rift valleys are a feature of the Peninsular Plateau: the Narmada and Tapi flow west through rift valleys.","e":"That is why they form estuaries, not deltas.","src":["PRT 19 Feb 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1048","q":"Which of the following options correctly matches the southernmost points of India?","o":["Southernmost island point – Kanyakumari","Southernmost point of Union – Munnar","Southernmost mainland point – Kanyakumari","Southernmost mainland point – Minicoy Island"],"a":2,"x":"Kanyakumari (Cape Comorin) is the southernmost point of mainland India; Indira Point on Great Nicobar is the southernmost point of India's territory.","e":"Northernmost: Indira Col. Westernmost: Guhar Moti (Gujarat). Easternmost: Kibithu (Arunachal).","src":["PRT 20 Feb 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1050","q":"The deltaic plain in the eastern part of the Great Indian Plains is primarily formed by:","o":["Ganga and Brahmaputra rivers","Ghaggar and Sutlej rivers","Yamuna and Chambal rivers","Narmada and Tapi rivers"],"a":0,"x":"The eastern deltaic plain of the Great Plains is formed by the Ganga and Brahmaputra; the Sundarbans delta is the world's largest.","e":"The Sundarbans is a UNESCO site known for the Royal Bengal tiger.","src":["PRT 20 Feb 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1078","q":"Which of the following landforms are commonly found in the Great Indian Desert?","o":["Plateaus and escarpments","Deltas and oxbow lakes","Moraines and cirques","Dunes and playas"],"a":3,"x":"The Thar desert has sand dunes (barchans, seif dunes) and playas, shallow salt lakes such as Sambhar that fill in the rains.","e":"Barchan = crescent-shaped dune.","src":["PRT 23 Feb 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1088","q":"During the Southwest Monsoon season, in which direction do the monsoon winds primarily blow over the peninsular region?","o":["From northwest to southeast","From northeast to southwest","From southeast to northwest","From southwest to northeast"],"a":3,"x":"The south-west monsoon winds blow from the south-west towards the north-east over peninsular India, from the Arabian Sea and Bay of Bengal towards land.","e":"Arabian Sea branch hits the Western Ghats first.","src":["PRT 23 Feb 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1109","q":"Which river exists seasonally in the Great Indian Desert and is considered ephemeral?","o":["Chambal","Yamuna","Luni","Beas"],"a":2,"x":"The Luni is the only notable river of the Thar desert; it flows only seasonally and drains into the Rann of Kutch.","e":"Its water turns saline after Balotra.","src":["PRT 23 Feb 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q1114","q":"Which region of the Great Indian Plains is known for the presence of newer alluvium (khadar)?","o":["Rajasthan plain","Riverine tracts along the Yamuna and Ganga","Punjab plain","Ganga delta"],"a":3,"x":"Khadar is newer alluvium laid down on floodplains every year. DSSSB's key marks the Ganga delta, where fresh alluvium keeps building up.","e":"Bhangar is the older alluvium above flood level.","src":["PRT 23 Feb 2026 S3"],"k":"The floodplains along the Yamuna and Ganga are also classic khadar; the key prefers the delta."},{"id":"q1133","q":"In which Indian state does the largest part of the Great Indian Desert lie?","o":["Haryana","Punjab","Gujarat","Rajasthan"],"a":3,"x":"About 60% of the Thar (Great Indian Desert) lies in western Rajasthan; the rest extends into Gujarat, Punjab, Haryana and Pakistan.","e":"Jaisalmer, Barmer and Bikaner are desert districts.","src":["PRT 26 Mar 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1159","q":"Which place in India receives the maximum rainfall in a year, making it the highest-rainfall region in the world?","o":["Bhopal","Mawsynram","Thiruvananthapuram","Guwahati"],"a":1,"x":"Mawsynram in Meghalaya's Khasi hills gets about 11,800 mm of rain a year, the highest in the world; nearby Cherrapunji (Sohra) is second.","e":"Rain from the Bay of Bengal branch is trapped by the funnel-shaped Khasi hills.","src":["PRT 26 Mar 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q1172","q":"What is the main reason for the seasonal reversal of winds in the Indian subcontinent?","o":["Differential heating of land and sea","Gravitational force of the moon","Movement of ocean currents","Rotation of the Earth on its axis"],"a":0,"x":"Monsoon winds reverse with the seasons because land and sea heat differently: in summer low pressure over hot land draws moist sea winds in; in winter winds blow from cold land to sea.","e":"'Mausim' is Arabic for season.","src":["PRT 26 Mar 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q1181","q":"Which of the following statements correctly describes the Great Indian Desert?","o":["It is located in the northeastern part of India and receives high rainfall","It lies to the east of the Aravalli Hills and has fertile alluvial soil","It is a hot desert located in northwestern India with arid climate and sandy soil","It is formed by volcanic eruptions and is rich in black soil"],"a":2,"x":"The Great Indian (Thar) Desert is a hot desert in north-western India, west of the Aravallis, with an arid climate, sandy soil and less than 150 mm of rain in places.","e":"The Luni is the only large river in the region.","src":["PRT 31 Mar 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1185","q":"Which island feature is found exclusively in the Lakshadweep group but not in the Andaman and Nicobar group?","o":["Indigenous tribal settlements","Atoll formations","Volcanic activity","Tropical evergreen forests"],"a":1,"x":"Lakshadweep is made of coral atolls. The Andaman and Nicobar Islands are mostly the tops of submerged mountains, with volcanoes such as Barren Island, and have tribes and evergreen forests.","e":"Barren Island is India's only active volcano.","src":["PRT 31 Mar 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1196","q":"By what popular name is the Godavari river known?","o":["Padma Ganga","Ramganga","Dev Ganga","Dakshin Ganga"],"a":3,"x":"The Godavari, India's second-longest river, is called the 'Dakshin Ganga' (Ganga of the South); it rises near Trimbakeshwar, Nashik.","e":"Its tributaries include the Pranhita, Indravati, Manjira and Sabari.","src":["PRT 31 Mar 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1199","q":"Which river is antecedent in origin and has cut deep gorges through the Himalayas?","o":["Chambal","Yamuna","Son","Indus"],"a":3,"x":"The Indus is an antecedent river: it is older than the Himalayas and kept cutting down as they rose, carving deep gorges.","e":"The Sutlej and Brahmaputra are antecedent too.","src":["PRT 31 Mar 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1211","q":"Which statement about the coastal plains of India is correct?","o":["The Western Coastal Plain is wide and has deltas","The Western Coastal Plain is broad and has lagoons","The Eastern Coastal Plain is narrow and has estuaries","The Eastern Coastal Plain is broad and has delta formations"],"a":3,"x":"The Eastern Coastal Plain is broad and has large deltas (Mahanadi, Godavari, Krishna, Kaveri). The Western Coastal Plain is narrow, with estuaries and lagoons (Kerala's backwaters).","e":"Chilika Lake (Odisha) is India's largest brackish-water lagoon.","src":["PRT 31 Mar 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1224","q":"The Lakshadweep islands are mainly made up of _____________.","o":["Volcanic rocks","Coral islands","Granite rocks","Sand dunes"],"a":1,"x":"The Lakshadweep islands are coral islands (atolls and reefs) in the Arabian Sea.","e":"The Andaman and Nicobar Islands are mainly submerged mountain peaks, with some volcanoes.","src":["PRT 31 Mar 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q3770","q":"Which National Highway among the following connects the maximum number of states?","o":["NH -27","NH-44","NH-30","NH-53"],"a":1,"x":"NH-44 (Srinagar–Kanyakumari) passes through the most states and union territories.","e":"","src":["Nursery Teacher 21 Oct 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q3793","q":"In which Indian state will you find the Arambol Beach?","o":["Karnataka","Goa","Kerala","Andhra Pradesh"],"a":1,"x":"Arambol beach is in North Goa.","e":"","src":["Nursery Teacher 21 Oct 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q4185","q":"The Yamuna does not pass through which of the following states?","o":["Uttar Pradesh","Madhya Pradesh","Uttarakhand","Himachal Pradesh"],"a":1,"x":"The Yamuna flows through Uttarakhand, Himachal Pradesh, Haryana, Delhi and Uttar Pradesh, not Madhya Pradesh.","e":"","src":["Nursery Teacher 23 Oct 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q4187","q":"The source of the Narmada river is located in the _________________ hills of Madhya Pradesh.","o":["Amarkantak","Pachmarhi","Shivpuri","Tamiya"],"a":0,"x":"The Narmada rises at Amarkantak in Madhya Pradesh.","e":"","src":["Nursery Teacher 23 Oct 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q4231","q":"Which is the highest peak among the following?","o":["Chaukhamba","Hardeol","Saser Kangri","Kamet"],"a":3,"x":"Kamet (7,756 m) is higher than Saser Kangri, Hardeol and Chaukhamba.","e":"","src":["Nursery Teacher 23 Oct 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q4233","q":"The Kishtwar Himalayas is located in which of the following states/union territories?","o":["Uttarakhand","Jammu and Kashmir","Assam","Arunachal Pradesh"],"a":1,"x":"The Kishtwar Himalayas are in Jammu and Kashmir.","e":"","src":["Nursery Teacher 23 Oct 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q1371","q":"In which Union Territory is Sukhna Lake situated?","o":["Jammu and Kashmir","Ladakh","Chandigarh","Lakshadweep"],"a":2,"x":"Sukhna Lake is a reservoir at the foot of the Shivalik hills in Chandigarh, created in 1958 by Le Corbusier's team.","e":"Chandigarh is a UT and the capital of both Punjab and Haryana.","src":["TGT 24 Jun 2023 S2"]},{"id":"q1441","q":"'Bangar' and 'Khadar' are sub-types of which soil?","o":["Black soil","Alluvial soil","Red soil","Laterite soil"],"a":1,"x":"Bhangar (older alluvium) and khadar (newer alluvium) are the two types of alluvial soil in the Northern Plains.","e":"Alluvial soil covers about 40% of India's land.","src":["TGT 25 Jun 2023 S2"]},{"id":"q1467","q":"The Narmada originates from which of the following places?","o":["Amarkantak plateau","Mahabaleshwar","Brahmagiri hills","Chotanagpur Plateau"],"a":0,"x":"The Narmada rises at the Amarkantak plateau in Anuppur district, Madhya Pradesh.","e":"The Krishna rises at Mahabaleshwar; the Kaveri in the Brahmagiri hills.","src":["TGT 7 Feb 2024 S2"]},{"id":"q1482","q":"Which of these hills is NOT included in the Purvanchal or Eastern hills?","o":["Manipur hills","Shevroy Hills","Patkai hills","Naga hills"],"a":1,"x":"The Shevaroy hills are in Tamil Nadu (Eastern Ghats; Yercaud). The Patkai, Naga and Manipur hills are part of the Purvanchal (Eastern hills).","e":"The Mizo (Lushai) hills are also in Purvanchal.","src":["TGT 7 Feb 2024 S2"]},{"id":"q1485","q":"What is the length of India's mainland coastline?","o":["6,548 km","6,100 km","7,215 km","7,517 km"],"a":1,"x":"India's mainland coastline is traditionally about 6,100 km; with the islands it is about 7,516 km.","e":"Longest coastline state: Gujarat.","src":["TGT 29 Aug 2025"],"k":"A 2024 remeasurement using finer maps put the total coastline at 11,098 km; exam keys still use 6,100 km (mainland) and 7,516.6 km (total)."},{"id":"q1487","q":"Which of the following mountain ranges defines India's northern boundary?","o":["The Aravalli Range","The Himalayas","The Eastern Ghats","The Western Ghats"],"a":1,"x":"The Himalayas form India's northern boundary.","e":"The Himalayas run about 2,400 km from the Indus to the Brahmaputra.","src":["TGT 29 Aug 2025"]},{"id":"q1509","q":"Which of the following rivers marks the easternmost boundary of the Himalayas?","o":["Jhelum","Mahanadi","Brahmaputra","Godavari"],"a":2,"x":"The Himalayas are bounded by the Indus in the west and the Brahmaputra in the east.","e":"Namcha Barwa marks the eastern end, where the Brahmaputra turns south.","src":["TGT 24 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q1528","q":"According to the Köppen classification, which type of climate is found in Arunachal Pradesh?","o":["Amw – monsoon with short dry season","Dfc – cold humid winter with short summer","BShw – semi-arid steppe climate","Cwg – monsoon with dry winter"],"a":1,"x":"In NCERT's Köppen classification of India, Arunachal Pradesh has the Dfc type: a cold, humid winter with a short summer.","e":"Amw (monsoon with short dry season): the west coast south of Goa. BWhw (hot desert): western Rajasthan.","src":["TGT 24 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q1548","q":"Jelep La and Nathu La are on the main Indo-Tibet trade route through which valley?","o":["Damodar Valley","Chumbi Valley","Ketti Valley","Gurez Valley"],"a":1,"x":"Jelep La and Nathu La, in Sikkim, lie on the old Indo-Tibet trade route through the Chumbi Valley.","e":"Nathu La reopened for India–China border trade in 2006.","src":["TGT 31 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q1656","q":"Around 50 percent of the basin area of Godavari lies in ______.","o":["Andhra Pradesh","Maharashtra","Madhya Pradesh","Odisha"],"a":1,"x":"About half (49%) of the Godavari basin lies in Maharashtra (NCERT Class 9).","e":"","src":["TGT 10 Oct 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2196","q":"Rohtang Pass connects _________________.","o":["The Kullu Valley with the Lahaul and Spiti Valleys of Madhya Pradesh, India.","The Kullu Manali with the Lahaul and Spiti Valleys of Himachal Pradesh, India.","The Kullu Valley with the Lahaul and Spiti Valleys of Kashmir, India.","The Kullu Valley with the Lahaul and Spiti Valleys of Himachal Pradesh, India."],"a":3,"x":"Rohtang Pass connects the Kullu valley with the Lahaul and Spiti valleys of Himachal Pradesh.","e":"","src":["TGT 13 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2350","q":"Which of the following pass is NOT found in Western Ghats?","o":["Thal Ghat","Bhor Ghat","Pal Ghat","Haldighati"],"a":3,"x":"Haldighati is in the Aravallis of Rajasthan; Thal Ghat, Bhor Ghat and Palghat are passes of the Western Ghats.","e":"","src":["TGT 14 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2371","q":"Which of the following river is called ‘Padma’ when it enters Bangladesh?","o":["Yamuna","Ganga","Brahmaputra","Godavari"],"a":1,"x":"The Ganga is called the Padma in Bangladesh.","e":"","src":["TGT 14 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2373","q":"Which of the following statement is correct? I. The folds of Great Himalayas are asymmetrical in nature. II. The outer most range of the Himalayas is called Shiwaliks.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"The folds of the Great Himalayas are asymmetrical, and the Shiwaliks are the outermost range (NCERT Class 9).","e":"","src":["TGT 14 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q3313","q":"The Patkai range consist of hills along India's border with which of the following countries?","o":["Pakistan","China","Bhutan","Myanmar"],"a":3,"x":"The Patkai hills lie along India's border with Myanmar.","e":"","src":["TGT 18 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q3314","q":"The Barak Valley is present in which of the following North-eastern States?","o":["Arunachal Pradesh","Tripura","Assam","Nagaland"],"a":2,"x":"The Barak Valley is in southern Assam.","e":"","src":["TGT 18 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q3357","q":"The Nallamala Range is located in which of the following states?","o":["Andhra Pradesh","Karnataka","Kerala","Tamil Nadu"],"a":0,"x":"The Nallamala hills are in Andhra Pradesh (and Telangana), part of the Eastern Ghats.","e":"","src":["TGT 18 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q3572","q":"Which of the following is largest state of India in terms of area?","o":["Uttar Pradesh","Maharashtra","Rajasthan","West Bengal"],"a":2,"x":"Rajasthan is India's largest state by area.","e":"","src":["TGT 1 Oct 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q5077","q":"Where does Alaknanda River join Bhagirathi River in India?","o":["Dehradun","Devprayag","Prayagraj","Champawat"],"a":1,"x":"The Alaknanda and Bhagirathi meet at Devprayag to form the Ganga.","e":"","src":["TGT 26 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5078","q":"Which is a major tributary of Bhagirathi river?","o":["Bhilangna","Pindar","Dhauliganga","Bishenganga"],"a":0,"x":"The Bhilangna is a major tributary of the Bhagirathi, meeting it at Tehri.","e":"","src":["TGT 26 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5730","q":"Which of the following rivers has its source in the Brahmagiri Range of the Western Ghats?","o":["Mahanadi","Krishna","Kaveri","Tapi"],"a":2,"x":"The Kaveri rises at Talakaveri in the Brahmagiri range of the Western Ghats, Karnataka.","e":"","src":["TGT 4 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q5731","q":"The source of the Teesta river is located in which of the following states?","o":["West Bengal","Sikkim","Arunachal Pradesh","Meghalaya"],"a":1,"x":"The Teesta rises from glaciers in Sikkim.","e":"","src":["TGT 4 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q5754","q":"Golden Quadrilateral Super Highways does not pass through which of the following states?","o":["Maharashtra","Madhya Pradesh","Tamil Nadu","West Bengal"],"a":1,"x":"The Golden Quadrilateral passes through Maharashtra, Tamil Nadu and West Bengal, but not Madhya Pradesh.","e":"","src":["TGT 5 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q5755","q":"World’s longest highway tunnel, Atal Tunnel is built in which of the following mountain ranges?","o":["Pir Panjal Ranges","Dhauladhar Ranges","Zaskar Range","Aravali Range"],"a":0,"x":"The Atal Tunnel near Rohtang is built under the Pir Panjal range in Himachal Pradesh.","e":"","src":["TGT 5 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q5775","q":"Which among the following is a river of Mewar Plateau?","o":["Narmada","Banas","Tapi","Mahi"],"a":1,"x":"The Banas is the main river of the Mewar plateau in Rajasthan.","e":"","src":["TGT 5 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q5818","q":"What is the parent range of the Doddabetta peak?","o":["Western Ghats","Nilgiri Hills","Cardamom Hills","Nallamala Hills"],"a":1,"x":"Doddabetta, Tamil Nadu's highest peak, is in the Nilgiri Hills.","e":"","src":["TGT 5 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5840","q":"The Chitrakoot waterfalls, which is also the widest waterfall in India, is located in which of the following states?","o":["West Bengal","Telangana","Chhattisgarh","Odisha"],"a":2,"x":"Chitrakote Falls on the Indravati in Chhattisgarh is India's widest waterfall.","e":"","src":["TGT 5 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q5863","q":"The pir panjal range is located in ______.","o":["Jammu and Kashmir","Uttarakhand","Meghalaya","Nagaland"],"a":0,"x":"The Pir Panjal range is in Jammu and Kashmir (and Himachal Pradesh).","e":"","src":["TGT 6 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q5864","q":"Which of the following river is also called “Dakshin Ganga”?","o":["Mahanadi","Godavari","Krishna","Narmada"],"a":1,"x":"The Godavari is called the 'Dakshin Ganga'.","e":"","src":["TGT 6 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q5903","q":"Which of the following statement is correct? I. The river Indus rises in Tibet, near lake Mansarowar. II. Brahmaputra river is also known as Jamuna in Tibet.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":0,"x":"The Indus rises in Tibet near Lake Mansarovar; the Brahmaputra is called Tsangpo in Tibet (Jamuna is its name in Bangladesh).","e":"","src":["TGT 6 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q5949","q":"Jelep La pass is located in which state ?","o":["Jammu & Kashmir","Sikkim","Uttarakhand","Himachal Pradesh"],"a":1,"x":"Jelep La pass is in Sikkim, on the route to Tibet.","e":"","src":["TGT 7 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5974","q":"In which of the following state of India, you will find the natural phenomenon called Chir Batti?","o":["Gujarat","Karnataka","Kerala","Tamil Nadu"],"a":0,"x":"'Chir Batti', mysterious ghost lights, are seen in the Banni grasslands of Kutch, Gujarat.","e":"","src":["TGT 7 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q6041","q":"India is surrounded by the ___________ in the east, the _____________ in the west, and the _____________ to the south.","o":["Bay of Bengal--Arabian Sea--Indian Ocean","Arabian Sea--Indian Ocean--Bay of Bengal","Arabian Sea--Bay of Bengal--Indian Ocean","Indian Ocean--Arabian Sea--Bay of Bengal"],"a":0,"x":"India has the Bay of Bengal to the east, the Arabian Sea to the west and the Indian Ocean to the south.","e":"","src":["TGT 8 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6042","q":"Nilgiri Hills is also known as:","o":["Blue Mountains","Green Mountains","Red Mountains","Pink Mountains"],"a":0,"x":"The Nilgiri Hills are known as the 'Blue Mountains'.","e":"","src":["TGT 8 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6263","q":"Which of the following statement is correct? I. The Western Ghats are higher than the Eastern Ghats. II. The height of the Western Ghats progressively increases from north to south.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"The Western Ghats are higher than the Eastern Ghats, and their height increases from north to south.","e":"","src":["TGT 9 Oct 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q6308","q":"Which of the following statement is correct? I. Bhimtal is a natural lake. II. Gobind Sagar is a not a natural lake.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Bhimtal is a natural lake, while Gobind Sagar is a reservoir formed by the Bhakra dam.","e":"","src":["TGT 9 Oct 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q7491","q":"Which of the following pair of river – flow direction is correct? I. Tapi – West II. Narmada – East","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":0,"x":"The Tapi flows west, but the Narmada also flows west, not east.","e":"","src":["TGT 1 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q7514","q":"Which of the following is correct about Mahanadi? I. Mahanadi rises in highlands of Chhattisgarh. II. Its length is about 860 km.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"The Mahanadi rises in the highlands of Chhattisgarh and is about 860 km long.","e":"","src":["TGT 1 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q7534","q":"Which of the following pair of river – tributary is correct? I. Krishna – Bhavani II. Kaveri – Bhima","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":3,"x":"Neither pair is right: the Bhavani is a tributary of the Kaveri and the Bhima of the Krishna.","e":"","src":["TGT 7 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q7578","q":"What is the time lag from Gujarat to Arunachal Pradesh?","o":["15 minutes","30 minutes","1 hour","2 hours"],"a":3,"x":"The time difference between Gujarat and Arunachal Pradesh is about two hours (roughly 30° of longitude).","e":"","src":["TGT 8 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q7965","q":"Arrange the following airports with respect to their location from South to North: 1: Guru Ram Dass Jee International Airport 2: Veer Savarkar International Airport 3: Indira Gandhi International Airport 4: Bharat Ratna Babasaheb Dr. B.R. Ambedkar International Airport","o":["4, 3, 2, 1","2, 4, 3, 1","3, 4, 1, 2","2, 3, 1, 4"],"a":1,"x":"South to north: Port Blair (Veer Savarkar), Nagpur (Dr Ambedkar), Delhi (Indira Gandhi), Amritsar (Guru Ram Dass Jee).","e":"","src":["TGT 6 Jul 2023 S2"]},{"id":"q8765","q":"Sukhna Lake is located in which of the following union territories?","o":["Lakshadweep","Jammu and Kashmir","Chandigarh","Ladakh"],"a":2,"x":"Sukhna Lake is in Chandigarh.","e":"","src":["TGT 24 Jun 2023 S2"]},{"id":"q3137","q":"‘Wular’ the largest lake of fresh water in India is situated in which of the following states/union territories?","o":["Assam","Kashmir","Punjab","Andhra Pradesh"],"a":1,"x":"Wular Lake in Jammu and Kashmir is India's largest freshwater lake.","e":"","src":["Special Educator 17 Oct 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q7597","q":"The famous Pichola lake is located in which of the following states?","o":["Andhra Pradesh","Rajasthan","Sikkim","Tamil Nadu"],"a":1,"x":"Lake Pichola is in Udaipur, Rajasthan.","e":"","src":["Special Educator 8 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q7598","q":"The Cardamom Hills is a part of which of the following ranges?","o":["Western Ghats","Eastern Ghats","Aravalli","Vindhyas"],"a":0,"x":"The Cardamom Hills are part of the southern Western Ghats.","e":"","src":["Special Educator 8 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q8797","q":"Which region will benefit the most from the Ken-Betwa River Linking Project?","o":["Deccan Plateau in Karnataka","Bundelkhand (Uttar Pradesh and Madhya Pradesh)","Himalayan foothills in Uttarakhand","Coastal Andhra Pradesh"],"a":1,"x":"The Ken–Betwa link project is meant mainly to benefit the drought-hit Bundelkhand region of UP and MP.","e":"","src":["Special Educator 24 Jun 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q8798","q":"What separates the Little Andaman from the Great Andaman in the Andaman Islands group?","o":["Malacca Strait","Ten Degree Channel","Duncan Passage","Gulf of Mannar"],"a":2,"x":"The Duncan Passage separates Little Andaman from South Andaman (Great Andaman); the Ten Degree Channel separates the Andamans from the Nicobars.","e":"","src":["Special Educator 24 Jun 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q9841","q":"Which of the following rivers makes the second biggest waterfall in India, known as Sivasamudram?","o":["Mahanadi","Krishna","Godavari","Kaveri"],"a":3,"x":"The Kaveri forms the Sivasamudram Falls in Karnataka.","e":"","src":["Special Educator 14 Jul 2024 S1"]},{"id":"q9844","q":"Indira Point, which is considered the southernmost tip of India, is located on which of the following islands?","o":["Little Andaman Island","Long Island","Campbell Bay","Great Nicobar Island"],"a":3,"x":"Indira Point, India's southernmost tip, is on Great Nicobar Island.","e":"","src":["Special Educator 14 Jul 2024 S1"]},{"id":"q0763","q":"Which of the following statements about the Lakshadweep islands is correct?","o":["A dormant volcano named Narcondam is located in Lakshadweep.","Kavaratti island is the administrative headquarters of Lakshadweep.","The Lakshadweep islands are located in the Bay of Bengal.","Wheeler Island is a part of the Lakshadweep group."],"a":1,"x":"Kavaratti is the administrative headquarters of Lakshadweep, in the Arabian Sea. Narcondam volcano is in the Andamans (Bay of Bengal), and Wheeler (Abdul Kalam) Island is off Odisha.","e":"Minicoy is Lakshadweep's southernmost and second-largest island.","src":["PGT 25 Sep 2025"]},{"id":"q0781","q":"Which of the following pairs of rivers in India and their sources is correctly matched?","o":["Indus – Nasik","Ganga – Gangotri Glacier","Brahmaputra- Amarkantak Plateau","Godavari – Siachen Glacier"],"a":1,"x":"The Ganga rises as the Bhagirathi from the Gangotri Glacier (Gaumukh) in Uttarakhand. The Indus rises in Tibet, the Brahmaputra near Mansarovar, and the Godavari near Nashik.","e":"The Bhagirathi and Alaknanda meet at Devprayag to form the Ganga.","src":["PGT 11 Jul 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0814","q":"India primarily experiences which type of climate?","o":["Monsoon climate","Equatorial climate","Tundra climate","Mediterranean climate"],"a":0,"x":"India has a tropical monsoon climate: seasonal reversal of winds and most rain in the south-west monsoon months.","e":"Summers are hot and winters relatively dry, with regional variation.","src":["PGT 22 Sep 2025"]},{"id":"q0835","q":"Which among the following is NOT a characteristic of peninsular rivers of India?","o":["Most of the major peninsular rivers except Narmada and Tapti flow from west to east.","The Mahanadi rises near Sihawa in Raipur district of Chhattisgarh.","Most of the major peninsular rivers except Narmada and Tapti flow from east to west.","The Peninsular drainage system is older than the Himalayan."],"a":2,"x":"The false statement says most peninsular rivers flow east to west. Most (Mahanadi, Godavari, Krishna, Kaveri) flow west to east into the Bay of Bengal; the Narmada and Tapi flow west.","e":"The Peninsular drainage is older than the Himalayan.","src":["PGT 26 Sep 2025"]},{"id":"q0879","q":"The Meghalaya plateau is separated from the Peninsular plateau by which of the following?","o":["Indo-Burma","Great Boundary","Malda Gap","West Coast"],"a":2,"x":"The Meghalaya (Shillong) plateau is separated from the Chotanagpur part of the Peninsular plateau by the Malda Gap.","e":"Garo, Khasi and Jaintia hills make up the Meghalaya plateau.","src":["PGT 24 Sep 2025"]},{"id":"q6390","q":"Cuttack is situated on the bank of which of the following rivers?","o":["Mahanadi","Ganga","Kaveri","Gomti"],"a":0,"x":"Cuttack stands on the Mahanadi in Odisha.","e":"","src":["PGT 18 Jul 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q6476","q":"Phawngpui is the highest mountain peak of which of the following states?","o":["Mizoram","Sikkim","Meghalaya","Nagaland"],"a":0,"x":"Phawngpui (Blue Mountain) is the highest peak of Mizoram.","e":"","src":["PGT 30 Jul 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6495","q":"Which among the following is not a tributary of Godavari river?","o":["Manjira","Penganga","Bhima","Indravati"],"a":2,"x":"The Bhima is a tributary of the Krishna; the Manjira, Penganga and Indravati join the Godavari.","e":"","src":["PGT 23 Jul 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q6515","q":"Which among the following is a west flowing river?","o":["Mahanadi","Godavari","Tapti","Krishna"],"a":2,"x":"The Tapi flows west into the Arabian Sea; the Mahanadi, Godavari and Krishna flow east.","e":"","src":["PGT 30 Jul 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q6569","q":"Which among the following peaks is also known as 'Temple of God'?","o":["Trisul I","Kamet","Nanda Devi","Hardeol"],"a":3,"x":"Hardeol peak in Uttarakhand is known as the 'Temple of God'.","e":"","src":["PGT 3 Jul 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q6570","q":"Guwahati is situated on the banks of which of the following rivers?","o":["Kaveri","Mahanadi","Brahmaputra","Yamuna"],"a":2,"x":"Guwahati lies on the banks of the Brahmaputra.","e":"","src":["PGT 3 Jul 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q6592","q":"Sabarmati river originates from which of the following mountain ranges?","o":["Satpura","Aravalli","Vindhayas","Eastern Ghats"],"a":1,"x":"The Sabarmati rises in the Aravalli hills of Rajasthan.","e":"","src":["PGT 3 Jul 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6593","q":"Which among the following rivers is also known as Red river in India?","o":["Yamuna","Brahmaputra","Kosi","Gandak"],"a":1,"x":"The Brahmaputra is sometimes called the 'Red River' of India because of the red soil it carries.","e":"","src":["PGT 3 Jul 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6614","q":"Which among the following rivers is a tributary of the Yamuna river, starting from Madhya Pradesh?","o":["Chambal","Kosi","Gandak","Son"],"a":0,"x":"The Chambal rises in Madhya Pradesh (near Mhow) and joins the Yamuna.","e":"","src":["PGT 4 Jul 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q6615","q":"Saser Muztagh range is the easternmost subrange of ___________ range.","o":["Western Ghats","Eastern Ghats","Vindhayas","Karakoram"],"a":3,"x":"The Saser Muztagh is the easternmost subrange of the Karakoram.","e":"","src":["PGT 4 Jul 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q6638","q":"The ancient name of which of the following rivers is Satadree?","o":["Beas","Sutlej","Ravi","Jhelum"],"a":1,"x":"The Sutlej was known as Shatadru (Satadree) in ancient times.","e":"","src":["PGT 18 Jul 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6683","q":"Which among the following pairs is incorrect?","o":["Ganga - East flowing river","Mahi - West flowing river","Krishna - West flowing river","Mahanadi - East flowing river"],"a":2,"x":"The Krishna flows east into the Bay of Bengal, so 'Krishna – west flowing' is the wrong pair.","e":"","src":["PGT 30 Jul 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q7132","q":"The Shyok and Gilgit are tributaries of which river?","o":["Ganga","Brahmaputra","Godavari","Indus"],"a":3,"x":"The Shyok and Gilgit are tributaries of the Indus.","e":"","src":["PGT 31 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q7192","q":"Which among the following rivers is also known as 'salt river' because of its excessive salinity?","o":["Luni","Mahi","Krishna","Tapti"],"a":0,"x":"The Luni in Rajasthan is called the 'salt river' because its water turns saline.","e":"","src":["PGT 16 Jul 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q7232","q":"Amarkantak is an important peak located in which of the following mountain ranges?","o":["Vindhyas","Western Ghats","Eastern Ghats","Maikal"],"a":3,"x":"Amarkantak lies in the Maikal range, where the Narmada and Son rise.","e":"","src":["PGT 31 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q7275","q":"Which of the following is the largest District in India as per area?","o":["Kuchchh","Mahe","Mumbai","Bangalore"],"a":0,"x":"Kutch in Gujarat is India's largest district by area.","e":"","src":["PGT 10 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q7725","q":"Which of the following rivers originates from a glacier near Bokhar Chu in the Tibetan region?","o":["Ravi","Chenab","Indus","Brahmaputra"],"a":2,"x":"The Indus rises from a glacier near Bokhar Chu in Tibet.","e":"","src":["PGT 16 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q8120","q":"Which of the following hills is NOT located in the North Eastern states of India?","o":["Garo hills","Khasi hills","Rajmahal hills","Jaintia hills"],"a":2,"x":"The Rajmahal hills are in Jharkhand; the Garo, Khasi and Jaintia hills are in Meghalaya.","e":"","src":["PGT 21 Jun 2023 S1"]},{"id":"q8501","q":"The longitudinal valley lying between lesser Himalaya and the Shiwaliks are known as ________.","o":["Bhabar","Duns","Kankar","Doabs"],"a":1,"x":"Duns are longitudinal valleys between the Lesser Himalaya and the Shiwaliks, like Dehradun.","e":"","src":["PGT 22 Jun 2023 S2"]},{"id":"q8503","q":"Which is the World’s longest Highway tunnel built with ultra-modern specifications in the Pir Panjal range of Himalayas?","o":["Banihal Tunnel","Atal Tunnel","Dr. Syama Prasad Mookerjee Tunnel","Chattergala Tunnel"],"a":1,"x":"The Atal Tunnel under Rohtang Pass in the Pir Panjal range is the world's longest highway tunnel above 10,000 feet.","e":"","src":["PGT 22 Jun 2023 S2"]},{"id":"q9543","q":"Lucknow is situated on the banks of which of the following rivers?","o":["Jhelum","Gomti","Yamuna","Hooghly"],"a":1,"x":"Lucknow stands on the Gomti river.","e":"","src":["PGT 30 Jun 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q9669","q":"Which among the following rivers is also known as the 'twin' or 'handmaid' of the Narmada?","o":["Mahi","Tapti","Sabarmati","Luni"],"a":1,"x":"The Tapti is called the twin or handmaid of the Narmada.","e":"","src":["PGT 29 Jun 2021"]},{"id":"q1634","q":"Which of the following is NOT a tributary of Ganga?","o":["Lohit","Ghaghara","Betwa","Son"],"a":0,"x":"The Lohit is a tributary of the Brahmaputra; the Ghaghara, Betwa (via Yamuna) and Son belong to the Ganga system.","e":"","src":["Senior Scientific Assistant (Lie-Detection) 10 Oct 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1674","q":"The famous Pong Dam Lake is located in which of the following states?","o":["Rajasthan","Tripura","Assam","Himachal Pradesh"],"a":3,"x":"The Pong Dam lake (Maharana Pratap Sagar) on the Beas is in Himachal Pradesh.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Computer) 10 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1675","q":"The highest mountain peak of _________ is Deomali.","o":["West Bengal","Assam","Odisha","Maharashtra"],"a":2,"x":"Deomali is the highest peak in Odisha.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Computer) 10 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1692","q":"In the question, there are two statements given, labelled as Assertion (A) and Reason (R). You have to choose the correct alternative from the given options. Assertion (A) : Most of the Himalayan rivers are perennial. Reason (R) : They are fed by melting snow.","o":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"a":0,"x":"Most Himalayan rivers are perennial because they are fed by melting snow as well as rain.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Computer) 10 Sep 2021 S1","Technical Assistant (Electronic & Communication) 8 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2254","q":"Which of the following is NOT a freshwater lake?","o":["Dal Lake","Loktak Lake","Barapani Lake","Sambhar Lake"],"a":3,"x":"Sambhar Lake in Rajasthan is a salt lake; Dal, Loktak and Barapani are freshwater.","e":"","src":["Senior Scientific Assistant (Ballistics) 13 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2255","q":"India shares its land boundaries with ______ and ______ in the north-west.","o":["Pakistan; Afghanistan","Pakistan; China","Nepal; Bhutan","Myanmar; Bangladesh"],"a":0,"x":"In the north-west India shares land borders with Pakistan and Afghanistan.","e":"","src":["Senior Scientific Assistant (Ballistics) 13 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2574","q":"Khasi Hills are a part of Garo-Khasi Range in ________.","o":["Assam","Meghalaya","Manipur","Odisha"],"a":1,"x":"The Khasi Hills, part of the Garo–Khasi range, are in Meghalaya.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Medical Electronics) 16 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2647","q":"The northern plains of India are formed by the alluvial deposits brought by 3 rivers. Which of the following is NOT one of them?","o":["The Brahmaputra","The Ganga","The Indus","The Yamuna"],"a":3,"x":"The northern plains were built by the Indus, Ganga and Brahmaputra river systems.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Interior Design) 16 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q3088","q":"Pushkar, which is a holy place for Hindus is located in which of the following states of India?","o":["Haryana","Delhi","Gujrat","Rajasthan"],"a":3,"x":"Pushkar is in Rajasthan.","e":"","src":["Draftsman 17 Oct 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q3168","q":"Which one of the following state is located in the Western Himalayas?","o":["Arunachal Pradesh","Nagaland","Uttarakhand","Mizoram"],"a":2,"x":"Uttarakhand is in the Western Himalayas; Arunachal, Nagaland and Mizoram are in the east.","e":"","src":["Pharmacist 18 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q3169","q":"In which state will you find the rivers Lahen, Lachung, Rangit and Rangpo?","o":["Sikkim","Jharkhand","Assam","Chhattisgarh"],"a":0,"x":"The Lachen, Lachung, Rangit and Rangpo rivers flow through Sikkim.","e":"","src":["Pharmacist 18 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q3178","q":"Which of the following neighbouring countries shares its boundary with the Indian state Sikkim?","o":["Pakistan","Bhutan","Bangladesh","Maldives"],"a":1,"x":"Sikkim borders Bhutan, Nepal and China.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Plastic) 18 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q3501","q":"Which of the following is not a tributary of Ganga?","o":["Ghaghra","Kosi","Manas","Mahananda"],"a":2,"x":"The Manas is a tributary of the Brahmaputra; the Ghaghra, Kosi and Mahananda join the Ganga.","e":"","src":["Scientific Assistant (Lie-Detection) 1 Oct 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q3987","q":"Which hills are located to the south of the Kaziranga National Park?","o":["Khasi","Garo","Jaintia","Mikir"],"a":3,"x":"The Mikir (Karbi Anglong) Hills lie south of Kaziranga National Park.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Printing) 22 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q4118","q":"Which among the following ranges is also known as the Outer Himalayas?","o":["Zaskar","Aravalli","Shiwalik","Pir Panjal"],"a":2,"x":"The Shiwaliks are the Outer Himalayas.","e":"","src":["Veterinary and Livestock Inspector 23 Apr 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q4119","q":"Which among the following is not a tributary of Mahanadi?","o":["Ong","Tel","Hasdeo","Banas"],"a":3,"x":"The Banas is a tributary of the Chambal; the Ong, Tel and Hasdeo join the Mahanadi.","e":"","src":["Veterinary and Livestock Inspector 23 Apr 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q4163","q":"Which of the following is the west flowing river in India?","o":["Koyna","Tapi","Kaveri","Krishna"],"a":1,"x":"The Tapi flows west into the Arabian Sea; the Krishna, Kaveri and Koyna system drain east.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Automobiles) 23 Oct 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q4255","q":"Which of the following waterfalls is located on the Ranchi Purulia Highway in the state of Jharkhand?","o":["The Jog Falls","The Hundru Falls","The Chuliya Falls","The Dudhsagar Falls"],"a":1,"x":"The Hundru Falls, on the Ranchi–Purulia road, are in Jharkhand.","e":"","src":["Workshop Instructor 24 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q4657","q":"In which range of Himalaya, the 'Photu La' (Fotu La) pass is located?","o":["Ladakh range","Zanskar range","Pir Panjal range","Karakoram range"],"a":1,"x":"Fotu La pass is in the Zanskar range, on the Srinagar–Leh road.","e":"","src":["Librarian 25 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q4672","q":"Koyna dam is situated on Koyna river which is a tributary of the:","o":["Mahi river","Kaveri river","Godavari river","Krishna river"],"a":3,"x":"The Koyna, on which the Koyna Dam stands, is a tributary of the Krishna.","e":"","src":["Scientific Assistant (biology) 25 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q4723","q":"The one of the highest peaks of the western ghats at a (2,695metres) elevation above sea level is ____________.","o":["Dhaulagiri","Kamet","Annapurna","Anai Mudi"],"a":3,"x":"Anai Mudi (2,695 m) is the highest peak of the Western Ghats.","e":"","src":["Laboratory Assistant (HRD Quality Control) 25 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q4830","q":"The ___________ mountain range continue eastwards as the Bharner and Kaimur hills.","o":["Aravalli","Vindhyas","Satpura","Western Ghats"],"a":1,"x":"The Vindhyas continue eastwards as the Bharner and Kaimur hills.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Architecture) 26 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q5121","q":"Which among the following mountain ranges is the longest in the Lesser Himalayas?","o":["Mahabharat Lekh","Dhauladhar","Pir Panjal","Nag Tibba"],"a":2,"x":"The Pir Panjal is the longest range of the Lesser Himalayas.","e":"","src":["Assistant Engineer (Civil) 26 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q5250","q":"The ancient name of the_________ river is Vitasta.","o":["Jhelum","Beas","Ganga","Chenab"],"a":0,"x":"Vitasta is the ancient name of the Jhelum.","e":"","src":["Assistant Engineer (Civil) 27 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5251","q":"The Kamet is the highest summit in the ________ mountain range.","o":["Western Ghats","Zaskar","Eastern Ghats","Aravalli"],"a":1,"x":"Kamet is the highest peak of the Zaskar range.","e":"","src":["Assistant Engineer (Civil) 27 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5292","q":"Which of the following is the highest peak in the state of Maharashtra as of August 2021?","o":["Anjaneri","Lohagad","Kalsubai","Mount Everest"],"a":2,"x":"Kalsubai is the highest peak in Maharashtra.","e":"","src":["Assistant Engineer (Civil) 27 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q5348","q":"Port Blair is located in:","o":["North Andaman Island","Car Nicobar","South Andaman Island","Little Andaman Island"],"a":2,"x":"Port Blair (Sri Vijaya Puram) is on South Andaman Island.","e":"","src":["Laboratory Assistant (Physics) 28 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q5456","q":"Peak XV in the Himalayas was renamed _____ in 1865.","o":["K2","Mount Everest","Kanchenjunga","Nanda Devi"],"a":1,"x":"Peak XV was renamed Mount Everest in 1865.","e":"","src":["Assistant Chemist 29 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q5635","q":"The Gulf of Khambhat, also called “Gulf of Cambay” is the gulf of ______.","o":["Bay of Bengal","Arabian Sea","Pacific Ocean","Atlantic Ocean"],"a":1,"x":"The Gulf of Khambhat (Cambay) is an inlet of the Arabian Sea.","e":"","src":["Labour Welfare Inspector 30 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5644","q":"Which one of the following hill stations of Odisha is popularly known as the ‘Kashmir of Odisha’?","o":["Orasuni","Dumuriput","Taptapani","Daringbadi"],"a":3,"x":"Daringbadi in Kandhamal district is called the 'Kashmir of Odisha'.","e":"","src":["Driver 30 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q5645","q":"Which city among the following has ‘summers that are cool and very rainy’?","o":["Udaipur","Amritsar","Shillong","Indore"],"a":2,"x":"Shillong has cool, very rainy summers.","e":"","src":["Driver 30 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q5647","q":"In which Indian state will you find the ‘Saramati Peak’?","o":["Chhattisgarh","Himachal Pradesh","Nagaland","Arunachal Pradesh"],"a":2,"x":"Saramati (about 3,841 m) is the highest peak of Nagaland, on the Myanmar border.","e":"","src":["Driver 30 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q5672","q":"‘Chilika’ in Odisha is an example of a ______.","o":["Waterfall","Starait","Lagoon","Hill"],"a":2,"x":"Chilika in Odisha is India's largest brackish-water lagoon.","e":"","src":["Driver 30 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q5797","q":"The __________ are also known as Sahyadri hills.","o":["Eastern Ghats","Western Ghats","Aravalli","Vindhya"],"a":1,"x":"The Western Ghats are also called the Sahyadri.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Electrical) 5 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5926","q":"Which river divides peninsular block of India into two parts?","o":["Narmada","Mahanadi","Ganga","Kaveri"],"a":0,"x":"The Narmada divides the Peninsular Plateau into the Central Highlands and the Deccan Plateau.","e":"","src":["Laboratory Assistant (Chemistry) 7 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q6003","q":"Cuttack is located on the banks of which of the following rivers?","o":["Krishna","Yamuna","Mahanadi","Sutlej"],"a":2,"x":"Cuttack stands on the Mahanadi.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Electronic & Communication) 8 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6056","q":"____________ is an island in the Hirakud reservoir completely inhabited by wild cattle which were once left behind by villagers when the village was vacated for construction of the dam.","o":["Cattle Island","Sagar Island","Abdul Kalam Island","Sriharikota Island"],"a":0,"x":"Cattle Island in the Hirakud reservoir is inhabited by wild cattle left behind when villages were vacated.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Mechanical) 8 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q6282","q":"\"Chinatown\", India’s only Chinese settlement is located in which of the following cities?","o":["Kolkata","Jaipur","Agra","Bhubaneshwar"],"a":0,"x":"India's only Chinatown is in Kolkata.","e":"","src":["Section Officer (Horticulture) 9 Oct 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q6328","q":"India shares the longest boundary with which of the following countries?","o":["Bangladesh","China","Bhutan","Nepal"],"a":0,"x":"India's longest land border is with Bangladesh (about 4,096 km).","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Beauty Culture) 26 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q6329","q":"Which among the following mountain ranges is also known as Krishnagiri range?","o":["Zanskar","Karakoram","Ladakh","Western Ghats"],"a":1,"x":"The Karakoram range is also called Krishnagiri.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Beauty Culture) 26 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q6408","q":"Betalongchhip is the highest mountain peak of which of the following states?","o":["West Bengal","Assam","Tripura","Mizoram"],"a":2,"x":"Betalongchhip is the highest peak in Tripura.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Commercial Art) 14 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6429","q":"The famous Nallamala Hills are one of the ranges of the __________ that stretch over the Mahabubnagar and Kadapa districts of Andhra Pradesh.","o":["Aravalli","Vindhayas","Western Ghats","Eastern Ghats"],"a":3,"x":"The Nallamala Hills are part of the Eastern Ghats.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Digital Electronics) 7 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q6430","q":"Which among the following lakes in not in Jammu and Kashmir?","o":["Dal lake","Wular lake","Rudrasagar lake","Tulian Lake"],"a":2,"x":"Rudrasagar Lake is in Tripura, not Jammu and Kashmir.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Digital Electronics) 7 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q6534","q":"Which among the following is an east flowing river in India?","o":["Narmada","Tapi","Sabarmati","Krishna"],"a":3,"x":"The Krishna flows east into the Bay of Bengal; the Narmada, Tapi and Sabarmati flow west.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Garments Fabrication Technology) 2 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q6711","q":"Which of the following pair regarding range enclosing the Ladakh is correct? I. Karakoram – North II. Zanskar – South","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Ladakh is enclosed by the Karakoram to the north and the Zanskar to the south.","e":"","src":["Investigator 26 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q7357","q":"Which of the following statement is correct about Aravali Hills? I. They are highly eroded and broken hills. II. They extend from Odisha to Delhi.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":0,"x":"The Aravallis are highly eroded, broken hills extending from Gujarat to Delhi, not from Odisha.","e":"","src":["Physical Education Teacher 2 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q7381","q":"Which of the following pair of river - tributary is correct? I. Tapi – Mahi II. Narmada – Wardha","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":3,"x":"Neither pair is right: the Mahi is a separate west-flowing river, and the Wardha is a tributary of the Godavari, not the Narmada.","e":"","src":["Physical Education Teacher 2 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q7402","q":"Narmada basin covers the part of ______. I. Gujarat II. Madhya Pradesh","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"The Narmada basin covers parts of Madhya Pradesh, Gujarat and Maharashtra.","e":"","src":["Physical Education Teacher 2 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q7421","q":"Which among the following islands was formerly known as ‘Wheeler Island’?","o":["Sriharikota","Abdul Kalam","Havelock","Sivasamudram"],"a":1,"x":"Wheeler Island in Odisha was renamed Abdul Kalam Island.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Fashion Design) 14 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q7422","q":"Rudraprayag is the place of confluence of Alaknanda and _________ rivers.","o":["Mandakini","Dhauliganga","Nandakini","Pindar"],"a":0,"x":"The Alaknanda meets the Mandakini at Rudraprayag.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Fashion Design) 14 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q7442","q":"The Jaintia Hills is located in which of the following states?","o":["Meghalaya","Tripura","Tamil Nadu","Himachal Pradesh"],"a":0,"x":"The Jaintia Hills are in Meghalaya.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Library Science) 7 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q7465","q":"Bangladesh does NOT share it's boundary with which of the following states?","o":["Assam","Tripura","West Bengal","Sikkim"],"a":3,"x":"Sikkim does not border Bangladesh; Assam, Tripura and West Bengal do.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Pharmacy) 2 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q7482","q":"In the question, there are two statements given, labelled as Assertion (A) and Reason (R). You have to choose the correct alternative from the given options. Assertion (A): River Narmada flows westward. Reason (R): Narmada drains into the Bay of Bengal.","o":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"a":2,"x":"The Narmada flows west, but into the Arabian Sea, not the Bay of Bengal.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Pharmacy) 2 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q8194","q":"Which of these is NOT a part of The North-Eastern Plateau Region of India?","o":["Chhotanagpur (Jharkhand)","Odisha Plateau","Tamil Nadu uplands","West Bengal"],"a":2,"x":"The Tamil Nadu uplands are not part of the North-Eastern Plateau region.","e":"","src":["Junior Engineer (Electrical) or Section Officer (Electrical) 22 Jun 2022 S1"]},{"id":"q8577","q":"Which of the following is the main source of water for Wular Lake?","o":["River Chenab","River Jhelum","River Shyok","River Tawi"],"a":1,"x":"The Jhelum is the main source of water for Wular Lake.","e":"","src":["Shift Incharge 25 Jun 2023 S2"]},{"id":"q8713","q":"The Gulf of Kutch is situated on the coast of which state in India?","o":["Gujarat","Maharashtra","Goa","Karnataka"],"a":0,"x":"The Gulf of Kutch is on the Gujarat coast.","e":"","src":["Tailor Master 25 Jun 2023 S3"]},{"id":"q9897","q":"Which mountain range passes through Nashik district of Maharashtra?","o":["Dhauladhar","Vindhya","Palani","Satmala"],"a":3,"x":"The Satmala range passes through Nashik district, Maharashtra.","e":"","src":["Junior Engineer (Mechanical) 5 Oct 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q9898","q":"Which of the following are the two most popular high-altitude lakes in Ladakh?","o":["Tsomgo Chho and Gadsar","Tarsar and Samiti","Pangong Tso and Tso Moriri","Gurudongmar and Tso Chen"],"a":2,"x":"Pangong Tso and Tso Moriri are Ladakh's best-known high-altitude lakes.","e":"","src":["Junior Engineer (Mechanical) 5 Oct 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q9937","q":"The south-eastern part of which state lies in the Malwa plateau?","o":["Bihar","West Bengal","Rajasthan","Odisha"],"a":2,"x":"South-eastern Rajasthan lies on the Malwa plateau.","e":"","src":["Junior Engineer (Electrical) or Section Officer (Electrical) 16 Mar 2022"]},{"id":"q9971","q":"Which of the Panch Prayags is the place where the Alaknanda meets the Bhagirathi to form the Ganga?","o":["Devprayag","Rudraprayag","Nandprayag","Karnaprayag"],"a":0,"x":"At Devprayag the Alaknanda meets the Bhagirathi to form the Ganga.","e":"","src":["Junior Engineer (Electrical) or Section Officer (Electrical) 22 Jun 2022 S3"]}]},{"id":"resources","n":"Resources, Agriculture and Industry","qs":[{"id":"q0029","q":"According to India’s Water Bodies Census 2018-19, which of the following types of water bodies accounts for the highest proportion in India?","o":["Tanks","Reservoirs","Ponds","Lakes"],"a":2,"x":"India's first Water Bodies Census (2018-19) counted about 24.2 lakh water bodies. Ponds were the largest share (about 59.5%), then tanks and reservoirs.","e":"West Bengal has the most water bodies; Tamil Nadu has the most lakes; Andhra Pradesh the most tanks.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0038","q":"According to India’s Water Bodies Census 2018-19, which state reported the highest number of lakes?","o":["Kerala","Karnataka","Tamil Nadu","West Bengal"],"a":2,"x":"The Water Bodies Census 2018-19 found Tamil Nadu has the most lakes, while West Bengal has the most water bodies overall (mostly ponds).","e":"Andhra Pradesh leads in tanks.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0102","q":"Which of the following pairs of agencies involved in mineral exploration and their headquarters is correct?","o":["Geological Survey of India (GSI) – Kolkata","Oil and Natural Gas Corporation (ONGC) – Jaipur","Mineral Exploration Corporation Ltd. (MECL) – Chennai","National Mineral Development Corporation (NMDC) – Bhopal"],"a":0,"x":"The Geological Survey of India (1851) has its headquarters in Kolkata. The other pairs are wrong: ONGC is in New Delhi (registered at Dehradun), MECL in Nagpur, NMDC in Hyderabad.","e":"GSI was set up mainly to find coal for the railways.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0113","q":"India has the biggest reserve of _______ in the Koderma district of Jharkhand.","o":["iron ore","mica","coal","manganese"],"a":1,"x":"The Koderma belt of Jharkhand holds India's biggest mica reserves; mica is used in electrical and electronic insulation.","e":"Other mica areas: Nellore (Andhra Pradesh) and Bhilwara–Ajmer (Rajasthan).","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0145","q":"In which of the following years was India's first water bodies census conducted?","o":["2015-16","2021-22","2018-19","2011-12"],"a":2,"x":"India's first Water Bodies Census was carried out in 2018-19 with the 6th Minor Irrigation Census; the report came out in 2023.","e":"It counted about 24.2 lakh water bodies.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0148","q":"Which of the following crops is best suited for black soil?","o":["Sugarcane","Cotton","Rice","Millet"],"a":1,"x":"Black (regur) soil holds moisture well and is ideal for cotton, which is why it is called black cotton soil.","e":"Found on the Deccan lava plateau: Maharashtra, Gujarat, MP.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0179","q":"In which of the following years was the foundation stone for the Visakhapatnam Steel Plant laid?","o":["1957","1981","1971","1966"],"a":2,"x":"Indira Gandhi laid the foundation stone of the Visakhapatnam Steel Plant in January 1971 after the 'Visakha Ukku, Andhrula Hakku' agitation.","e":"It is India's first shore-based integrated steel plant (RINL).","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0198","q":"The North Karanpura and South Karanpura coal reserves are located in which State?","o":["Jharkhand","West Bengal","Chhattisgarh","Andhra Pradesh"],"a":0,"x":"North and South Karanpura coalfields are in the Damodar valley of Jharkhand.","e":"Jharia is India's main coking-coal field; Raniganj, the first to be worked, is in West Bengal.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0239","q":"Which of the following is a section of a dam through which it is intended that water will flow either intermittently or continuously?","o":["Basin","Weir","Guls","Kuls"],"a":1,"x":"A weir is a low barrier across a river over which water flows continuously or at intervals; it raises the level upstream for irrigation or diversion.","e":"A dam stores water; a weir mostly controls the level.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0361","q":"What economic role do ports play in India's development?","o":["Primarily for military purposes","Mainly for fishing activities","Solely for recreational purposes","Facilitate trade and commerce"],"a":3,"x":"Ports are gateways for international trade; about 95% of India's trade by volume moves through them.","e":"Sagarmala is India's port-led development programme.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S3","PRT 10 Apr 2026 S2","PRT 18 Feb 2026 S1","PRT 19 Feb 2026 S3","PRT 20 Feb 2026 S2","PRT 31 Mar 2026 S1","Stenographer 19 Apr 2021 S1","Stenographer 23 Apr 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0381","q":"Man-made water bodies that were traditionally created to allow boats and other vessels to travel between two major water bodies are known as:","o":["ports","lagoons","canals","brooks"],"a":2,"x":"Canals are artificial waterways, traditionally dug so boats could travel between two water bodies.","e":"Suez Canal: Mediterranean to Red Sea. Panama Canal: Atlantic to Pacific.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q1597","q":"Which of the following is a non-traditional energy source?","o":["Petroleum","Coal","Natural Gas","Wind"],"a":3,"x":"Wind is a non-traditional (non-conventional, renewable) energy source. Petroleum, coal and natural gas are conventional fossil fuels.","e":"Tamil Nadu and Gujarat lead India in wind power.","src":["Patwari 17 Sep 2022 S2"]},{"id":"q1599","q":"India's first fully organic state is ______.","o":["Meghalaya","Nagaland","Sikkim","Tripura"],"a":2,"x":"Sikkim was declared India's first fully organic state in January 2016.","e":"Sikkim won the UN FAO's Future Policy Gold Award in 2018.","src":["Patwari 17 Sep 2022 S2"]},{"id":"q1602","q":"Which of the following minerals is not metallic?","o":["Copper","Silver","Gold","Limestone"],"a":3,"x":"Limestone is a non-metallic mineral, mainly calcium carbonate, used for cement. Copper, silver and gold are metallic.","e":"Other non-metallic minerals: mica, gypsum, salt.","src":["Patwari 17 Sep 2022 S2"]},{"id":"q1609","q":"In which state of India is a geothermal power plant located?","o":["Assam","Tripura","Nagaland","Himachal Pradesh"],"a":3,"x":"Himachal Pradesh's Manikaran (Parbati valley) has hot springs where India's geothermal energy potential was explored with a pilot plant.","e":"Puga valley in Ladakh is India's most promising geothermal site.","src":["Patwari 17 Sep 2022 S3"]},{"id":"q0490","q":"Which among the following is not an example of abiotic natural resource?","o":["Iron","Coal","Silver","Gold"],"a":1,"x":"Coal forms from buried plant matter, so it is counted as a biotic resource. Iron, silver and gold are abiotic.","e":"Petroleum is biotic for the same reason.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0530","q":"Which among the following is not an exhaustible resource?","o":["Natural gas","Petroleum","Biogas","Coal"],"a":2,"x":"Biogas is renewable because it is made continuously from cattle dung and organic waste. Natural gas, petroleum and coal are exhaustible fossil fuels.","e":"Biogas is mostly methane.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0611","q":"Which among the following is the first thermal power plant of National Thermal Power Corporation Limited?","o":["Talcher Super Thermal Power Station","Sikka Thermal Power Plant","Rihand Thermal Power Plant","Singrauli Super Thermal Power Plant"],"a":3,"x":"Singrauli Super Thermal Power Station in Uttar Pradesh was NTPC's first power project (first unit 1982).","e":"NTPC was set up in 1975; it is a Maharatna company.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0650","q":"Which among the following is an example of non-conventional source of energy?","o":["Oil","Coal","Bioenergy","Natural Gas"],"a":2,"x":"Bioenergy (from biomass, biogas, biofuels) is a non-conventional, renewable source. Oil, coal and natural gas are conventional fossil fuels.","e":"Other non-conventional sources: solar, wind, tidal, geothermal.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0737","q":"As of April 2020, which state produces the maximum quantity of coffee in India?","o":["Karnataka","Odisha","Tamil Nadu","Kerala"],"a":0,"x":"Karnataka produces about 70% of India's coffee, mainly in Kodagu, Chikkamagaluru and Hassan.","e":"Coffee first came to India at Baba Budan Giri in Chikkamagaluru.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6785","q":"The Srisailam Dam is built across which of the following rivers?","o":["Krishna","Godavari","Mahanadi","Tapti"],"a":0,"x":"The Srisailam Dam is on the Krishna river, on the Andhra Pradesh–Telangana border.","e":"","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q1752","q":"Which of the following is an example of Non-Metallic mineral? I. Limestone II. Mica III. Gypsum","o":["I and II","II and III","I and III","I, II and III"],"a":3,"x":"Limestone, mica and gypsum are all non-metallic minerals; iron ore, bauxite and copper are metallic.","e":"Mica: Jharkhand, Andhra Pradesh; gypsum: Rajasthan.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1753","q":"Which of the following energy is renewable? I. Solar Energy II. Tidal Energy","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Solar and tidal energy are both renewable because they are naturally replenished; coal, oil and gas are non-renewable.","e":"Gulf of Khambhat and Kutch have tidal energy potential.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1836","q":"Which of the following is/are examples of Metallic minerals? I. Iron Ore II. Bauxite","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Iron ore and bauxite (aluminium ore) are both metallic minerals.","e":"Metallic: iron, bauxite, copper, manganese; non-metallic: mica, limestone, gypsum.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q1838","q":"Which of the following country of Asia has large iron ore deposits? I. India II. Bangladesh","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":0,"x":"India has large iron ore deposits (Odisha, Chhattisgarh, Karnataka, Jharkhand); Bangladesh has almost none, so only I is correct.","e":"Bailadila (Chhattisgarh) is a major iron ore mine.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q1948","q":"Cotton requires ______ temperatures and ______ rainfall.","o":["low; heavy","low; light","high; light","high; heavy"],"a":2,"x":"Cotton needs high temperature (21–30°C), light rainfall (50–100 cm), 210 frost-free days and bright sunshine; black soil suits it best.","e":"Gujarat and Maharashtra lead in cotton.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2033","q":"Nomadic farming is practiced in some parts of India like ______. I. Rajasthan II. Jammu and Kashmir","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Nomadic herding is practised in the dry parts of Rajasthan and in Jammu and Kashmir (e.g. the Gujjar Bakarwals), where herders move with their animals.","e":"Nomadic herding: moving with animals for pasture.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2077","q":"The Northern plains of India are made up of which soil?","o":["Alluvial","Red","Black","Laterite"],"a":0,"x":"The Northern Plains are made of alluvial soil laid down by the Indus, Ganga and Brahmaputra; they are India's most fertile farmland.","e":"Alluvial soil covers about 40% of India.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2403","q":"Saline soils are also known as _________ .","o":["Regur soil","Cotton soil","Tamak soil","Usara Soil"],"a":3,"x":"Saline soils are also called usara soils; they have too much sodium and magnesium salts, and gypsum is used to reclaim them.","e":"Found in western Gujarat, the Sundarbans and Punjab–Haryana.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2416","q":"Which city of India will get the country's first underwater Metro?","o":["Kochi","Kolkata","Mumbai","Chennai"],"a":1,"x":"Kolkata has India's first underwater metro, a tunnel under the Hooghly river on the East–West Metro line, opened in 2024.","e":"Kolkata also had India's first metro (1984).","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2531","q":"Which type of soil is not suitable for natural cultivation?","o":["Laterite Soil","Black soil","Alluvial Soil","Red Soil"],"a":0,"x":"Laterite soil is heavily leached, acidic and poor in nutrients, so it is least suited to cultivation without heavy manuring; it suits tea, coffee and cashew.","e":"Laterite = 'brick' soil.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2602","q":"Which of the following is a rabi crop? I. Wheat II. Rice","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":0,"x":"Wheat is a rabi crop, sown in winter (Oct–Dec) and harvested in spring; rice is a kharif crop.","e":"Rabi: wheat, gram, mustard; kharif: rice, maize, cotton.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2604","q":"Which of the following is a copper producing area of India? I. Khetri II. Balaghat","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Khetri (Rajasthan) and Balaghat's Malanjkhand (Madhya Pradesh) are both major copper-producing areas.","e":"Singhbhum (Jharkhand) also has copper.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2675","q":"Which of the following statement(s) is/are correct regarding rice? I. Grows best in alluvial clayey soil. II. It is a staple diet in tropical regions.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Rice grows best in alluvial clayey soil that holds water, and it is a staple food in tropical regions.","e":"Rice needs high temperature and over 100 cm rain.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2875","q":"Which of the following is/are coal producing area(s) in India? I. Raniganj II. Jharia III. Bokaro","o":["I and II","II and III","I and III","I, II and III"],"a":3,"x":"Raniganj (West Bengal), Jharia and Bokaro (Jharkhand) are major coalfields in the Damodar valley.","e":"Jharia has India's largest coking coal reserves.","src":["Junior Clerk 17 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2913","q":"The first tidal energy station was built in ______.","o":["India","China","France","Italy"],"a":2,"x":"The world's first tidal power station was built at La Rance, France, in 1966.","e":"India's tidal potential: Gulf of Khambhat, Gulf of Kutch, Sundarbans.","src":["Junior Clerk 17 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2914","q":"In which type of soil, wheat thrives the best? I. Alluvial clayey soil II. Well drained loamy soil","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":1,"x":"Wheat grows best in well-drained loamy soil with moderate temperature and rainfall (NCERT Class 8).","e":"Rice: clayey soil with standing water.","src":["Junior Clerk 17 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6110","q":"Which of the following is a fibre crop? I. Jute II. Cotton","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Jute and cotton are both fibre crops.","e":"","src":["Junior Clerk 9 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q6113","q":"_______________ was the first country in the world to develop Hydroelectricity.","o":["Norway","Scotland","France","Japan"],"a":0,"x":"According to NCERT Class 8, Norway was the first country in the world to develop hydroelectricity.","e":"","src":["Junior Clerk 9 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q6153","q":"Which of the following is a producer of hydel power in India? I. Bhakra Nagal II. Nagarjun Sagar","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Bhakra Nangal (Satluj) and Nagarjuna Sagar (Krishna) are both multipurpose projects that produce hydroelectricity.","e":"","src":["Junior Clerk 9 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q3873","q":"Which among the following is an example of zaid crops?","o":["Millets","Rice","Barley","Cucumber"],"a":3,"x":"Zaid crops grow in the short summer season between rabi and kharif, e.g. cucumber, watermelon, muskmelon and fodder crops.","e":"","src":["Stenographer 8 Aug 2021","Store Keeper 22 Apr 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q4048","q":"The Kadra Dam of Karnataka is built across which of the following rivers?","o":["Krishna","Kali","Kaveri","Periyar"],"a":1,"x":"The Kadra Dam is on the Kali river in Uttara Kannada district, Karnataka.","e":"","src":["Stenographer 23 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q4051","q":"Which among the following soils is formed due to leaching by heavy rains and destruction of humus by bacteria?","o":["Arid","Laterite","Alluvial","Black"],"a":1,"x":"Laterite soil forms where heavy rain leaches away nutrients and high heat lets bacteria destroy humus (NCERT Class 10).","e":"","src":["Stenographer 23 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q4103","q":"Which among the following is an example of kharif crop?","o":["Maize","Wheat","Watermelon","Almond"],"a":0,"x":"Maize is a kharif crop; wheat is rabi and watermelon is zaid.","e":"","src":["Stenographer 23 Apr 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q4889","q":"Port of Kandla is also known as ______.","o":["Syama Prasad Mookerjee Port Trust","Deendayal Port Trust","Jawaharlal Nehru Port Trust","Sardar Port Trust"],"a":1,"x":"Kandla Port in Gujarat was renamed Deendayal Port in 2017.","e":"","src":["Stenographer 26 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q5015","q":"In which of the following states is the Salem Steel Plant located?","o":["Andhra Pradesh","Karnataka","Tamil Nadu","Kerala"],"a":2,"x":"Salem Steel Plant (SAIL) is in Salem, Tamil Nadu.","e":"","src":["Stenographer 26 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6865","q":"Chamera Dam is built across the __________ river.","o":["Ravi","Chenab","Saraswati","Krishna"],"a":0,"x":"The Chamera Dam is on the Ravi river in Chamba, Himachal Pradesh.","e":"","src":["Stenographer 14 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q6866","q":"Which of the following soils requires least tilling because of its characteristic of self-ploughing?","o":["Black","Alluvial","Laterite","Arid"],"a":0,"x":"Black soil develops deep cracks in dry weather, which lets air in; this 'self-ploughing' means it needs little tilling (NCERT).","e":"","src":["Stenographer 14 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q6913","q":"____________ soils are widely used for making bricks for use in house construction.","o":["Black","Laterite","Arid","Alluvial"],"a":1,"x":"Laterite soil hardens on exposure to air and is widely used to make bricks.","e":"","src":["Stenographer 14 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6914","q":"Bargi Dam in Madhya Pradesh is built across the _________ river.","o":["Sone","Barna","Narmada","Chambal"],"a":2,"x":"The Bargi Dam is on the Narmada near Jabalpur, Madhya Pradesh.","e":"","src":["Stenographer 14 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6957","q":"Khadar and Bhangar are types of which of the following soil?","o":["Black","Alluvial","Laterite","Saline"],"a":1,"x":"Khadar (new) and Bhangar (old) are two types of alluvial soil.","e":"","src":["Stenographer 8 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q7004","q":"Which soil is formed by weathering old crystalline or metamorphic rocks?","o":["Alluvial","Arid","Red","Black"],"a":2,"x":"Red soil forms on old crystalline and metamorphic rocks; its colour comes from iron (NCERT Class 10).","e":"","src":["Stenographer 14 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q5178","q":"In which of the following states is the Rewa Solar Project located?","o":["Madhya Pradesh","Andhra Pradesh","Uttar Pradesh","Arunachal Pradesh"],"a":0,"x":"The Rewa Ultra Mega Solar project is in Rewa district, Madhya Pradesh.","e":"","src":["Personal Assistant 27 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q5401","q":"Tarapur Nuclear Reactor is located in which of the following states?","o":["Rajasthan","Maharashtra","Tamil Nādu","Odisha"],"a":1,"x":"Tarapur Atomic Power Station, India's first nuclear power plant (1969), is in Palghar, Maharashtra.","e":"","src":["Personal Assistant 29 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q5435","q":"Bombay High (now Mumbai High) field is an offshore oilfield located in the_____","o":["Arabian Sea","Indian Ocean","Bay of Bengal","South China Sea"],"a":0,"x":"Mumbai High, India's largest offshore oilfield, is in the Arabian Sea off Mumbai.","e":"","src":["Personal Assistant 29 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q9496","q":"Which of the following nuclear power plants is located in Tamil Nadu?","o":["Tarapur","Kota","Kalpakkam","Narora"],"a":2,"x":"The Kalpakkam nuclear power plant is in Tamil Nadu; Tarapur is in Maharashtra, Kota (Rawatbhata) in Rajasthan and Narora in UP.","e":"","src":["Assistant Superintendent 14 Jul 2024 S1"]},{"id":"q4461","q":"In which of the following states the primitive form of cultivation is called 'Bewar' or Dahiya?","o":["Andhra Pradesh","Madhya Pradesh","Odisha","Jharkhand"],"a":1,"x":"In Madhya Pradesh shifting cultivation is called 'bewar' or 'dahiya' (NCERT Class 10).","e":"Jhumming – north-east; Podu – Andhra Pradesh.","src":["Office Superintendent 24 Oct 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q8875","q":"Which among the following hydroelectric projects is built across the Periyar River?","o":["Koyna","Tehri","Sardar Sarovar","Idukki"],"a":3,"x":"The Idukki arch dam is on the Periyar river in Kerala.","e":"","src":["MTS 2 Mar 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q8886","q":"Which of the following is NOT an example of a Rabi crop?","o":["Linseed","Peas","Sugarcane","Mustard"],"a":2,"x":"Sugarcane is not a rabi crop; linseed, peas and mustard are rabi crops.","e":"","src":["MTS 2 Mar 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q8934","q":"Which of the following is a major factor influencing the location of an iron and steel plant?","o":["Availability of luxury goods market","High population density","Presence of educational institutions","Proximity to iron ore reserves"],"a":3,"x":"Iron and steel plants are usually located near sources of iron ore and coal.","e":"","src":["MTS 3 Mar 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q8996","q":"Which of the following is NOT a characteristic of Intensive Subsistence Farming?","o":["Heavy reliance on manual labour","Small landholdings and high population pressure","Dependence on irrigation and fertilisers","Extensive use of advanced machinery"],"a":3,"x":"Intensive subsistence farming uses simple tools and a lot of labour on small plots, not advanced machinery (NCERT Class 8).","e":"","src":["MTS 3 Mar 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q9011","q":"The Pavagada Solar Park is located in which state?","o":["Andhra Pradesh","Maharashtra","Karnataka","Telangana"],"a":2,"x":"Pavagada Solar Park is in Tumakuru district, Karnataka.","e":"","src":["MTS 3 Mar 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q9039","q":"In which type of cropping pattern is the practice of planting two or more crops sequentially on the same plot of land followed in India?","o":["Commercial farming","Crop rotation","Mixed cropping","Intercropping"],"a":1,"x":"Growing different crops one after another on the same land is crop rotation.","e":"","src":["MTS 5 Mar 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q9051","q":"Which of the following farming systems is scientifically managed and highly capital- intensive?","o":["Primitive subsistence","Shifting cultivation","Plantation farming","Subsistence dry farming"],"a":2,"x":"Plantation farming is scientifically managed and highly capital-intensive, e.g. tea, coffee and rubber.","e":"","src":["MTS 5 Mar 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q9070","q":"The 1,200-MW Kalai-II hydropower project on the Lohit River is located in which Indian state?","o":["Assam","Arunachal Pradesh","Sikkim","Himachal Pradesh"],"a":1,"x":"The Kalai-II hydropower project on the Lohit river is in Arunachal Pradesh.","e":"","src":["MTS 6 Mar 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q9077","q":"Which among the following hydroelectric projects is located in Madhya Pradesh?","o":["Idukki","Bhakra Nangal","Indira Sagar","Rihand"],"a":2,"x":"The Indira Sagar project on the Narmada is in Madhya Pradesh.","e":"","src":["MTS 6 Mar 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q9124","q":"Which of the following hydroelectric projects is NOT built on the Sutlej River?","o":["Nathpa Jhakri Hydroelectric Project","Bhakra Nangal Hydroelectric Project","Karcham Wangtoo Hydroelectric Project","Nagarjuna Sagar Hydroelectric Project"],"a":3,"x":"Nagarjuna Sagar is on the Krishna; Nathpa Jhakri, Bhakra Nangal and Karcham Wangtoo are on the Sutlej.","e":"","src":["MTS 6 Mar 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q9147","q":"The Kudankulam Nuclear Power Plant, India’s largest nuclear power plant, is located in which state?","o":["Tamil Nadu","Kerala","Andhra Pradesh","Karnataka"],"a":0,"x":"The Kudankulam Nuclear Power Plant, India's largest, is in Tirunelveli district, Tamil Nadu.","e":"","src":["MTS 6 Mar 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q9187","q":"Which type of agriculture is also called ‘slash and burn’ farming?","o":["Shifting cultivation","Nomadic herding","Mixed farming","Plantation farming"],"a":0,"x":"Shifting cultivation is also called 'slash and burn' farming.","e":"","src":["MTS 8 Mar 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q9220","q":"Which of the following Indian states is NOT a partner in the Konkan Railway?","o":["Telangana","Karnataka","Maharashtra","Goa"],"a":0,"x":"The Konkan Railway partner states are Maharashtra, Goa, Karnataka and Kerala; Telangana is not one.","e":"","src":["MTS 8 Mar 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q9224","q":"In India, what is the practice called when two or more crops are grown together at the same time without any specific row arrangement?","o":["Mixed intercropping","Strip intercropping","Row intercropping","Relay intercropping"],"a":0,"x":"Growing two or more crops together with no fixed row pattern is mixed intercropping.","e":"","src":["MTS 8 Mar 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q9240","q":"Which Indian state is known as the 'Granary of India' due to its high agricultural productivity?","o":["Punjab","Maharashtra","Bihar","Gujarat"],"a":0,"x":"Punjab is called the 'Granary of India' for its high wheat and rice output.","e":"","src":["MTS 8 Mar 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q9262","q":"Which of the following hydroelectric projects is built on the Bhagirathi River?","o":["Tehri","Indira Sagar","Srisailam","Nathpa Jhakri"],"a":0,"x":"The Tehri Dam is on the Bhagirathi river in Uttarakhand.","e":"","src":["MTS 8 Mar 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q9349","q":"The jute industry is concentrated in which of the following states of India?","o":["Gujarat, Rajasthan, and Karnataka","Madhya Pradesh, Tamil Nadu, and Kerala","Uttar Pradesh, Rajasthan, and Karnataka","West Bengal, Assam, and Bihar"],"a":3,"x":"India's jute industry is concentrated in West Bengal, Assam and Bihar, mainly along the Hugli.","e":"","src":["MTS 25 Feb 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q9435","q":"Black soils of the Deccan Plateau are best for growing ________.","o":["Rice","Saffron","Cotton","Tea"],"a":2,"x":"The black soils of the Deccan are best suited to cotton, so they are called black cotton soil.","e":"","src":["MTS 26 Feb 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q3869","q":"The Peechi Dam, built across Manali river, is located in which of the following states?","o":["Uttar Pradesh","Karnataka","Maharashtra","Kerala"],"a":3,"x":"The Peechi Dam is on the Manali river in Thrissur district, Kerala.","e":"","src":["Store Keeper 22 Apr 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q3872","q":"Which among the following is the most productive (fertile) category of soil?","o":["Arid","Alluvial","Laterite","Red"],"a":1,"x":"Alluvial soil is the most fertile and productive soil in India, covering the Northern Plains and river deltas.","e":"","src":["Store Keeper 22 Apr 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q3640","q":"On which of the following river, the Sardar Sarovar Project has been constructed?","o":["Vaitarna","Saraswati","Sabarmati","Narmada"],"a":3,"x":"The Sardar Sarovar Project is a dam on the Narmada in Gujarat.","e":"","src":["Fire Operator 20 Apr 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q4015","q":"Bajra grows best in which soil?","o":["Black soil","Alluvial soil","Laterite soil","Red soil"],"a":0,"x":"Bajra grows well on sandy soils and shallow black soil (NCERT Class 10).","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 22 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q4548","q":"Black soil is found mostly in the:","o":["Deccan region","Himalayan region","Gangetic plain","Eastern Ghats"],"a":0,"x":"Black (regur) soil is found mostly on the Deccan plateau, formed from lava rocks.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 25 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q4624","q":"Which soil after adopting appropriate soil conservation techniques is highly suitable for growing tea and coffee?","o":["Forest Soil","Alluvial Soil","Laterite Soil","Arid Soil"],"a":2,"x":"Laterite soils, with proper conservation, are very useful for growing tea and coffee (NCERT Class 10).","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 25 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q4081","q":"Pandoh Dam is built across the ________ river.","o":["Sutlej","Ravi","Beas","Chenab"],"a":2,"x":"The Pandoh Dam is on the Beas river in Mandi district, Himachal Pradesh.","e":"","src":["Jr. Telephone Operator 23 Apr 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q4082","q":"Which among the following statements is not true about Alluvial Soil?","o":["The colour of the alluvial soils varies from the light grey to ash grey.","They are generally rich in phosphorous but poor in potash.","Bhangar represents a system of older alluvium, deposited away from the flood plains.","Khadar is the new alluvium and is deposited by floods annually."],"a":1,"x":"Alluvial soils are generally rich in potash but poor in phosphorus, so statement B is the wrong one (NCERT Class 10).","e":"Bhangar = old alluvium; Khadar = new alluvium.","src":["Jr. Telephone Operator 23 Apr 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5481","q":"Which of the following statements regarding Black soil are correct? (a) Black soil is also known cotton soil and Regursoil. (b) The soil is rich in iron, lime, calcium, potash, magnesium (c) The soil has high water retaining capacity. (d) The pH of black soil is 9 - 10.","o":["(a), (b) and (d)","(c), (b) and (d)","(a), (b) and (c)","(a), (c) and (d)"],"a":2,"x":"Black (regur, cotton) soil is rich in lime, iron, magnesium and potash and holds moisture well; its pH is not 9–10.","e":"","src":["Accountant 29 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q4418","q":"The 'Kiriburu' mines is famous for ______mineral in India.","o":["Iron ore","Coal","Manganese","Copper"],"a":0,"x":"The Kiriburu mines are known for iron ore.","e":"","src":["Hindi Translator-cum-Assisstant 24 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0903","q":"Match List I (Industry) with List II (Location): A. Visvesvaraya Iron and Steel Plant, B. Indian Aluminium Company Ltd., C. Fertiliser Corporation of India Ltd., D. Hindustan Antibiotics Ltd. — I. Hirakud, II. Sindri, III. Pimpri, IV. Bhadravati.","o":["A - IV, B - I, C - II, D - III","A - II, B - IV, C - I, D - III","A - IV, B - II, C - I, D - III","A - I, B - II, C - III, D - IV"],"a":0,"x":"Visvesvaraya Iron and Steel Plant – Bhadravati (Karnataka); Indian Aluminium Company – Hirakud (Odisha); Fertiliser Corporation of India – Sindri (Jharkhand); Hindustan Antibiotics – Pimpri (Pune).","e":"Sindri was India's first large fertiliser plant (1951).","src":["PRT 17 Feb 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q0921","q":"On the map of India, where would the Korba Thermal Power Plant be located?","o":["Chhattisgarh","Maharashtra","Jharkhand","Odisha"],"a":0,"x":"The Korba Super Thermal Power Station (NTPC) is in Chhattisgarh's Korba coalfield, which is why Korba is called the 'power capital' of India.","e":"Coal-rich Chhattisgarh: Korba, Hasdeo-Arand.","src":["PRT 18 Feb 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q0922","q":"On the map of India, where is the Korba Thermal Power Plant located?","o":["Chhattisgarh","Maharashtra","Jharkhand","Odisha"],"a":0,"x":"The Korba super thermal power plant (NTPC) is in Korba district, Chhattisgarh, on the Hasdeo river near large coalfields.","e":"Korba is called the 'power capital of India'.","src":["PRT 18 Feb 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q0932","q":"Which of the following is NOT a major iron ore belt of India?","o":["Singhbhum belt","Ratnagiri–Mumbai","Ballari–Chitradurga","Durg–Bastar–Chandrapur"],"a":1,"x":"NCERT's major iron ore belts: Odisha–Jharkhand (Singhbhum), Durg–Bastar–Chandrapur, Ballari–Chitradurga–Chikkamagaluru–Tumakuru, and Maharashtra–Goa. 'Ratnagiri–Mumbai' is not one of them.","e":"Kudremukh (Karnataka) was a famous iron ore export mine.","src":["PRT 18 Feb 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q0946","q":"According to the Coffee Board of India, which country was the largest importer of Indian coffee in 2020–21?","o":["Russian Federation","Poland","Italy","Germany"],"a":2,"x":"Italy has long been the largest importer of Indian coffee, followed by Germany and Russia (Coffee Board of India).","e":"Karnataka produces about 70% of India's coffee.","src":["PRT 18 Feb 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q0956","q":"According to the Indian Minerals Yearbook 2023, which Indian state is the largest producer of bauxite?","o":["Gujarat","Madhya Pradesh","Odisha","Jharkhand"],"a":2,"x":"Odisha is India's largest bauxite producer (Panchpatmali in Koraput), followed by Jharkhand and Gujarat (Indian Minerals Yearbook).","e":"NALCO's plant is at Damanjodi, Odisha.","src":["PRT 18 Feb 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1001","q":"Which of the following states has the largest wind farm cluster in India?","o":["Gujarat","Tamil Nadu","Maharashtra","Kerala"],"a":1,"x":"Tamil Nadu has India's largest wind farm cluster at Muppandal near Kanyakumari.","e":"Gujarat and Tamil Nadu lead in wind capacity.","src":["PRT 19 Feb 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1002","q":"Which of the following belts in India is rich in copper and zinc minerals associated mainly with the Dharwar system of rocks?","o":["Himalayan Belt","North-Western Region (Aravali Range)","South-Western Plateau Region (Sahyadri Range)","North-Eastern Plateau Region"],"a":1,"x":"The North-Western region along the Aravallis (Rajasthan, Gujarat) is rich in copper and zinc minerals linked to Dharwar-system rocks, e.g. Khetri (copper) and Zawar (zinc).","e":"Rajasthan leads in zinc and lead.","src":["PRT 19 Feb 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1064","q":"Identify the correct pair (railway zone and headquarters).","o":["East Central Railway zone – Gorakhpur","Central Railway zone – Mumbai CST","North Central Railway zone – Hajipur","South Central Railway zone – Chennai"],"a":1,"x":"Central Railway is headquartered at Mumbai CST. East Central is at Hajipur, North Central at Prayagraj and South Central at Secunderabad.","e":"North Eastern Railway is at Gorakhpur.","src":["PRT 20 Feb 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1092","q":"Which port in India has been developed to relieve pressure on Mumbai port?","o":["Jawaharlal Nehru Port","Visakhapatnam Port","Marmagao Port","Haldia Port"],"a":0,"x":"Jawaharlal Nehru Port (Nhava Sheva, 1989) was built to relieve congestion at Mumbai port; it is India's largest container port.","e":"Kandla was similarly built to make up for losing Karachi.","src":["PRT 23 Feb 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1110","q":"According to the Indian Minerals Yearbook 2022, which state is the leading producer of bauxite in India?","o":["Jharkhand","Odisha","Karnataka","Maharashtra"],"a":1,"x":"Odisha is India's leading bauxite producer (Koraput, Kalahandi, Rayagada); bauxite is the ore of aluminium.","e":"NALCO's plants are at Damanjodi and Angul, Odisha.","src":["PRT 23 Feb 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q1140","q":"Which transport system in India is best suited for the movement of perishable goods and urgent cargo?","o":["Railways","Airways","Inland waterways","Roadways"],"a":1,"x":"Air transport is fastest, so it suits perishable goods (flowers, fruit, medicines) and urgent, high-value cargo despite its cost.","e":"Railways and waterways suit bulky, heavy cargo.","src":["PRT 26 Mar 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1155","q":"Iron and steel industries are mainly located near:","o":["Raw material sources","Agricultural zones","Ports","Tourist places"],"a":0,"x":"Iron and steel plants need heavy raw materials (iron ore, coal, limestone), so they are built near raw-material sources, e.g. the Chotanagpur belt: Jamshedpur, Bokaro, Bhilai, Rourkela.","e":"Weight-losing raw material → industry near the mines.","src":["PRT 26 Mar 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q1215","q":"Which industrial region of India is known for a concentration of heavy industries?","o":["Delhi–Gurgaon","Bengaluru–Tamil Nadu","Mumbai–Pune","Chota Nagpur Plateau"],"a":3,"x":"The Chota Nagpur Plateau (Jharkhand, West Bengal, Odisha) has heavy industry — iron and steel at Jamshedpur, Bokaro and Durgapur — because coal and iron ore are close by.","e":"It is called the 'Ruhr of India'.","src":["PRT 31 Mar 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q9800","q":"______ is the largest producer of manganese ores in India.","o":["Rajasthan","Odisha","Jharkhand","Karnataka"],"a":1,"x":"Odisha is India's largest producer of manganese ore.","e":"","src":["PRT 7 Mar 2022 S1"]},{"id":"q3766","q":"Which Indian state has the highest concentration of ‘bauxite’ as per report of Indian bureau of mines?","o":["Maharashtra","Odisha","Jharkhand","Chhattisgarh"],"a":1,"x":"Odisha has the largest bauxite reserves and output in India.","e":"","src":["Nursery Teacher 21 Oct 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q3794","q":"Which town has the oldest Oil refinery in India?","o":["Digboi-Assam","Mathura-Uttar Pradesh","Bina-Madhya Pradesh","Haldia-West Bengal"],"a":0,"x":"Digboi in Assam has India's oldest oil refinery (1901).","e":"","src":["Nursery Teacher 21 Oct 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q4505","q":"Which of the following is the largest completed hydroelectric power plant by capacity in India?","o":["Bhakra Nangal Dam","Koyna Hydroelectric Project","Srisailam Dam","Sardar Sarovar Dam"],"a":1,"x":"The Koyna project in Maharashtra is usually cited as India's largest completed hydroelectric power plant.","e":"","src":["Nursery Teacher 24 Oct 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q4506","q":"Urvara and Usara are ancient Indian broad classifications of ________.","o":["farming methods","cropping season","crops","soil"],"a":3,"x":"Urvara (fertile) and Usara (sterile) were ancient Indian classes of soil.","e":"","src":["Nursery Teacher 24 Oct 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q4508","q":"In which of the following areas of India are you most likely to find podzol soil?","o":["Rajasthan","Telangana","Jammu and Kashmir","Bihar"],"a":2,"x":"Podzol soils form under coniferous forests in cold, wet highlands such as Jammu and Kashmir.","e":"","src":["Nursery Teacher 24 Oct 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q7836","q":"Kakrapar Atomic Power Station is located in which of the following states of India?","o":["Maharashtra","Rajasthan","Gujarat","West Bengal"],"a":2,"x":"The Kakrapar Atomic Power Station is in Surat district, Gujarat.","e":"","src":["Nursery Teacher 24 Jun 2023 S1"]},{"id":"q1436","q":"Which state has major reserves of low-grade brown coal called lignite, which is used for power generation?","o":["Gujarat","West Bengal","Odisha","Tamil Nadu"],"a":3,"x":"Tamil Nadu has India's largest lignite (brown coal) reserves, at Neyveli, used for power generation.","e":"Lignite is also mined in Gujarat and Rajasthan.","src":["TGT 25 Jun 2023 S2"]},{"id":"q1520","q":"Which of the following crops requires a hot and humid climate in the Indian climatic condition, with an average temperature of 21°C to 27°C?","o":["Wheat","Sugarcane","Gram","Mustard"],"a":1,"x":"Sugarcane needs a hot, humid climate (21–27°C) and 75–100 cm of rain; Uttar Pradesh is the largest producer.","e":"Wheat, gram and mustard are cool-season rabi crops.","src":["TGT 24 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q2331","q":"Which of the following pair of soil – state is correct? I. Laterite soil – Kerala II. Black soil – Madhya Pradesh","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Laterite soil is found in Kerala and black soil in Madhya Pradesh, so both pairs are correct.","e":"","src":["TGT 14 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q3542","q":"Cultivation of grapes is called ______.","o":["Sericulture","Horticulture","Viticulture","Mariculture"],"a":2,"x":"Growing grapes is called viticulture.","e":"Sericulture – silkworms.","src":["TGT 1 Oct 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q3573","q":"Which of the following statement is correct? I. Wheat requires moderate temperature and rainfall during growing season. II. Jute is also known as the ‘Golden Fibre’.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Wheat needs moderate temperature and rainfall while growing, and jute is called the 'golden fibre'.","e":"","src":["TGT 1 Oct 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q4703","q":"In which of the following state India's first jute mill was established?","o":["West Bengal","Madhya Pradesh","Himachal Pradesh","Rajasthan"],"a":0,"x":"India's first jute mill was set up at Rishra, West Bengal, in 1855.","e":"","src":["TGT 25 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q4704","q":"Which of the following is a Ferrous Mineral?","o":["Copper","Bauxite","Manganese","Lead"],"a":2,"x":"Manganese is a ferrous mineral; copper, bauxite and lead are non-ferrous.","e":"","src":["TGT 25 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q5204","q":"The finest quality of iron ore is ______.","o":["Siderite","Hematite","Magnetite","Limonite"],"a":2,"x":"Magnetite is the finest iron ore (about 70% iron); hematite is the most widely used.","e":"","src":["TGT 27 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q5710","q":"Which of the following states is the highest producer of sugarcane in the country?","o":["Punjab","Karnataka","Uttar Pradesh","Maharashtra"],"a":2,"x":"Uttar Pradesh is India's largest sugarcane producer.","e":"","src":["TGT 4 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5841","q":"India is the largest global producer of which of the following products?","o":["Milk","Sugarcane","Fish","Rice"],"a":0,"x":"India is the world's largest producer of milk.","e":"","src":["TGT 5 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q5881","q":"In which type of farming, a single crop is grown on a large area?","o":["Primitive subsistence farming","Intensive subsistence farming","Plantation farming","Horticulture"],"a":2,"x":"In plantation farming a single crop, such as tea or rubber, is grown on a large area.","e":"","src":["TGT 6 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5950","q":"Which of the following is a rabi crop?","o":["Cotton","Jowar","Wheat","Chilly"],"a":2,"x":"Wheat is a rabi crop; cotton and jowar are kharif crops.","e":"","src":["TGT 7 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5957","q":"Where is the Somasila Dam is located ?","o":["Himachal Pradesh","Uttar Pradesh","Andhra Pradesh","Madhya Pradesh"],"a":2,"x":"The Somasila Dam is on the Penna river in Andhra Pradesh.","e":"","src":["TGT 7 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5969","q":"In the manufacture of steel, Iron ore, coking coal and lime stone are required in the ratio of approximately ______.","o":["2:4:1","1:4:2","4:3:1","4:2:1"],"a":3,"x":"Steel making needs iron ore, coking coal and limestone in the ratio of about 4:2:1 (NCERT Class 10).","e":"","src":["TGT 7 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q6080","q":"Tamil Nadu, Andhra Pradesh and Kerala are better suited for growing cashew nuts because of the presence of __________ soil.","o":["Yellow","Laterite","Alluvial","Black"],"a":1,"x":"Laterite soil suits cashew nuts, which is why Tamil Nadu, Andhra Pradesh and Kerala grow them (NCERT Class 10).","e":"","src":["TGT 8 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q6081","q":"Red loamy soil can be found predominantly in which of the following regions?","o":["Piedmont zone of the Western Ghats","Eastern coastal plains","Deccan plateau","Foothills of the Himalayas"],"a":0,"x":"Red loamy soil occurs mainly in the piedmont zone of the Western Ghats.","e":"","src":["TGT 8 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q6243","q":"Which of the following statement is correct? I. Khadar soil is more fertile than the bangar soil. II. Entire northen plains are made of alluvial soil.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Khadar (new alluvium) is more fertile than bangar, and the Northern Plains are made of alluvial soil.","e":"","src":["TGT 9 Oct 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q6262","q":"In which method of soil conservation the bare ground between plants is covered with a layer of organic matter like straw?","o":["Contour barriers","Mulching","Terrace farming","Shelter belts"],"a":1,"x":"In mulching, bare ground between plants is covered with organic matter such as straw to hold soil moisture.","e":"","src":["TGT 9 Oct 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q7490","q":"Which of the following metal deposits are found in Asia? I. Manganese II. Bauxite III. Nickel","o":["I and II","II and III","I and III","I, II and III"],"a":3,"x":"Asia has deposits of manganese, bauxite and nickel.","e":"","src":["TGT 1 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q7532","q":"Rice needs ______ temperature and ______ humidity.","o":["low; high","high; high","high; low","low; low"],"a":1,"x":"Rice needs high temperature and high humidity.","e":"","src":["TGT 7 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q7579","q":"Which of the following is mica producing area in India? I. Gaya II. Hazaribagh III. Ajmer","o":["I and II","II and III","I and III","I, II and III"],"a":3,"x":"Gaya, Hazaribagh (the mica belt of Bihar–Jharkhand) and Ajmer are mica-producing areas.","e":"","src":["TGT 8 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q7963","q":"Which of the following is Asia's first mechanically drilled oil well where oil was found at 118 feet on 26 March 1867?","o":["Nahorkatia, Assam","Makum, Assam","Digboi, Assam","Badarpur, Assam"],"a":1,"x":"Asia's first mechanically drilled oil well was at Makum, Assam, where oil was struck in 1867.","e":"","src":["TGT 6 Jul 2023 S2"]},{"id":"q8002","q":"Shearing is the process of:","o":["removal of wool from a sheep","dyeing the silk","cultivation of cotton","rearing of silkworm"],"a":0,"x":"Shearing is the removal of wool from a sheep (NCERT Class 7).","e":"","src":["TGT 6 Jul 2023 S2"]},{"id":"q8055","q":"Blue revolution is related with:","o":["sericulture","floriculture","pisciculture","horticulture"],"a":2,"x":"The Blue Revolution relates to fisheries (pisciculture).","e":"","src":["TGT 6 Jul 2023 S2"]},{"id":"q3058","q":"The ‘Polavaram Project’ is constructed on which of the following rivers?","o":["Kaveri","Narmada","Krishna","Godavari"],"a":3,"x":"The Polavaram project is on the Godavari in Andhra Pradesh.","e":"","src":["Special Educator 17 Oct 2021","Special Educator 17 Oct 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q8814","q":"Which of the following agricultural practices is the most influenced by terrain and altitude?","o":["Tea plantations are suited for areas with level fields and standing water.","Apple orchards flourish below 1500 metres in elevation.","Paddy cultivation thrives in rolling terrains where water drains away.","Coconut orchards are typically found at low altitudes near the sea level."],"a":3,"x":"Coconut groves grow at low altitudes near the coast; tea needs well-drained slopes, not standing water.","e":"","src":["Special Educator 26 Jun 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q8840","q":"Which of the following irrigation methods is most suitable for row crops such as maize, sugarcane, and cotton?","o":["Border-strip irrigation","Drip irrigation","Basin irrigation","Furrow irrigation"],"a":3,"x":"Furrow irrigation suits row crops like maize, sugarcane and cotton.","e":"","src":["Special Educator 29 Jun 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0768","q":"In which state are the Kolihan copper mines located?","o":["Madhya Pradesh","Jharkhand","Rajasthan","Andhra Pradesh"],"a":2,"x":"The Kolihan copper mine is part of the Khetri copper belt in Jhunjhunu district, Rajasthan, run by Hindustan Copper Limited.","e":"Other copper areas: Malanjkhand (Madhya Pradesh), Singhbhum (Jharkhand).","src":["PGT 25 Sep 2025"]},{"id":"q0777","q":"Which of the following is a rabi crop of the northern states of India?","o":["Cotton","Maize","Gram","Rice"],"a":2,"x":"Gram (chickpea) is a rabi crop, sown in winter (October–December) and harvested in spring. Cotton, maize and rice are kharif crops.","e":"Other rabi crops: wheat, barley, peas, mustard.","src":["PGT 25 Sep 2025"]},{"id":"q0805","q":"What was the major objective behind the launch of National Highway Development Programme (NHDP) in India in the year 1998?","o":["To reduce rural-urban migration","To strengthen coastal protection","To boost economic development through road connectivity","To provide employment in the agricultural sector"],"a":2,"x":"The National Highways Development Programme (1998) was launched to boost economic growth through better road links, including the Golden Quadrilateral and the North-South and East-West corridors.","e":"The Golden Quadrilateral links Delhi, Mumbai, Chennai and Kolkata.","src":["PGT 22 Sep 2025"]},{"id":"q0811","q":"Which of the following states in India is a major producer of iron ore?","o":["Kerala","Uttarakhand","Punjab","Odisha"],"a":3,"x":"Odisha is India's largest producer of iron ore, from mines in Keonjhar, Sundargarh and Mayurbhanj.","e":"Karnataka and Chhattisgarh follow.","src":["PGT 22 Sep 2025"]},{"id":"q0824","q":"Which of the following term defines an association between national security and the availability of natural resources for energy consumption?","o":["Energy Security","Forest Security","Foreign Security","Mineral Security"],"a":0,"x":"Energy security is having reliable, affordable access to energy sources, which is tied to national security.","e":"India imports over 80% of its crude oil, which is why energy security matters.","src":["PGT 26 Sep 2025"]},{"id":"q0831","q":"Which is the largest (highest capacity) nuclear plant in India?","o":["Muppandal","Mundra","Kudankulam","Vindhyachal"],"a":2,"x":"Kudankulam in Tamil Nadu, built with Russia, is India's largest nuclear power plant by capacity.","e":"Tarapur (1969) was India's first nuclear power plant.","src":["PGT 26 Sep 2025"]},{"id":"q0843","q":"What percentage of the world’s water resources does India account for?","o":["4 per cent","6 per cent","3 per cent","9 per cent"],"a":0,"x":"India has about 2.45% of the world's land area and about 4% of its water resources, but about 18% of its population.","e":"Hence the pressure on water per person.","src":["PGT 14 Jul 2025"]},{"id":"q0872","q":"According to India's Ministry of Coal, which group of states has the largest coal reserves (as of April 2022)?","o":["West Bengal, Andhra Pradesh and Telangana","Odisha, Jharkhand and Chhattisgarh","West Bengal, Madhya Pradesh and Telangana","Maharashtra, Bihar and Andhra Pradesh"],"a":1,"x":"Odisha, Jharkhand and Chhattisgarh together hold about 70% of India's coal reserves; Jharkhand and Odisha have the largest shares.","e":"Most Indian coal is Gondwana coal from river valleys such as the Damodar and Mahanadi.","src":["PGT 24 Sep 2025"]},{"id":"q6477","q":"Which among the following is the longest canal of India?","o":["Ganges canal","Narmada Canal","Indira Gandhi canal","Anupgarh canal"],"a":2,"x":"The Indira Gandhi Canal in Rajasthan is India's longest canal.","e":"","src":["PGT 30 Jul 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6514","q":"Which among the following is a carbonate ore?","o":["Bauxite","Siderite","Galena","Fluorspar"],"a":1,"x":"Siderite (iron carbonate) is a carbonate ore; bauxite is an oxide, galena a sulphide and fluorspar a fluoride.","e":"","src":["PGT 30 Jul 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q7161","q":"The breeding, rearing, and transplantation of fish by artificial means is known as __________.","o":["Pisciculture","Pomiculture","Apiculture","Arboriculture"],"a":0,"x":"Breeding and rearing fish is called pisciculture.","e":"Apiculture – bees; pomiculture – fruit.","src":["PGT 31 Jul 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q7253","q":"The Alamatti dam is built over which of the following rivers?","o":["Krishna","Kaveri","Chambal","Ravi"],"a":0,"x":"The Almatti Dam is on the Krishna river in Karnataka.","e":"","src":["PGT 29 Jul 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q7276","q":"The water holding capacity is the highest in which of the following soils?","o":["Silt Soil","Chalk Soil","Clayey Soil","Peat Soil"],"a":2,"x":"Clayey soil has the highest water-holding capacity.","e":"","src":["PGT 10 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q7686","q":"Where was India's first cotton mill, known as the Fort Gloster Mill, established?","o":["Calcutta","Mumbai","Surat","Daman"],"a":0,"x":"India's first cotton mill, Fort Gloster Mill, was set up near Calcutta in 1818.","e":"","src":["PGT 31 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q8122","q":"When was the Rourkela Steel Plant set up?","o":["1958","1955","1959","1960"],"a":2,"x":"Rourkela Steel Plant in Odisha, built with German help, was set up in 1959.","e":"","src":["PGT 21 Jun 2023 S1"]},{"id":"q8382","q":"Identify the commercial source of energy.","o":["Firewood","Coal","Agricultural waste","Dried dung"],"a":1,"x":"Coal is a commercial energy source; firewood, dung and farm waste are non-commercial.","e":"","src":["PGT 5 Jul 2023 S1"]},{"id":"q8383","q":"Which type of soil is widely distributed over the Ganga basin?","o":["Red soil","Black soil","Alluvial soil","Laterite soil"],"a":2,"x":"Alluvial soil covers the Ganga basin and the northern plains.","e":"","src":["PGT 5 Jul 2023 S1"]},{"id":"q8412","q":"The National Highway Network is the responsibility of the __________.","o":["Special Roads Authority","Central and State Governments","State Government","Central Government"],"a":3,"x":"National Highways are the responsibility of the Central Government; state highways belong to the states.","e":"","src":["PGT 21 Jun 2023 S2"]},{"id":"q8468","q":"Which of the following is the largest centre in Uttar Pradesh for the cotton textile industry?","o":["Saharanpur","Kanpur","Meerut","Bareilly"],"a":1,"x":"Kanpur is Uttar Pradesh's largest cotton textile centre.","e":"","src":["PGT 22 Jun 2023 S2"]},{"id":"q9523","q":"Which among the following statements is incorrect?","o":["Cucumber is a Zaid crop.","Crops sown in the winter season (October to December) and harvested in the spring season (April-May) are called Rabi crops.","Maize is a Rabi crop.","A cash crop is cultivated to be sold in the market to earn profits from the sale."],"a":2,"x":"Maize is mainly a kharif crop, so calling it a rabi crop is wrong.","e":"","src":["PGT 30 Jun 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q9542","q":"__________ soil, locally called regur, is widely used for producing cotton, wheat, linseed, millets, tobacco & oilseeds.","o":["Black","Mountain","Laterite","Desert"],"a":0,"x":"Black soil, locally called regur, is best known for growing cotton.","e":"","src":["PGT 30 Jun 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q9565","q":"Which among the following statements is incorrect?","o":["Barley is a Rabi crop.","Wheat is a Rabi crop.","Bajra is a Kharif crop.","Mustard is a Kharif crop."],"a":3,"x":"Mustard is a rabi crop, not kharif; barley and wheat are rabi and bajra is kharif.","e":"","src":["PGT 28 Jun 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q9566","q":"India's first railway line which ran between Mumbai and Thane was opened in ______.","o":["1862","1853","1871","1868"],"a":1,"x":"India's first passenger train ran from Bombay (Bori Bunder) to Thane on 16 April 1853.","e":"","src":["PGT 28 Jun 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q9668","q":"Sonalika and Kalyan Sona, introduced in 1963, are High Yielding Variety (HYV) seeds of which of the following crops?","o":["wheat","maize","barley","rice"],"a":0,"x":"Sonalika and Kalyan Sona are high-yielding wheat varieties from the Green Revolution.","e":"","src":["PGT 29 Jun 2021"]},{"id":"q9868","q":"Which of the following river basins is known for having the richest coal deposits in India?","o":["Krishna Basin","Ganga Basin","Godavari Basin","Damodar Basin"],"a":3,"x":"The Damodar valley has India's richest coal deposits (Jharia, Raniganj, Bokaro).","e":"","src":["PGT 25 Sep 2025"]},{"id":"q9873","q":"Which is the longest inland waterway in India?","o":["National Waterway 2","National Waterway 1","National Waterway 4","National Waterway 3"],"a":1,"x":"National Waterway 1 (Ganga–Bhagirathi–Hooghly, Prayagraj to Haldia) is India's longest.","e":"","src":["PGT 25 Sep 2025"]},{"id":"q10037","q":"In which year in India air transport was launched between Allahabad and Naini?","o":["1911","1890","1903","1885"],"a":0,"x":"India's first air mail flight, from Allahabad to Naini, was in 1911.","e":"","src":["Junior Engineer (Civil) or Section Officer (Civil) 27 Jun 2022 S1"]},{"id":"q1916","q":"The ‘slash and burn’ agriculture in Assam is known as which of the following?","o":["Bewar","Dahiya","Jhumming","Khuruva"],"a":2,"x":"Slash-and-burn farming in Assam and the north-east is called jhumming.","e":"","src":["Scientific Assistant (Ballistics) 11 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2576","q":"India has around what percent of world’s renewable water resources?","o":["12%","14%","4%","7%"],"a":2,"x":"India has about 4% of the world's renewable water resources.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Medical Electronics) 16 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2648","q":"Topping the list of hydroelectric power plants in India is the Tehri Dam in _______ the highest hydroelectric power project in the country.","o":["Gujarat","Uttarakhand","Tamil Nadu","Maharashtra"],"a":1,"x":"The Tehri Dam, India's highest hydroelectric project, is in Uttarakhand.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Interior Design) 16 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q3179","q":"Which type of soil develops on crystalline igneous rocks in areas of low rainfall in the eastern and southern part of the Deccan Plateau?","o":["Alluvial soils","Arid soils","Red soils","Black soils"],"a":2,"x":"Red soil forms on crystalline igneous rocks in the low-rainfall eastern and southern Deccan.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Plastic) 18 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q3336","q":"Sardar Sarovar Dam has been built over ________ river.","o":["Narmada","Tapi","Brahmputra","Kaveri"],"a":0,"x":"The Sardar Sarovar Dam is on the Narmada in Gujarat.","e":"","src":["Senior Scientific Assistant (Biology) 18 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q4256","q":"In Odisha, the primitive form of cultivation is called:","o":["Milpa","Podu","Koman","Khil"],"a":2,"x":"Slash-and-burn farming is called Koman or Bringa in Odisha (NCERT).","e":"","src":["Workshop Instructor 24 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q4906","q":"Which of the following options contains the plantation crops only?","o":["Rice, Coffee, Rubber, Urad","Coffee, Rubber, Banana, Wheat","Cotton, Jute, Coffee, Rice","Tea, Coffee, Rubber, Banana"],"a":3,"x":"Tea, coffee, rubber and banana are plantation crops.","e":"","src":["Assistant Bacteriologist 26 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5056","q":"Which of the following is not a non-renewable type of resource?","o":["Hydropower","Coal","Petroleum","Natural resources"],"a":0,"x":"Hydropower is renewable; coal and petroleum are non-renewable.","e":"","src":["Scientific Assistant (Physics) 26 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q5122","q":"Black soil is also known as 'Black _____ Soil'.","o":["Maize","Cotton","Rice","Bajara"],"a":1,"x":"Black soil is also called black cotton soil.","e":"","src":["Assistant Engineer (Civil) 26 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q5123","q":"Baglihar Dam, built across Chenab river, is located in ____________.","o":["Jammu and Kashmir","Punjab","Uttarakhand","Himachal Pradesh"],"a":0,"x":"The Baglihar Dam on the Chenab is in Jammu and Kashmir.","e":"","src":["Assistant Engineer (Civil) 26 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q5231","q":"Which of the following is NOT a rabi crops?","o":["Barley","Peas","Mustard","Jowar"],"a":3,"x":"Jowar is mainly a kharif crop; barley, peas and mustard are rabi.","e":"","src":["Senior Scientific Assistant (Chemistry) 27 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5270","q":"Which of the following is NOT a method of soil conservation?","o":["Mulching","Terrace farming","Contour ploughing","Mitigation mechanism"],"a":3,"x":"Mulching, terrace farming and contour ploughing conserve soil; 'mitigation mechanism' is not a method.","e":"","src":["Senior Scientific Assistant (Physics) 27 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q5457","q":"The Supa dam is built across the river _____ in Karnataka.","o":["Sharavathy","Kalinadi","Hemavathy","Krishna"],"a":1,"x":"The Supa Dam in Karnataka is on the Kali (Kalinadi) river.","e":"","src":["Assistant Chemist 29 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q5617","q":"Which type of soil covers most of the Deccan Plateau?","o":["Black soils","Alluvial soils","Red and yellow soils","Laterite soils"],"a":0,"x":"Black soil covers most of the Deccan Plateau (the Deccan trap region).","e":"","src":["Labour Welfare Inspector 30 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5618","q":"Which of the following canal systems is not in Haryana?","o":["Bhakra Canal","Betwa Canal","Gurgaon Canal","Western Yamuna Canal"],"a":1,"x":"The Betwa Canal is in Uttar Pradesh–Madhya Pradesh; Bhakra, Gurgaon and Western Yamuna canals serve Haryana.","e":"","src":["Labour Welfare Inspector 30 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5621","q":"Which of the following crops are Zaid crops?","o":["Rice and Maize","Wheat and Barley","Cotton and Jute","Watermelon and Cucumber"],"a":3,"x":"Watermelon and cucumber are zaid (summer) crops.","e":"","src":["Labour Welfare Inspector 30 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6281","q":"Which of the following years the National Highways Authority of India (NHAI) was operationalised in India","o":["1985","1960","1995","1975"],"a":2,"x":"The National Highways Authority of India became operational in 1995.","e":"","src":["Section Officer (Horticulture) 9 Oct 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q7404","q":"In ______, rows of trees are planted to check the wind movement to protect soil cover.","o":["Shelter belts","Contour ploughing","Intercropping","Contour barriers"],"a":0,"x":"Shelter belts are rows of trees planted to check wind and protect soil.","e":"","src":["Physical Education Teacher 2 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q8196","q":"Which of the following port is the premier iron ore exporting port of India?","o":["Marmagao port","Jawaharlal Nehru port","Deendayal Port","New Mangalore port"],"a":0,"x":"Marmagao port in Goa is India's premier iron-ore exporting port.","e":"","src":["Junior Engineer (Electrical) or Section Officer (Electrical) 22 Jun 2022 S1"]},{"id":"q8220","q":"The Noamundi and Gua mines for Iron ore are located in ______.","o":["Rajasthan","Gujrat","Jharkhand","Chattisgarh"],"a":2,"x":"The Noamundi and Gua iron ore mines are in Jharkhand.","e":"","src":["Legal Assistant or Assistant Law Officer 20 Jun 2022 S2"]},{"id":"q8222","q":"Which of the following is an abiotic factor of resistance which can affect production of food crops?","o":["Diseases","Nematodes","Insects","Drought"],"a":3,"x":"Drought is an abiotic stress; diseases, nematodes and insects are biotic.","e":"","src":["Legal Assistant or Assistant Law Officer 20 Jun 2022 S2"]},{"id":"q8708","q":"Which of the following pairs of railway zones and its headquarters is INCORRECT?","o":["East Central –Hajipur","North Eastern – Gorakhpur","South Central –Secunderabad","South Eastern – Chennai"],"a":3,"x":"The South Eastern Railway is headquartered in Kolkata, not Chennai.","e":"","src":["Tailor Master 25 Jun 2023 S3"]},{"id":"q8709","q":"Gas Authority of India Limited was set up in:","o":["1980","1981","1983","1984"],"a":3,"x":"GAIL (Gas Authority of India Ltd) was set up in 1984.","e":"","src":["Tailor Master 25 Jun 2023 S3"]},{"id":"q8710","q":"Known as the ‘Golden Fibre’, which of the following crops is mainly grown in tropical areas?","o":["Cotton","Jute","Silk","Coir"],"a":1,"x":"Jute is called the 'golden fibre'.","e":"","src":["Tailor Master 25 Jun 2023 S3"]},{"id":"q8711","q":"Mundra Ultra Mega Power Project or Mundra UMPP is a subbituminous coal-fired power plant in Tunda village at Mundra, located in _________.","o":["Dahod","Kheda","Kutch","Mahisagar"],"a":2,"x":"The Mundra Ultra Mega Power Project is in Kutch, Gujarat.","e":"","src":["Tailor Master 25 Jun 2023 S3"]},{"id":"q8712","q":"Through which of the following states does the HVJ gas pipeline NOT pass?","o":["Maharashtra","Madhya Pradesh","Gujarat","Uttar Pradesh"],"a":0,"x":"The HVJ (Hazira–Vijaipur–Jagdishpur) gas pipeline runs through Gujarat, MP and UP, not Maharashtra.","e":"","src":["Tailor Master 25 Jun 2023 S3"]},{"id":"q9708","q":"Which district of Andhra Pradesh produces the best quality mica?","o":["Vishakhapatnam","Nellore","Guntur","Kurnool"],"a":1,"x":"Nellore district in Andhra Pradesh produces the best quality mica.","e":"","src":["Junior Engineer 29 Jun 2022 S3"]},{"id":"q9713","q":"In which year, Metro Railway, Kolkata was declared as New Zonal Railway?","o":["2010","2011","2009","2012"],"a":0,"x":"Kolkata Metro was declared Indian Railways' 17th zone in 2010.","e":"","src":["Junior Engineer 29 Jun 2022 S3"]},{"id":"q9727","q":"Inland waterways authority was set up in the year?","o":["1966","1986","1976","1996"],"a":1,"x":"The Inland Waterways Authority of India was set up in 1986.","e":"","src":["Junior Engineer 29 Jun 2022 S2"]},{"id":"q9758","q":"Where is Paradwip port which specializes in the export of iron ore, located?","o":["Odisha","Karnataka","Andhra Pradesh","West Bengal"],"a":0,"x":"Paradip port, known for iron-ore exports, is in Odisha.","e":"","src":["Junior Engineer 28 Jun 2022 S3"]},{"id":"q9946","q":"The Bhadla Solar Park has become the world’s largest solar park, with 2,245 MW of solar projects commissioned. Where is this park located?","o":["Rajasthan","Telangana","Tamil Nadu","Maharashtra"],"a":0,"x":"The Bhadla Solar Park is in Jodhpur district, Rajasthan.","e":"","src":["Junior Engineer (Electrical) or Section Officer (Electrical) 16 Mar 2022"]}]},{"id":"people","n":"Population and Census","qs":[{"id":"q0035","q":"According to Census of India 2011, which state recorded the lowest female literacy rate?","o":["Odisha","Madhya Pradesh","Rajasthan","Chhattisgarh"],"a":2,"x":"Census 2011: Rajasthan had the lowest female literacy (about 52%), and Kerala the highest (about 92%).","e":"Lowest overall literacy in 2011: Bihar (about 62%).","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0039","q":"As per NFHS 2015, which religion has the highest percentage of Scheduled Castes in India?","o":["Buddhists","Hindu","Sikhs","Christians"],"a":0,"x":"Most Indian Buddhists are Navayana (neo-Buddhists) who followed Dr B. R. Ambedkar's mass conversion of 1956, many from Scheduled Caste communities, so the SC share among Buddhists is the highest.","e":"Ambedkar converted to Buddhism at Nagpur's Deekshabhoomi on 14 October 1956.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0084","q":"Which of the following can be used as official languages in the Indian state of Meghalaya?","o":["English, Khasi and Garo","Meitei, Bengali and English","Hindi and English","English, Urdu and Garo"],"a":0,"x":"English is Meghalaya's official language, and Khasi and Garo are associate official languages under the Meghalaya State Language Act, 2005.","e":"Meghalaya's three hill regions are named after its peoples: Khasi, Garo and Jaintia Hills.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0116","q":"Which religious community forms the largest minority group in India, according to the 2011 Census?","o":["Sikhs","Muslims","Christians","Buddhists"],"a":1,"x":"Census 2011: Muslims were the largest religious minority, about 14.2% of the population.","e":"Then Christians (2.3%) and Sikhs (1.7%).","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0153","q":"What was the main emphasis during Census 1991, regarding women's economic activities in India?","o":["Participation in community development projects","Unpaid work in family farm and family enterprise","Educational achievements","Paid work outside the household"],"a":1,"x":"The 1991 Census tried specially to record women's unpaid work on family farms and in family enterprises, which earlier censuses had largely missed.","e":"Census 2011 was India's 15th census and the 7th after Independence.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0182","q":"Which of the following states has the highest population density as per the Census of 2011, excluding Delhi?","o":["Andhra Pradesh","Chhattisgarh","Assam","Bihar"],"a":3,"x":"Census 2011: Bihar had the highest population density among states, 1,106 per sq km; national average 382.","e":"Delhi (NCT) was higher, at 11,320. Lowest: Arunachal Pradesh (17).","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0230","q":"As per the 2011 Census of India, which language had the highest number of native speakers in the state of Andhra Pradesh?","o":["Tamil","Kannada","Marathi","Telugu"],"a":3,"x":"Census 2011: Telugu had by far the most native speakers in Andhra Pradesh.","e":"Andhra State (1953) was the first state formed on a linguistic basis.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0266","q":"Which of following statements is INCORRECT regarding Census of India 2011?","o":["Indian Population growth percentage between 2001-2011 is 17.64%.","Percentage growth in 2001-2011 in females is 18.12 %.","Overall sex ratio at the national level has increased by 11 points.","Percentage growth in 2001-2011 in males is 17.19%."],"a":2,"x":"Census 2011 (final figures): the sex ratio rose from 933 in 2001 to 943, a rise of 10 points (provisional figures had shown 940). A rise of '11 points' is wrong; the growth percentages given are correct.","e":"Highest sex ratio: Kerala (1,084). Lowest: Haryana (879).","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0279","q":"According to Census 2011, which state has the highest proportion of elderly population (aged 60 years and above)?","o":["Punjab","Kerala","Maharashtra","Himachal Pradesh"],"a":1,"x":"Census 2011: Kerala had the highest proportion of elderly (60+), about 12.6%.","e":"The national figure was about 8.6%.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0301","q":"As per Census 2011, identify the Union Territory with the highest male literacy rate.","o":["Chandigarh","Daman and Diu","Lakshadweep","Puducherry"],"a":2,"x":"Census 2011: Lakshadweep recorded the highest male literacy among UTs, about 96%.","e":"Highest overall literacy among states: Kerala (about 94%).","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0357","q":"Which of the following is the largest religious minority in India as per Census 2011?","o":["Jainism","Sikhism","Christianity","Islam"],"a":3,"x":"Census 2011: Islam was the largest religious minority, about 14.2% of the population (17.22 crore).","e":"Jains are among the smallest minorities, about 0.4%.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0392","q":"Which of the following union territories has the highest decadal growth in literacy rate according to Census 2011?","o":["Puducherry","Lakshadweep","Dadra and Nagar Haveli","Chandigarh"],"a":2,"x":"Among UTs, Dadra and Nagar Haveli recorded the largest decadal rise in literacy between 2001 and 2011.","e":"Highest UT literacy in 2011: Lakshadweep.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0398","q":"Which of the following statements is correct according to Census 2011?","o":["Punjab is the state with highest population density.","Uttarakhand is the state with lowest sex ratio.","Arunachal Pradesh is the state with lowest population density.","Puducherry is the union territory with least population."],"a":2,"x":"Census 2011: Arunachal Pradesh has the lowest population density, 17 per sq km. Bihar (not Punjab) is highest; Haryana (not Uttarakhand) has the lowest sex ratio; Lakshadweep (not Puducherry) is the least populous UT.","e":"National density: 382 per sq km.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0428","q":"In India, demography studies population through components such as births, deaths and _______.","o":["Marriage","Migration","Employment","Literacy"],"a":1,"x":"Population change is studied through three components: births, deaths and migration.","e":"Growth rate = birth rate − death rate ± net migration.","src":["ASO 22 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0435","q":"Demographic features in India include population size, composition, regional distribution and which component of population change?","o":["Migration","Literacy","Social mobility","Urbanisation"],"a":0,"x":"Population change has three components: births, deaths and migration. So demographic features include size, composition, distribution and migration.","e":"Urbanisation largely results from rural-to-urban migration.","src":["ASO 22 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0461","q":"The word 'Demography' is derived from which two Greek words?","o":["Demos and Logos","Geo and Graphy","Demos and Graphy","Demos and Geo"],"a":2,"x":"'Demography' comes from the Greek 'demos' (people) and 'graphein/graphy' (to describe).","e":"Demography studies population size, composition and change.","src":["ASO 27 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0466","q":"What is the largest religious group in West Bengal as per the 2011 census?","o":["Christianity","Hinduism","Islam","Sikhism"],"a":1,"x":"In Census 2011, Hindus were about 70.5% of West Bengal's population and Muslims about 27%.","e":"Nationally Hindus were 79.8%.","src":["ASO 27 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q1570","q":"What was the sex ratio of India in 1901?","o":["980","972","964","955"],"a":1,"x":"India's sex ratio in 1901 was 972 females per 1,000 males; it fell for most of the 20th century to 927 in 1991.","e":"It recovered to 943 in 2011.","src":["Patwari 17 Sep 2022 S3"]},{"id":"q1592","q":"As per the census 2011, what was the sex ratio (females per 1000 males) of Haryana state?","o":["841","860","901","879"],"a":3,"x":"Census 2011: Haryana had the lowest sex ratio among states, 879.","e":"Its child sex ratio (834) was also the lowest.","src":["Patwari 20 Aug 2022 S2"]},{"id":"q1619","q":"As per Census 2011 of India, density of population in India is _____ persons per sq km.","o":["382","540","456","654"],"a":0,"x":"Census 2011: India's population density was 382 persons per sq km.","e":"It was 325 in 2001.","src":["Patwari 20 Aug 2022 S1"]},{"id":"q6204","q":"In which year did the first census was held in India?","o":["1901","1872","1890","1921"],"a":1,"x":"India's first census was held in 1872; the first synchronous census was in 1881.","e":"","src":["Junior Clerk 9 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q1292","q":"What is the population density of India as per the Census 2011 (in per square km)?","o":["382","415","402","395"],"a":0,"x":"Census 2011: India's population density was 382 persons per sq km, up from 325 in 2001.","e":"Highest among states: Bihar (1,106). Lowest: Arunachal Pradesh (17).","src":["Stenographer 19 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1305","q":"Till which year was India in the first stage of demographic transition?","o":["1931","1901","1911","1921"],"a":3,"x":"India was in the first stage of demographic transition (high birth and death rates, slow growth) until 1921, which is called the 'Great Divide'; after that death rates fell.","e":"1921 = year of the Great Divide in India's population history.","src":["Stenographer 19 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q4887","q":"The total population of Delhi was approximately ______ crores as per the Census of India 2011.","o":["1.89","1.39","1.68","1.99"],"a":2,"x":"Delhi's population in Census 2011 was about 1.68 crore.","e":"","src":["Stenographer 26 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q5014","q":"According to the Census of 2011, which of the following states has the maximum male literacy rate?","o":["Tripura","Odisha","Punjab","West Bengal"],"a":0,"x":"Of these states, Tripura had the highest male literacy rate in Census 2011 (about 92%).","e":"","src":["Stenographer 26 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q4962","q":"Which Indian state has the second highest population after Uttar Pradesh as per the Census of 2011?","o":["Tamil Nadu","Bihar","Maharashtra","Rajasthan"],"a":2,"x":"Maharashtra was the second most populous state in Census 2011, after Uttar Pradesh.","e":"","src":["Personal Assistant 26 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q5176","q":"Which of the following states and the percentage of its population with respect to the total population of India is approximately correct according to the Census of 2011?","o":["Rajasthan - 6.5%","Maharashtra - 9.29%","West Bengal - 9.79%","Bihar - 9.02%"],"a":1,"x":"Maharashtra had about 9.29% of India's population in Census 2011.","e":"UP about 16.5%.","src":["Personal Assistant 27 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q5177","q":"According to the Census of 2011, which of the following states has the literacy rate less than 70%?","o":["Mizoram","Bihar","Kerala","Himachal Pradesh"],"a":1,"x":"Bihar had the lowest literacy rate in Census 2011, about 61.8%.","e":"","src":["Personal Assistant 27 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q5399","q":"As per the government data, which of the following states was the first state that achieved a hundred percent literacy?","o":["Karnataka","Tamil Nādu","Kerala","Goa"],"a":2,"x":"Kerala was declared India's first fully literate state in 1991.","e":"","src":["Personal Assistant 29 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q5440","q":"At the time of independence, India had an approximate population of_____","o":["250 million","340 million","180 million","460 million"],"a":1,"x":"India had a population of about 340–345 million at independence in 1947.","e":"","src":["Personal Assistant 29 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q10077","q":"As per census 2011, what was the workforce participation rate for women at the all-India level?","o":["36.21 %","25.51 %","45.52 %","18.35 %"],"a":1,"x":"The work participation rate for women in Census 2011 was 25.51%.","e":"","src":["Assistant Superintendent 20 Jul 2024 S1"]},{"id":"q10139","q":"What is the average annual growth rate of India’s population as per the 2011 census?","o":["2.89 %","0.89 %","1.63 %","2.13 %"],"a":2,"x":"India's average annual population growth rate in 2001–2011 was 1.64%.","e":"","src":["Assistant Superintendent 20 Jul 2024 S3"]},{"id":"q4466","q":"Which year is known as “year of the great divide”?","o":["1911","1921","1947","2011"],"a":1,"x":"1921 is called the 'year of the great divide' because India's population has grown continuously since then (NCERT).","e":"","src":["Office Superintendent 24 Oct 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q4494","q":"In which of the following years the first National Population Register (NPR) was done.","o":["2008","2009","2010","2011"],"a":2,"x":"The first National Population Register was prepared in 2010, along with the house-listing phase of Census 2011.","e":"","src":["Office Superintendent 24 Oct 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q8882","q":"Which state in India has the highest Buddhist population, as per 2011 census report?","o":["West Bengal","Assam","Himachal Pradesh","Maharashtra"],"a":3,"x":"Maharashtra had the largest Buddhist population in Census 2011.","e":"","src":["MTS 2 Mar 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q8929","q":"According to the Census of India 2011, what was the total population of India?","o":["About 150 crore","About 102 crore","About 121 crore","About 133 crore"],"a":2,"x":"India's population in Census 2011 was about 121 crore.","e":"","src":["MTS 3 Mar 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q8957","q":"What was the approximate decadal growth rate of India's population from 2001 to 2011 as per the Census 2011?","o":["17.7%","18.7%","17.2%","18.2%"],"a":0,"x":"India's decadal population growth from 2001 to 2011 was about 17.7%.","e":"","src":["MTS 3 Mar 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q9009","q":"Which indicator shows the population increase calculated by subtracting the death rate from the birth rate?","o":["Fertility Rate","Population Density","Mortality Rate","Natural Growth Rate"],"a":3,"x":"Natural growth rate = birth rate − death rate.","e":"","src":["MTS 3 Mar 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q9158","q":"As per Census of India 2011, which is the third largest religion in India?","o":["Jain","Christian","Buddhist","Sikh"],"a":1,"x":"In Census 2011 Christians were India's third largest religious group, after Hindus and Muslims.","e":"","src":["MTS 6 Mar 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q9188","q":"Which of the following governmental authorities is responsible for conducting the Census of India?","o":["Ministry of Welfare & Society","Ministry of Environment","Ministry of Census & Population","Ministry of Home Affairs"],"a":3,"x":"The Census is conducted by the Registrar General and Census Commissioner under the Ministry of Home Affairs.","e":"","src":["MTS 8 Mar 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q9238","q":"Which census post-independence introduced the concept of “Scheduled Castes” and “Scheduled Tribes” classification?","o":["1951 Census","1941 Census","1911 Census","1931 Census"],"a":0,"x":"The 1951 Census, the first after independence, recorded Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes as separate categories.","e":"","src":["MTS 8 Mar 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q9276","q":"Which among the following reflects India’s population density as documented in Census 2011?","o":["298 persons per sq km","324 persons per sq km","410 persons per sq km","382 persons per sq km"],"a":3,"x":"India's population density in Census 2011 was 382 persons per sq km.","e":"","src":["MTS 8 Mar 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q9304","q":"As per the Census 2011, which of the following union territories has the highest percentage of the Muslim population out of the total population of the union territory?","o":["Lakshadweep","Andaman and Nicobar Islands","National Capital Territory of Delhi","UT of Dadra and Nagar Haveli and Daman and Diu"],"a":0,"x":"Lakshadweep has the highest share of Muslims among union territories (over 96% in Census 2011).","e":"","src":["MTS 24 Feb 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q9387","q":"As per Census 2011, which of the following states has the lowest percentage of the Christian population out of the total population of that state?","o":["Andhra Pradesh","Uttar Pradesh","Karnataka","Bihar"],"a":3,"x":"Of these states, Bihar had the lowest share of Christians in Census 2011 (about 0.12%).","e":"","src":["MTS 25 Feb 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q9430","q":"As per the Census 2011, Punjab had approximately what percentage of the Sikh population out of the total population of the state?","o":["79.83%","89.23%","57.69%","64.21%"],"a":2,"x":"Sikhs were about 57.7% of Punjab's population in Census 2011.","e":"","src":["MTS 26 Feb 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q9469","q":"According to the NSSO 61st Round Survey (2004–05), what was the approximate share of Other Backward Classes (OBCs) in India’s population?","o":["21%","29%","30%","41%"],"a":3,"x":"The NSSO 61st round (2004–05) put the OBC share of India's population at about 41%.","e":"","src":["MTS 26 Feb 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q3638","q":"“Asia has many places where people are few and few places where people are very many” is quoted by which of the following geographer?","o":["George Carlin","George B.Cressey","Arthur Gomes","Griffith Taylor"],"a":1,"x":"Geographer George B. Cressey said: 'Asia has many places where people are few and few places where people are very many', describing uneven population distribution (NCERT).","e":"","src":["Fire Operator 20 Apr 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q3676","q":"In the Great Himalayan range, the valleys are mostly inhabited by which nomadic group?","o":["Bhil","Bhotia","Saka","Maasai"],"a":1,"x":"The Bhotias are nomadic herders who move up the valleys of the Great Himalayas in summer and down in winter.","e":"","src":["Fire Operator 20 Apr 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2980","q":"What is the literacy-rate of the country as per the Census 2011?","o":["79.61 percent","75.46 percent","74.04 percent","71.23 percent"],"a":2,"x":"India's literacy rate in Census 2011 was 74.04% (male 82.14%, female 65.46%).","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 17 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q4013","q":"Density of Population is the ratio between ______ .","o":["the number of births to the number of deaths","the number of women to the number of men","the number of people to the size of land","the number of people to the number of houses"],"a":2,"x":"Population density is the number of people per unit area of land (per sq km).","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 22 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q4014","q":"Which of the following is not a geographical factor influencing the distribution of population?","o":["Climate","Landforms","Availability of water","Urbanization"],"a":3,"x":"Climate, landforms and availability of water are geographical factors; urbanisation is an economic factor (NCERT Class 12).","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 22 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q4294","q":"The least populated state of India according to 2011 census is _____________.","o":["Tripura","Sikkim","Lakshadweep","Maharashtra"],"a":1,"x":"Sikkim was the least populous state in Census 2011 (about 6.1 lakh).","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 24 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q4626","q":"As per the 2011 census, which is the most densely populated district in India?","o":["Kolkata","Chennai","North East Delhi","Mumbai Suburban"],"a":2,"x":"North East Delhi was India's most densely populated district in Census 2011 (about 36,000 people per sq km).","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 25 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q3112","q":"What is India’s population according to Census of India 2011?","o":["Approximately 119 crores","Approximately 125 crores","Approximately 121 crores","Approximately 123 crores"],"a":2,"x":"India's population in Census 2011 was about 121 crore.","e":"","src":["Accountant 29 Nov 2021 S3","Legal Assistant 17 Oct 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5479","q":"Which of the following states has no scheduled tribe as per census 2011?","o":["Mizoram","Nagaland","Kerala","Punjab"],"a":3,"x":"Punjab has no notified Scheduled Tribe (Census 2011).","e":"","src":["Accountant 29 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0909","q":"A population pyramid is used to show:","o":["Distribution of agricultural land","Urban-rural migration patterns","Age and sex structure of the population","Population density of countries"],"a":2,"x":"A population pyramid shows a population's age and sex structure: males on one side, females on the other, youngest at the bottom.","e":"A wide base means high birth rates; a narrow base means an ageing population.","src":["PRT 17 Feb 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q0960","q":"As per Census 2011, which major state had the lowest literacy rate?","o":["Rajasthan","Madhya Pradesh","Bihar","Uttar Pradesh"],"a":2,"x":"In Census 2011 Bihar had the lowest literacy rate among states (61.8%); Kerala had the highest (94%).","e":"National literacy in 2011: 74.04%.","src":["PRT 18 Feb 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q1253","q":"Which of the following is an indicator of population composition?","o":["Total fertility rate","Sex ratio","Population density","Birth rate"],"a":1,"x":"Population composition describes the make-up of a population: sex ratio, age structure, literacy, occupation. Birth rate and density describe growth and distribution.","e":"India's sex ratio (Census 2011): 943 females per 1,000 males.","src":["PRT 3 Apr 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1266","q":"What was the sex ratio in India as reported in Census 2011?","o":["930 females per 1000 males","943 females per 1000 males","920 females per 1000 males","950 females per 1000 males"],"a":1,"x":"Census 2011 (final): 943 females per 1,000 males, up from 933 in 2001.","e":"Child sex ratio (0–6 years) fell to 919.","src":["PRT 10 Apr 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q3796","q":"As per Census 2011 which Indian state occupied first place that attracts the migrants?","o":["Gujarat","Haryana","Maharashtra","Punjab"],"a":2,"x":"In Census 2011 Maharashtra received the largest number of in-migrants.","e":"","src":["Nursery Teacher 21 Oct 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q7834","q":"As per Census 2011, what was the child sex ratio in India?","o":["919","920","921","922"],"a":0,"x":"The child sex ratio (0–6 years) in Census 2011 was 919 girls per 1,000 boys.","e":"","src":["Nursery Teacher 24 Jun 2023 S1"]},{"id":"q1379","q":"The number of women who die as a result of complications from pregnancy or childbearing in a given year per 1,00,000 live births in that year is called ______.","o":["Motherly Mortality Rate (MMR)","Maternal Mortality Rate (MMR)","Mother Mortality Rate (MMR)","Maturation Mortality Rate (MMR)"],"a":1,"x":"The maternal mortality rate counts women who die from pregnancy- or childbirth-related complications per 1,00,000 live births in a year.","e":"India's MMR fell below 100 in SRS 2018–20; the SDG target is below 70 by 2030.","src":["TGT 24 Jun 2023 S2"]},{"id":"q1400","q":"As per the Census of India 2011, what percentage of Indians live in rural areas?","o":["81.2","61.5","68.8","77.3"],"a":2,"x":"Census 2011: 68.8% of Indians lived in rural areas and 31.2% in urban areas.","e":"Most urbanised large state: Tamil Nadu. Least: Himachal Pradesh.","src":["TGT 24 Jun 2023 S3"]},{"id":"q1402","q":"Which of the following states had the highest growth rate for population according to the Census of India, 2011 as compared to that of 2001?","o":["Himachal Pradesh","Meghalaya","Bihar","Uttar Pradesh"],"a":1,"x":"Of these, Meghalaya had the highest decadal population growth in 2001–11 (about 27.9%), the highest of any state; the national figure was 17.7%.","e":"Among all states and UTs, Dadra and Nagar Haveli was highest (about 55.9%).","src":["TGT 24 Jun 2023 S3"]},{"id":"q1475","q":"Which is the most populous state in India according to the 2011 Census?","o":["Madhya Pradesh","West Bengal","Bihar","Uttar Pradesh"],"a":3,"x":"Uttar Pradesh was the most populous state in Census 2011 (19.98 crore, 16.5% of India), followed by Maharashtra and Bihar.","e":"Least populous state: Sikkim.","src":["TGT 7 Feb 2024 S2"]},{"id":"q1499","q":"Which of the following age groups of women is generally taken while calculating the ‘Fertility Rate’?","o":["15 to 49 years","21 to 45 years","18 to 59 years","13 to 45 years"],"a":0,"x":"Fertility rates are calculated for women of reproductive age, 15 to 49 years.","e":"A total fertility rate of 2.1 is replacement level; India's TFR is 2.0 (NFHS-5).","src":["TGT 29 Aug 2025"]},{"id":"q1552","q":"As per Census 2011, what was the sex ratio of Kerala?","o":["1002","1084","1045","985"],"a":1,"x":"Census 2011: Kerala had the highest sex ratio among states, 1,084 females per 1,000 males.","e":"Among UTs, Puducherry was highest (1,037).","src":["TGT 31 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q2307","q":"Which of the following statement is correct? I. Internal migration changes the size of the population. II. Agriculture comes under primary activity.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":1,"x":"Internal migration does not change a country's population size; agriculture is a primary activity. Only II is correct.","e":"","src":["TGT 13 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q4791","q":"What is the population of rural India as per 2011 Census?","o":["About 833 million","About 733 million","About 933 million","About 783 million"],"a":0,"x":"Census 2011 counted about 833 million people in rural India.","e":"","src":["TGT 25 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q5599","q":"Which of the following tribes is a Dravidian ethnolinguistic group?","o":["Lambada","Konyak","Gonds","Miri"],"a":2,"x":"The Gonds are a Dravidian (Gondi-speaking) tribal group of central India.","e":"","src":["TGT 30 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q5835","q":"As per the 2011 census, which state has the highest tribal population?","o":["Madhya Pradesh","Nagaland","Odisha","Rajasthan"],"a":0,"x":"Madhya Pradesh had the largest Scheduled Tribe population in Census 2011.","e":"","src":["TGT 5 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q5902","q":"Which of the following is NOT a process of change of population?","o":["Birth","Death","Migration","Unemployment"],"a":3,"x":"Population changes through births, deaths and migration; unemployment is not one of these processes.","e":"","src":["TGT 6 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q6307","q":"According to 2011 census, which of the following state of India has lowest population density?","o":["Sikkim","Arunachal Pradesh","Nagaland","Mizoram"],"a":1,"x":"Arunachal Pradesh had the lowest population density in Census 2011 (17 per sq km).","e":"","src":["TGT 9 Oct 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q8816","q":"Which of the following languages is NOT a part of the Dravidian language family?","o":["Kannada","Assamese","Telugu","Malayalam"],"a":1,"x":"Assamese is an Indo-Aryan language; Kannada, Telugu and Malayalam are Dravidian.","e":"","src":["Special Educator 26 Jun 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q8821","q":"Which of the following states have small population shares despite their large geographical areas?","o":["Uttar Pradesh, Maharashtra, Bihar","Jammu & Kashmir, Arunachal Pradesh, Uttarakhand","Punjab, Haryana, Gujarat","West Bengal, Tamil Nadu, Rajasthan"],"a":1,"x":"Jammu & Kashmir, Arunachal Pradesh and Uttarakhand are large in area but have small population shares.","e":"","src":["Special Educator 26 Jun 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q9839","q":"Kokborok is the official language of which state ?","o":["Karnataka","Uttarakhand","Andra Pradesh","Tripura"],"a":3,"x":"Kokborok is an official language of Tripura.","e":"","src":["Special Educator 14 Jul 2024 S1"]},{"id":"q9840","q":"As per Census 2011, which of the following is/are CORRECT with respect to literacy rates in India? I. The literacy rate in the country was 74.04%. II. The literacy rate for males was 82.14%. III. The literacy rate for females was more that for than males.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","Only III"],"a":1,"x":"Census 2011: overall literacy 74.04%, male 82.14%, female 65.46%, so the female rate was not higher.","e":"","src":["Special Educator 14 Jul 2024 S1"]},{"id":"q9847","q":"For the purpose of Census 2011, what was the minimum age of a person to be treated as literate?","o":["7 years","9 years","6 years","5 years"],"a":0,"x":"In Census 2011 a person aged 7 or above who could read and write with understanding was counted as literate.","e":"","src":["Special Educator 14 Jul 2024 S1"]},{"id":"q0800","q":"The Census Act, __________, of India states that the Census Commissioner, India, is the statutory authority vested with the responsibility of conducting the Housing and Population Census and the Rules framed thereunder.","o":["1954","1948","1949","1950"],"a":1,"x":"The Census Act, 1948 makes the Census Commissioner the statutory authority for conducting the Housing and Population Census.","e":"India's first synchronous census was in 1881; Census 2011 was the 15th.","src":["PGT 11 Jul 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q8121","q":"In which year was the Census Act enacted in India?","o":["1947","1948","1949","1950"],"a":1,"x":"The Census Act was enacted in 1948.","e":"","src":["PGT 21 Jun 2023 S1"]},{"id":"q8458","q":"According to the Census of India 2011, identify the group of states with the lowest literacy rate in India.","o":["Bihar, Jharkhand, Chhattisgarh","Bihar, Arunachal Pradesh, Rajasthan","Rajasthan, Assam, Uttar Pradesh","Arunachal Pradesh, Odisha, Jharkhand"],"a":1,"x":"Census 2011: Bihar (61.8%), Arunachal Pradesh (65.4%) and Rajasthan (66.1%) had the lowest literacy rates.","e":"","src":["PGT 22 Jun 2023 S2"]},{"id":"q9853","q":"Malayalam is the official language of which of the following Indian states?","o":["Karnataka","Kerala","Tamil Nadu","Andhra Pradesh"],"a":1,"x":"Malayalam is the official language of Kerala.","e":"","src":["PGT 16 Jul 2025"]},{"id":"q10001","q":"Which of the following minority groups has the highest literacy rate according to Census 2011?","o":["Sikhs","Christians","Buddhists","Jain"],"a":3,"x":"Jains had the highest literacy rate among religious communities in Census 2011.","e":"","src":["Junior Engineer (Electrical) or Section Officer (Electrical) 22 Jun 2022 S2"]},{"id":"q10013","q":"Which of these South Indian states had lowest sex ratio as per census 2011?","o":["Tamil Nadu","Andhra Pradesh","Karnataka","Kerala"],"a":2,"x":"Of the southern states listed, Karnataka had the lowest sex ratio in Census 2011 (973).","e":"","src":["Junior Engineer (Civil) or Section Officer (Civil) 27 Jun 2022 S3"]},{"id":"q2228","q":"In India, as per the 2011 census, the share of workforce engaged in the agriculture sector (comprising of cultivators and agricultural labourers) has come down to _____________ in 2011.","o":["34.6 percent","44.6 percent","64.6 percent","54.6 percent"],"a":3,"x":"In Census 2011, cultivators and agricultural labourers made up 54.6% of the workforce.","e":"","src":["Laboratory Assistant (Lie Detection) 13 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2575","q":"The Census 2011 was the ______ national census survey conducted by the Census Organization of India.","o":["Fifteenth","Tenth","Thirteenth","Sixteenth"],"a":0,"x":"Census 2011 was India's 15th national census.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Medical Electronics) 16 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2799","q":"Which of the following districts is the highest literate district of India as per Census of India 2011?","o":["Serchhip","Kottayam","Mahe","Aizawl"],"a":0,"x":"Serchhip in Mizoram was India's most literate district in Census 2011.","e":"","src":["Inspecting Officer 16 Oct 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2947","q":"Which of the following States has the minimum percentage of Decadal Growth according to the Census 2011?","o":["Kerala","Manipur","Rajasthan","Punjab"],"a":0,"x":"Of these, Kerala had the lowest decadal population growth in Census 2011 (about 4.9%).","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Intrumentation & Control) 17 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q3083","q":"Which of the following states or Union Territory has the best sex ratio according to census of India 2011?","o":["Chhattisgarh","Tamil Nadu","Puducherry","Andhra Pradesh"],"a":2,"x":"Of these, Puducherry had the best sex ratio in Census 2011 (1,037).","e":"","src":["Draftsman 17 Oct 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q3117","q":"Which of the following states has the highest literacy rate among the Scheduled Tribe Population according to Census of India 2011?","o":["Gujarat","West Bengal","Rajasthan","Madhya Pradesh"],"a":0,"x":"Of these, Gujarat had the highest Scheduled Tribe literacy rate in Census 2011.","e":"","src":["Legal Assistant 17 Oct 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q3170","q":"In which year was the first census of independent India done?","o":["1947","1949","1951","1954"],"a":2,"x":"Independent India's first census was held in 1951.","e":"","src":["Pharmacist 18 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q3171","q":"As per ‘Household Social Consumption: Education in India as part of the 75th round of National Sample Survey – from July 2017 to June 2018’ ______ is the most literate state in India.","o":["Goa","Sikkim","Mizoram","Kerala"],"a":3,"x":"The NSS 75th round (2017-18) found Kerala the most literate state.","e":"","src":["Pharmacist 18 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q3988","q":"According to the 2001 Census, and 2011 census, which state is the highest populated state in India?","o":["Uttar Pradesh","Maharashtra","Bihar","Jharkhand"],"a":0,"x":"Uttar Pradesh was India's most populous state in both the 2001 and 2011 Censuses.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Printing) 22 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q4658","q":"According to Census of India 2011, what is the gap between male and female literacy rate in India?","o":["22.67%","20.43%","12.45%","16.68%"],"a":3,"x":"In Census 2011 the gap between male (82.14%) and female (65.46%) literacy was 16.68 percentage points.","e":"","src":["Librarian 25 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q5350","q":"The practice of women having several husbands is called:","o":["Polygyny","Polyandry","Exogamy","Endogamy"],"a":1,"x":"A woman having several husbands is polyandry.","e":"","src":["Laboratory Assistant (Physics) 28 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q5619","q":"Which type of settlements are generally found in the northeastern states of India?","o":["Fragmented Settlements","Clustered Settlements","Hamleted Settlements","Isolated Settlements"],"a":1,"x":"Clustered (compact) settlements are common in the fertile plains and the north-eastern states.","e":"","src":["Labour Welfare Inspector 30 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q8195","q":"As per census 2011, what was the literacy rate of Kerala?","o":["95%","94%","96%","97%"],"a":1,"x":"Kerala's literacy rate in Census 2011 was about 94%.","e":"","src":["Junior Engineer (Electrical) or Section Officer (Electrical) 22 Jun 2022 S1"]},{"id":"q8219","q":"As per census 2011 the number of districts in India were ______.","o":["560","640","580","456"],"a":1,"x":"Census 2011 covered 640 districts.","e":"","src":["Legal Assistant or Assistant Law Officer 20 Jun 2022 S2"]},{"id":"q9715","q":"Which North-East state had the highest literacy rate as per census 2011?","o":["Tripura","Mizoram","Manipur","Assam"],"a":1,"x":"Mizoram had the highest literacy rate in the North-East in Census 2011.","e":"","src":["Junior Engineer 29 Jun 2022 S3"]},{"id":"q9733","q":"What was the rural literacy rate as per the census 2011?","o":["55 percent","67.8 percent","62.3 percent","60.6 percent"],"a":1,"x":"Rural literacy in Census 2011 was about 67.8%.","e":"","src":["Junior Engineer 29 Jun 2022 S2"]},{"id":"q9884","q":"As per Census of India 2011, which of the following statements is/are correct? A) The second highest sex ratioamongstates was recorded in Tamil Nadu. B) The second highest sex ratio among union territories was recorded in Lakshadweep.","o":["Only A","Both A and B","Neither A nor B","Only B"],"a":1,"x":"Census 2011: Tamil Nadu had the second-highest sex ratio among states and Lakshadweep among UTs.","e":"","src":["Scientific Assistant 27 Jul 2024 S2"]},{"id":"q9896","q":"'Santhali' is the official language of which of the following states of India?","o":["Gujarat","Haryana","Bihar","Jharkhand"],"a":3,"x":"Santhali is an official language of Jharkhand.","e":"","src":["Scientific Assistant 27 Jul 2024 S2"]},{"id":"q9902","q":"What is the term used to describe the sex ratio for the age group 0-6 years in India?","o":["Youth Sex Ratio","Teen Sex Ratio","Infant Sex Ratio","Child Sex Ratio"],"a":3,"x":"The sex ratio for ages 0–6 is called the child sex ratio.","e":"","src":["Junior Engineer (Mechanical) 5 Oct 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q9943","q":"World Population Day is observed on:","o":["11th October","11th July","11th August","11th September"],"a":1,"x":"World Population Day is observed on 11 July.","e":"","src":["Junior Engineer (Electrical) or Section Officer (Electrical) 16 Mar 2022"]},{"id":"q9954","q":"According to the report (2011) of Ministry of Housing and Urban affairs, which state in India is the most urbanised with 62.2 per cent urban population?","o":["Uttar Pradesh","Andhra Pradesh","Punjab","Goa"],"a":3,"x":"Goa is India's most urbanised state (62.2% urban, Census 2011).","e":"","src":["Junior Engineer (Electrical) or Section Officer (Electrical) 16 Mar 2022"]}]},{"id":"world","n":"World and Neighbours","qs":[{"id":"q0068","q":"Which neighbouring country of India has a high proportion of people living in the Terai region, known for its fertile plains?","o":["Bhutan","Myanmar","Pakistan","Nepal"],"a":3,"x":"Nepal's Terai is the fertile, low-lying strip along the Indian border; roughly half of Nepal's people live there.","e":"Terai lies south of the Bhabar belt at the foot of the Himalayas, where streams re-emerge as marshy land.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0104","q":"In which country are the Rohingya people, an ethnic minority, primarily located?","o":["Myanmar","Bangladesh","Bhutan","Nepal"],"a":0,"x":"The Rohingya are a mostly Muslim minority from Rakhine State in Myanmar; since 2017 about a million have fled to Bangladesh.","e":"Cox's Bazar in Bangladesh holds the world's largest refugee camp.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0147","q":"Which of the following cities in Bangladesh is the most populous?","o":["Khulna","Dhaka","Rangpur","Chittagong"],"a":1,"x":"Dhaka, the capital, is the most populous city of Bangladesh; Chittagong is the second largest and its main port.","e":"Dhaka stands on the Buriganga river.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0226","q":"The Pashtun ethnic group is mainly concentrated in which neighbouring country of India?","o":["Bangladesh","Bhutan","Afghanistan","Nepal"],"a":2,"x":"The Pashtuns are the largest ethnic group of Afghanistan and are also numerous in Pakistan's Khyber Pakhtunkhwa.","e":"Khan Abdul Ghaffar Khan, the 'Frontier Gandhi', was a Pashtun leader.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0236","q":"Which commission was established to maintain liaison to ensure the most effective joint effort in maximising the benefits from common river systems between India and Bangladesh?","o":["Indo-Bangladesh Co-operation Commission","Joint Ministerial Commission on Water Resources (JMCWR)","Indo-Bangladesh Joint Rivers Commission (JRC)","Joint Consultative Commission (JCC)"],"a":2,"x":"The Indo-Bangladesh Joint Rivers Commission (1972) coordinates work on shared rivers so both countries get the most benefit.","e":"The Ganga Water Treaty was signed in 1996, for 30 years.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0261","q":"Which of the following is the largest ethnic group in Sri Lanka?","o":["Indian Tamils","Sinhalese","Sri Lankan Tamils","Sri Lankan Moors"],"a":1,"x":"The Sinhalese are the largest ethnic group in Sri Lanka (about three-quarters), mostly Theravada Buddhist.","e":"Sri Lankan Tamils are the largest minority.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0278","q":"Maldives Islands are situated to the south of _____.","o":["Lakshadweep","Majuli","Divar Island","Andaman and Nicobar Islands"],"a":0,"x":"The Maldives lie south of Lakshadweep across the Eight Degree Channel.","e":"Nine Degree Channel: between Kavaratti (Lakshadweep) and Minicoy. Ten Degree Channel: between Little Andaman and Car Nicobar.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0312","q":"The majority of Myanmar embraces which religion?","o":["Mahayana Buddhism","Theravada Buddhism","Nichiren Buddhism","Vajrayana Buddhism"],"a":1,"x":"Most people in Myanmar follow Theravada Buddhism, as in Sri Lanka, Thailand, Cambodia and Laos.","e":"Mahayana is dominant in China, Japan and Korea; Vajrayana in Tibet and Bhutan.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0495","q":"Which among the following is also known as \"Salt Sea\"?","o":["Dead Sea","Caspian Sea","Red Sea","Mediterranean Sea"],"a":0,"x":"The Dead Sea, between Jordan and Israel, is also called the 'Salt Sea'. It is about 430 m below sea level, the lowest land point on Earth.","e":"It is so salty that people float easily.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0496","q":"Mediterranean Sea is an intercontinental sea that stretches from the ____________ Ocean on the west to Asia on the east and separates Europe from Africa.","o":["Atlantic","Indian","Pacific","Arctic"],"a":0,"x":"The Mediterranean Sea stretches from the Atlantic Ocean in the west (through the Strait of Gibraltar) to Asia in the east, separating Europe from Africa.","e":"It connects to the Red Sea through the Suez Canal.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0536","q":"The Indian Ocean is bounded by _________ in the east.","o":["Antarctica","Australia","Africa","Iran"],"a":1,"x":"The Indian Ocean is bounded by Africa to the west, Asia to the north, Australia to the east and the Southern Ocean to the south.","e":"It is the only ocean named after a country.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0575","q":"Which among the following seas is the world's largest inland body of water?","o":["Black Sea","Mediterranean Sea","Caspian Sea","Red Sea"],"a":2,"x":"The Caspian Sea is the world's largest inland body of water, a saltwater lake between Europe and Asia.","e":"Largest freshwater lake by area: Lake Superior. Deepest: Lake Baikal.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0616","q":"Which among the following peaks is also known as \"Hidden Peak\"?","o":["Kanchenjunga","Gasherbrum I","Nanda Devi","Godwin Austen"],"a":1,"x":"Gasherbrum I (8,080 m) in the Karakoram is called 'Hidden Peak' because it is hard to see from the approach valleys.","e":"K2 is also called Godwin-Austen.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0655","q":"Puerto Rico Trench is the deepest trench of which of the following oceans?","o":["Atlantic","Indian","Pacific","Arctic"],"a":0,"x":"The Puerto Rico Trench is the deepest point of the Atlantic Ocean (about 8,400 m).","e":"The Mariana Trench (Challenger Deep, about 11,000 m) in the Pacific is the deepest on Earth.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0658","q":"Maldives is located to the ____________ direction of India.","o":["southeast","southwest","west","east"],"a":1,"x":"The Maldives lie to the south-west of India in the Arabian Sea, south of Lakshadweep.","e":"Sri Lanka lies to the south-east, across the Palk Strait.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q1988","q":"Which continent produces more than half of the world’s tin?","o":["Europe","Asia","North America","South America"],"a":1,"x":"Asia produces more than half of the world's tin; China, Malaysia and Indonesia are leading producers (NCERT).","e":"Tin is used for tinplating and solder.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2155","q":"Which of the following is the largest delta of the world?","o":["The Ganges-Brahmaputra Delta","The Nile Delta","The Mississippi Delta","The Yellow River Delta"],"a":0,"x":"The Ganga–Brahmaputra (Sundarbans) delta in India and Bangladesh is the world's largest delta.","e":"The Sundarbans is also the largest mangrove forest.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2911","q":"______ is the largest continent of the world.","o":["Africa","Asia","Europe","Antarctica"],"a":1,"x":"Asia is the largest continent, covering about 30% of the world's land area.","e":"Smallest: Australia.","src":["Junior Clerk 17 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6111","q":"Victoria fall is located on borders of Zimbabwe and _______________.","o":["Zambia","South Africa","Ethiopia","Egypt"],"a":0,"x":"Victoria Falls on the Zambezi lies on the border of Zambia and Zimbabwe.","e":"","src":["Junior Clerk 9 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q6112","q":"Which of the following statement regarding Pacific Ocean is correct? I. It is the largest Ocean of the world. II. It is in ‘S’ shape.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":0,"x":"The Pacific is the largest ocean; the Atlantic is the S-shaped one.","e":"","src":["Junior Clerk 9 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q4047","q":"The Strait of Malacca joins the Indian Ocean and the _________ Ocean.","o":["Southern","Arctic","Pacific","Atlantic"],"a":2,"x":"The Strait of Malacca, between Malaysia and Sumatra, joins the Indian Ocean and the Pacific Ocean.","e":"","src":["Stenographer 23 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q4885","q":"Which is the biggest country (by land area) in the continent of Africa?","o":["Algeria","Libya","Niger","Angola"],"a":0,"x":"Algeria is Africa's largest country by area.","e":"","src":["Stenographer 26 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q4890","q":"Which is the largest lake in the world by surface area?","o":["Caspian Sea","Lake Superior","Lake Victoria","Lake Huron"],"a":0,"x":"The Caspian Sea is the world's largest lake by surface area.","e":"","src":["Stenographer 26 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q5017","q":"Which of the following straits connects the Persian Gulf to the Gulf of Oman?","o":["Hormuz Strait","Bering Strait","Bab al-Mandab Strait","Palk Strait"],"a":0,"x":"The Strait of Hormuz connects the Persian Gulf to the Gulf of Oman.","e":"","src":["Stenographer 26 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6863","q":"The deepest point of the ________________ is known as Richards Deep.","o":["Peru-Chile Trench","South Sandwich Trench","Tonga Trench","Japan Trench"],"a":0,"x":"Richards Deep is the deepest point of the Peru–Chile (Atacama) Trench in the Pacific.","e":"","src":["Stenographer 14 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q6916","q":"Which among the following trench is not located in the Pacific Ocean?","o":["Peru-Chile Trench","South Sandwich Trench","Tonga Trench","Japan Trench"],"a":1,"x":"The South Sandwich Trench is in the South Atlantic; the Peru–Chile, Tonga and Japan trenches are in the Pacific.","e":"","src":["Stenographer 14 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6960","q":"Which among the following trench was earlier known as the Java Trench?","o":["Puerto Rico Trench","South Sandwich Trench","Sunda Trench","Mariana Trench"],"a":2,"x":"The Sunda Trench in the Indian Ocean was earlier called the Java Trench.","e":"","src":["Stenographer 8 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q7007","q":"The deepest point in the Indian Ocean is in which trench?","o":["Tonga trench","Philippine Trench","Puerto Rico Trench","Sunda trench"],"a":3,"x":"The deepest point of the Indian Ocean is in the Sunda (Java) Trench.","e":"","src":["Stenographer 14 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q5180","q":"Which strait is the only marine gateway between the icy Arctic and the Pacific Ocean located between Alaska and Russia?","o":["Hormuz strait","Bering strait","Bab al-Mandab strait","Palk strait"],"a":1,"x":"The Bering Strait, between Alaska and Russia, links the Arctic Ocean with the Pacific.","e":"","src":["Personal Assistant 27 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q5424","q":"Which of the following terms collectively refers to the armed organizations that fought against the Pakistan army during the Bangladesh Liberation War?","o":["Liberation Tigers","Mukti Bahini","Bengal Tigers","Freedom Bahini"],"a":1,"x":"The Mukti Bahini were the armed groups that fought the Pakistan army in the 1971 Bangladesh Liberation War.","e":"","src":["Personal Assistant 29 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q3463","q":"Niagara falls is located in ______.","o":["Asia","South Africa","Africa","North America"],"a":3,"x":"Niagara Falls lies on the border of the USA and Canada in North America, on the Niagara River.","e":"","src":["Store Keeper 19 Apr 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q3476","q":"Which country withdrew from the European Union on 31 January 2020?","o":["Austria","United Kingdom","Latvia","Estonia"],"a":1,"x":"The United Kingdom left the European Union on 31 January 2020 ('Brexit'), after the 2016 referendum.","e":"","src":["Store Keeper 19 Apr 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q3814","q":"Which of the following is the highest Plateau in the world?","o":["Tibet Plateau","Deccan Plateau","East African Plateau","Western Australia Plateau"],"a":0,"x":"The Tibetan Plateau, about 4,500 m high on average, is the world's highest plateau, called the 'Roof of the World'.","e":"","src":["Fire Operator 22 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2761","q":"Which among the following oceans has its apex in the north at the Bering Strait?","o":["Atlantic Ocean","Arctic Ocean","Pacific Ocean","Southern Ocean"],"a":2,"x":"The Pacific Ocean, roughly triangular, has its apex in the north at the Bering Strait.","e":"The Pacific is the largest and deepest ocean.","src":["Ahlmad 16 Oct 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2832","q":"Hindu-Kush, which is an extension of the ___________ range runs in Afghanistan.","o":["Aravalli","Karakoram","Satpura","Purvanchal"],"a":1,"x":"The Hindu Kush range in Afghanistan is a western extension of the Karakoram system (NCERT).","e":"","src":["Ahlmad 16 Oct 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q3257","q":"Which of the following neighbour of India is larger than India in terms of area?","o":["Bangladesh","Sri Lanka","China","Afghanistan"],"a":2,"x":"China is larger than India in area; Bangladesh, Sri Lanka and Afghanistan are smaller.","e":"India is the 7th largest country.","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 18 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q4016","q":"Which canal connects the Atlantic Ocean in the east to the Pacific Ocean in the west?","o":["Suez Canal","Panama Canal","Stockholm Canal","Canals of Bruges"],"a":1,"x":"The Panama Canal links the Atlantic Ocean on the east with the Pacific Ocean on the west.","e":"Suez links the Mediterranean and the Red Sea.","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 22 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q4296","q":"The deepest river in the world is ____________.","o":["River Amazon","River Nile","River Brahmaputra","River Congo"],"a":3,"x":"The Congo is the deepest river in the world, over 220 m deep in places.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 24 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q4301","q":"Where was the conference held to resolve the dispute between Israel and Arabs, in 1978?","o":["Brooke Place Manor","Great House","Marshall Hall","Camp David"],"a":3,"x":"The 1978 Camp David Accords between Egypt and Israel were negotiated at Camp David in the USA, with President Jimmy Carter.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 24 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q4388","q":"The shallowest ocean in the world is ___________.","o":["Indian Ocean","Atlantic Ocean","Arctic Ocean","Antarctic Ocean"],"a":2,"x":"The Arctic Ocean is the smallest and shallowest ocean.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 24 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q4395","q":"Shinzo Abe is the former Prime Minister of which country?","o":["Japan","South Korea","China","Philippines"],"a":0,"x":"Shinzo Abe was Japan's longest-serving Prime Minister.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 24 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q4544","q":"India's trade with which country has the largest trade deficit?","o":["United States of America","Germany","Japan","China"],"a":3,"x":"India's largest trade deficit is with China.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 25 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q4551","q":"Which among these is not a marginal sea that shares its borders with the Indian Ocean?","o":["Red Sea","Persian Gulf","Black Sea","Timor Sea"],"a":2,"x":"The Red Sea, Persian Gulf and Timor Sea are marginal seas of the Indian Ocean; the Black Sea is linked to the Mediterranean.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 25 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q4625","q":"Which of these islands in the Bay of Bengal is not a part of India?","o":["North Sentinel Island","St. Martin's Island","Neil Island","Sagar Island"],"a":1,"x":"St. Martin's Island in the Bay of Bengal belongs to Bangladesh; North Sentinel, Neil and Sagar islands are Indian.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 25 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q4080","q":"The Palk Strait connects India with _____________.","o":["Sri Lanka","China","Bangladesh","Pakistan"],"a":0,"x":"The Palk Strait lies between Tamil Nadu (India) and Sri Lanka.","e":"","src":["Jr. Telephone Operator 23 Apr 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6358","q":"Which of the following pair of tree – forest is correct? I. Temperate evergreen forest – eucalyptus II. Temperate deciduous forest – pine","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":0,"x":"Temperate evergreen forests have oak, pine and eucalyptus; pine is not typical of temperate deciduous forests, which have oak, ash and beech (NCERT Class 7).","e":"","src":["Care Taker 8 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q6359","q":"Which of the following country is touched by Sahara desert? I. Chad II. Egypt III. Algeria","o":["I and II","II and III","I and III","I, II and III"],"a":3,"x":"The Sahara covers parts of Chad, Egypt and Algeria, among other North African countries.","e":"","src":["Care Taker 8 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q4430","q":"On which continent is the 'Alpaca' animal (Camel Family) found from following?","o":["Asia","Europe","Africa","South America"],"a":3,"x":"The alpaca, a relative of the camel, is found in South America.","e":"","src":["Hindi Translator-cum-Assisstant 24 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1706","q":"Durand Line is in between which of the following countries?","o":["Pakistan and Afghanistan","Pakistan and India","Afghanistan and Iran","Afghanistan and Uzbekistan"],"a":0,"x":"The Durand Line is the border between Pakistan and Afghanistan.","e":"","src":["TGT 10 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1707","q":"Which among the following line divide India and Pakistan?","o":["Hindenberg Line","Line of Control","Maginot Line","Mannerheim Line"],"a":1,"x":"The Line of Control (LoC) divides Indian- and Pakistani-administered Kashmir.","e":"","src":["TGT 10 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2305","q":"Which country is the highest producer of rice in the world?","o":["India","China","Brazil","United states of America"],"a":1,"x":"China is the world's largest rice producer; India is second.","e":"","src":["TGT 13 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2329","q":"Mount Kilimanjaro is located in ______.","o":["Tanzania","Kazakhstan","Kyrgyzstan","Libya"],"a":0,"x":"Mount Kilimanjaro, Africa's highest peak, is in Tanzania.","e":"","src":["TGT 14 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2351","q":"Which pair of Peak – Country is correct? I. Makalu – India II. Nanga Parbat – Nepal","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":3,"x":"Makalu is on the Nepal–China border and Nanga Parbat is in Pakistan-administered Kashmir, so neither pair is correct.","e":"","src":["TGT 14 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q3380","q":"Which of the following Border Personnel Meeting points is not shared between India and China?","o":["Spanggur Gap","Nathula","Bum La","Banbasa"],"a":3,"x":"Spanggur Gap, Nathu La and Bum La are India–China Border Personnel Meeting points; Banbasa is on the India–Nepal border.","e":"","src":["TGT 18 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q4789","q":"In _______ Nepal adopted an interim Constitution.","o":["2006","2009","2007","2005"],"a":2,"x":"Nepal adopted an interim constitution in 2007; its present constitution dates from 2015.","e":"","src":["TGT 25 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q5038","q":"In 1956, an Act was passed to recognise ____as the only official language of Sri Lanka.","o":["Tamil","Hindi","Sinhala","English"],"a":2,"x":"Sri Lanka's 1956 Act made Sinhala the only official language (NCERT Class 10).","e":"","src":["TGT 26 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q5537","q":"Which is the second-highest mountain on Earth?","o":["Mount Everest","Altai Mountains","K2","Hindu Raj"],"a":2,"x":"K2 (8,611 m) is the second-highest mountain on Earth.","e":"","src":["TGT 2 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5538","q":"The approximate height of the World's highest mountain \"Mount Everest\" is:","o":["8,600.00m","8,800.86 m","8,748.76m","8,848.86m"],"a":3,"x":"Mount Everest's height, as announced by Nepal and China in 2020, is 8,848.86 m.","e":"","src":["TGT 2 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5579","q":"Select the name of the peninsular country:","o":["India","Philippines","Austria","Brazil"],"a":0,"x":"India is a peninsular country, surrounded by sea on three sides.","e":"","src":["TGT 2 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q5692","q":"Select the right location of World's longest river.","o":["Africa","Asia","Europe","America"],"a":0,"x":"The Nile in Africa is the world's longest river.","e":"","src":["TGT 4 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q6241","q":"Which country is the largest producer of Jute in the world?","o":["India","United states of America","China","Brazil"],"a":0,"x":"India is the world's largest producer of jute.","e":"","src":["TGT 9 Oct 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q6242","q":"Which of the following countries does not share land boundary with India?","o":["China","Afghanistan","Myanmar","Tajikistan"],"a":3,"x":"Tajikistan does not share a land boundary with India; China, Afghanistan and Myanmar do.","e":"","src":["TGT 9 Oct 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q6306","q":"Mount Dhaulagiri lies in ______.","o":["Nepal","India","Bhutan","Pakistan"],"a":0,"x":"Dhaulagiri, the seventh-highest mountain, is in Nepal.","e":"","src":["TGT 9 Oct 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6452","q":"When was the Indus Water Treaty signed between India and Pakistan?","o":["1970","1983","1947","1960"],"a":3,"x":"India and Pakistan signed the Indus Waters Treaty in 1960, brokered by the World Bank.","e":"","src":["TGT 26 Jul 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q6453","q":"Which among the following is the World's largest and fastest growing Delta?","o":["Kaveri Delta","Godavari Delta","Sunderban Delta","Krishna Delta"],"a":2,"x":"The Sundarbans (Ganga–Brahmaputra) delta is the world's largest and fastest-growing delta.","e":"","src":["TGT 26 Jul 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q7512","q":"Which continent is the largest producer of diamonds in the world?","o":["Asia","North America","Europe","Africa"],"a":3,"x":"Africa is the largest diamond-producing continent.","e":"","src":["TGT 1 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q7557","q":"Which of the following pair of forest – tree is correct? I. Montane – Palm II. Mangrove – Birches","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":3,"x":"Neither pair is right: palms are not montane trees, and birches do not grow in mangroves.","e":"","src":["TGT 7 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q6661","q":"The lowest point of _____________ is Challenger Deep in the Mariana Trench.","o":["Atlantic Ocean","Indian Ocean","Arctic Ocean","Pacific Ocean"],"a":3,"x":"Challenger Deep in the Mariana Trench is the deepest point of the Pacific Ocean.","e":"","src":["PGT 10 Jul 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6662","q":"Mount Elbrus is the highest point of which of the following countries?","o":["Russia","United States of America","Vietnam","China"],"a":0,"x":"Mount Elbrus in the Caucasus is Russia's (and Europe's) highest peak.","e":"","src":["PGT 10 Jul 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q7162","q":"The Drakensberg mountain range is in which of the following continents?","o":["Europe","Africa","North America","Asia"],"a":1,"x":"The Drakensberg range is in southern Africa.","e":"","src":["PGT 31 Jul 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q7231","q":"The Duncan Passage joins the Bay of Bengal with ______________.","o":["Andaman Sea","Black Sea","Caspian Sea","Red Sea"],"a":0,"x":"The Duncan Passage, between Rutland Island and Little Andaman, joins the Bay of Bengal and the Andaman Sea.","e":"","src":["PGT 31 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q7295","q":"The \"Dead Sea\" is located at which of the following places?","o":["Asia","Africa","Europe","North America"],"a":0,"x":"The Dead Sea is in West Asia, between Israel and Jordan.","e":"","src":["PGT 4 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q7328","q":"Which of the following mountain ranges separates Europe from Asia?","o":["Andes","Himalayas","Kunlun","Urals"],"a":3,"x":"The Ural Mountains separate Europe from Asia.","e":"","src":["PGT 31 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q7655","q":"Maitri Setu connecting Tripura with Bangladesh is built on which river?","o":["Gomati","Khowai","Manu","Feni"],"a":3,"x":"The Maitri Setu between Tripura and Bangladesh is built over the Feni river.","e":"","src":["PGT 29 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q7775","q":"Where is the deepest mine in the world located?","o":["India","Brazil","South Africa","Australia"],"a":2,"x":"The world's deepest mine (Mponeng gold mine) is in South Africa.","e":"","src":["PGT 22 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q9522","q":"Which among the following is the deepest lake in the world?","o":["Baikal","Victoria","Titicaca","Caspian"],"a":0,"x":"Lake Baikal in Russia is the world's deepest lake.","e":"","src":["PGT 30 Jun 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q9564","q":"Kiel Canal lies between Baltic Sea and North Sea ports. It is in __________.","o":["Russia","France","Vietnam","Germany"],"a":3,"x":"The Kiel Canal, linking the North Sea and the Baltic Sea, is in Germany.","e":"","src":["PGT 28 Jun 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q9597","q":"Which is the second largest continent on Earth?","o":["Asia","North America","South America","Africa"],"a":3,"x":"Africa is the second-largest continent after Asia.","e":"","src":["PGT 28 Jun 2021"]},{"id":"q9628","q":"What is the name of the small strip of land that connects the North America and the South America?","o":["Isthmus of Panama","Suez Canal","Adam's Bridge","Bermuda Strip"],"a":0,"x":"The Isthmus of Panama connects North and South America.","e":"","src":["PGT 29 Jun 2021"]},{"id":"q9670","q":"Which among the following oceans includes the Baltic Sea?","o":["Atlantic Ocean","Indian Ocean","Arctic Ocean","Pacific Ocean"],"a":0,"x":"The Baltic Sea is part of the Atlantic Ocean.","e":"","src":["PGT 29 Jun 2021"]},{"id":"q2232","q":"All of the following countries lie between India and Vietnam, except ______________.","o":["Myanmar","Thailand","Cambodia","Malaysia"],"a":3,"x":"Myanmar, Thailand and Cambodia lie between India and Vietnam; Malaysia lies further south.","e":"","src":["Laboratory Assistant (Lie Detection) 13 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2233","q":"From the following, which has the lowest density of population?","o":["Oceania","Asia","Northern America","Europe"],"a":0,"x":"Oceania has the lowest population density of the continental regions listed.","e":"","src":["Laboratory Assistant (Lie Detection) 13 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2946","q":"The Mozambique Channel is an arm of the Indian Ocean located between the Southeast African countries of Mozambique and:","o":["Madagascar","Namibia","Zambia","Botswana"],"a":0,"x":"The Mozambique Channel lies between Mozambique and Madagascar.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Intrumentation & Control) 17 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q3292","q":"Which of the following pair of peak – country is NOT correct? I. Namcha Barwa – Nepal II. Nanda Devi – India","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":0,"x":"Namcha Barwa is in Tibet (China), not Nepal; Nanda Devi is in India.","e":"","src":["Senior Scientific Assistant (Document) 18 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q3741","q":"Rohingya Muslims have mainly migrated from which of the following countries?","o":["Myanmar","Sri Lanka","Pakistan","Nepal"],"a":0,"x":"The Rohingya Muslims have mainly fled Myanmar.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Modern Office Practice) English 21 Oct 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q4829","q":"The Florida Strait connects Gulf of Mexico and the _________ Ocean.","o":["Pacific","Southern","Indian","Atlantic"],"a":3,"x":"The Straits of Florida connect the Gulf of Mexico with the Atlantic Ocean.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Architecture) 26 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q5651","q":"‘Ganges Water-Sharing Treaty’ was signed between India and ______.","o":["Nepal","Bhutan","Myanmar","Bangladesh"],"a":3,"x":"India and Bangladesh signed the Ganges Water Sharing Treaty in 1996 for 30 years, covering the Farakka barrage.","e":"","src":["Driver 30 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q5659","q":"Which one of the following neighboring country of India is having constitutional monarchy form of government as on September 2021?","o":["Myanmar","Sri Lanka","Nepal","Bhutan"],"a":3,"x":"Bhutan has been a constitutional monarchy since 2008.","e":"","src":["Driver 30 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q5661","q":"In 1951 India signed a Treaty of Peace and Friendship with ______ a neighboring country.","o":["Bhutan","Nepal","Sri Lanka","Myanmar"],"a":3,"x":"India signed a Treaty of Peace and Friendship with Burma (Myanmar) in 1951.","e":"Nepal: 1950; Bhutan: 1949.","src":["Driver 30 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q5798","q":"The longest river of which of the following continents is Volga?","o":["Europe","South America","North America","Asia"],"a":0,"x":"The Volga is Europe's longest river.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Electrical) 5 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6004","q":"Mount Vinson Massif is the highest mountain of ___________.","o":["Africa","Antarctica","Europe","South America"],"a":1,"x":"Mount Vinson (Vinson Massif) is Antarctica's highest mountain.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Electronic & Communication) 8 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6057","q":"Mount Aconcagua is the highest mountain in _____________.","o":["Australia","Europe","South America","North America"],"a":2,"x":"Aconcagua is the highest mountain in South America.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Mechanical) 8 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q6409","q":"The Strait of Gibraltar joins _______________ Ocean and Mediterranean Sea.","o":["Atlantic","Pacific","Indian","Southern"],"a":0,"x":"The Strait of Gibraltar joins the Atlantic Ocean and the Mediterranean Sea.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Commercial Art) 14 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6708","q":"Which temperate grasslands are found in Australia?","o":["Down","Pampas","Veld","Steppe"],"a":0,"x":"The temperate grasslands of Australia are the Downs.","e":"","src":["Investigator 26 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q7033","q":"Which among the following pairs of desert and country is incorrect?","o":["Great Basin Desert - United States of America","Great Victoria Desert - Argentina","Mojave Desert - United States of America","Taklamakan Desert - China"],"a":1,"x":"The Great Victoria Desert is in Australia, not Argentina.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Medical Lab Technology) 1 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q7356","q":"Which of the following countries are drained by Amazon basin? I. Peru II. Bolivia III. Ecuador","o":["I and II","II and III","I and III","I, II and III"],"a":3,"x":"The Amazon basin drains Peru, Bolivia and Ecuador, as well as Brazil and others.","e":"","src":["Physical Education Teacher 2 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q7443","q":"Which among the following pairs of longest river and country is INCORRECT?","o":["Yangtze - China","Murray-Darling - Australia","Severn - United States of America","Amazon - South America"],"a":2,"x":"The Severn is the United Kingdom's longest river, not the USA's.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Library Science) 7 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q7466","q":"The Bosphorus Strait joins __________ and Sea of Marmara.","o":["Red Sea","Black Sea","Caspian Sea","Mediterranean Sea"],"a":1,"x":"The Bosphorus joins the Black Sea and the Sea of Marmara.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Pharmacy) 2 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q9709","q":"Which of these is situated to the south of the Lakshadweep Islands?","o":["Bangladesh","Maldives","Sri Lanka","Myanmar"],"a":1,"x":"The Maldives lie to the south of the Lakshadweep Islands.","e":"","src":["Junior Engineer 29 Jun 2022 S3"]}]}]});
